window.__ARXIV_DATA__ = {"papers":[{"id":"2611.23228v1","title":"Spool: Low-Latency Streaming Inference for Hybrid Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Applications such as visual accessibility agents and robotics require answering questions about recent video content promptly, over a bounded window of past frames (e.g., the last two minutes). Hybrid vision-language models (VLMs), which interleave attention with linear recurrent layers such as Gated DeltaNet (GDN), are attractive for this setting, but maintaining a sliding window is expensive. The reason is that recurrent layers combine all past frames into a shared state, so expiring old frames normally requires re-running the entire window through the model. We observe that the recurrent update is affine in the state with input-dependent coefficients, so the effect of each frame can be captured as a transformation that composes associatively. We present Spool, a training-free framework that caches per-frame state transformations and composes them efficiently as the window advances, removing expired frames from each recurrent layer's state without replaying frames through the backbone. Removal is exact within each layer; as with key-value eviction in attention, expired frames can retain residual influence through the cached inputs computed by earlier layers. We design a dedicated data structure and GPU kernels to achieve low caching and composition overhead. Across five open-source hybrid VLMs and four video reasoning benchmarks, Spool achieves the lowest p95 time-to-first-token in all 20 model-benchmark tests, reducing it by a geometric mean of $6.6\\times$ over the faster of two recent vLLM baselines, while improving accuracy by 2.3 percentage points on average.","authors":["Rui Ning","Om Chabra","Hari Balakrishnan"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:59:27Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:59:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23228v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23228v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23228v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":86149,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Applications such as visual accessibility agents and robotics require answering questions about recent video content promptly, over a bounded window of past frames (e.g., the last two minutes). Hybrid vision-language models (VLMs), which interleave attention...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23226v1","title":"FastFrames: Video Generation at Interactive Latency","abstract":"Autoregressive video diffusion models generate frames based on new observations and inputs, making them a promising medium for interactive visual experiences. However, existing models are orders of magnitude too slow for fluid interaction. This paper introduces FastFrames, a family of fast autoregressive video diffusion models that achieves interactive latency without compromising quality. We profile the inference pipeline of state-of-the-art models and find that operations often treated as fixed implementation details, such as VAE decoding and KV cache updates, in fact account for over half of end-to-end inference latency. We challenge the assumption that these components are indispensable and replace them with lightweight alternatives. Our 1.3B variant runs at 36 ms per frame, or 28 responses per second (RPS), on a single B200 GPU while matching the generation quality of a state-of-the-art model that is nearly 8x slower. Our 14B variant runs at 45 ms per frame, or 22 RPS, on an 8x B200 GPU node and yields significantly better visual quality and realism. We show that FastFrames enables real-time interaction in applications such as camera-conditioned and physics-conditioned video generation.","authors":["Gene Chou","Guandao Yang","Fujun Luan","Lars Mescheder","Wei Dong","Stephan Richter","Vladlen Koltun"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:59:13Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:59:13Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23226v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23226v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23226v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":72450,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Autoregressive video diffusion models generate frames based on new observations and inputs, making them a promising medium for interactive visual experiences. However, existing models are orders of magnitude too slow for fluid interaction. This paper introduc...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23212v1","title":"EviSlide-MIL: Multi-Query Pooling and Shared Prediction for Class-Incremental Whole-Slide Image Classification","abstract":"Histopathological analysis of whole-slide images (WSIs) plays a critical role in cancer diagnosis, and multiple instance learning (MIL) is widely adopted for WSI analysis under weak supervision. As new tumor subtypes are introduced, extending existing MIL models requires learning new classes while retaining knowledge of previously learned ones. This presents a class-incremental learning challenge, where models must acquire new classes with limited access to historical training data and recognize all learned classes without task identifiers at inference. A major obstacle is forgetting, as updates to feature aggregation and prediction modules can degrade performance on previously learned classes. To address this challenge, we propose \\textbf{EviSlide-MIL}, a class-incremental WSI framework that integrates multiple learnable queries per class with a shared prediction module. The queries aggregate frozen patch features into class-specific representations, while the shared prediction module limits parameter growth across incremental stages. By freezing queries associated with previously learned classes, EviSlide-MIL preserves their attention patterns for identical inputs, while experience replay and knowledge distillation further mitigate forgetting as shared parameters are updated. Experiments on eight tumor subtypes from The Cancer Genome Atlas demonstrate that EviSlide-MIL achieves improved performance and reduced forgetting compared with competing methods across most learning settings, demonstrating its potential for scalable and knowledge-preserving WSI classification. Code and pretrained models will be released.","authors":["Zhouhan Qian","Sicheng Chen","Chad Wong","Tianyi Zhang","Enhui Chai","Zeyu Liu","Fei Xia"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:50:42Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:50:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23212v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23212v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23212v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":70817,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Histopathological analysis of whole-slide images (WSIs) plays a critical role in cancer diagnosis, and multiple instance learning (MIL) is widely adopted for WSI analysis under weak supervision. As new tumor subtypes are introduced, extending existing MIL mod...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23211v1","title":"Multimodal Masked Diffusion Autoencoder for Modeling Impaired Driving","abstract":"Human cognitive state (e.g., distraction, stress), gaze, and behavior interact with the driving environment in subtle ways that AI could use to assist drivers, but ground-truth labels for human cognitive state levels are costly to obtain at scale. We propose a multimodal masked-diffusion autoencoder, ID-MAE, that adds a self-supervised objective to impairment prediction: alongside predicting the impairment label as a masked token, it reconstructs masked gaze and trajectory. To probe the effects of different modalities and types of impairments on model inference, we introduce DIVERT, a driving-simulator dataset containing 103 hours of driving data (n = 109) with ten impairment inductions spanning cognitive, visual, and emotional states, recorded with gaze, vehicle state, and scene video. We demonstrate how ID-MAE affords significant improvements in impairment inference results on both the DIVERT dataset and existing datasets, and enables strong results in additional tasks, such as trajectory and gaze prediction. For location-matched pairs, our model achieves a paired AUC of 0.706 for impairment detection on DIVERT, compared to 0.590 without reconstruction loss, improving over existing baselines. We probe the design space in terms of input modalities, masking scheme, positional encoding, attention mechanism, and decoding. We also investigate the interaction between various impairments in training and inference, demonstrating that visually demanding distractions are separable from cognitive distractions. Code and data will be available upon acceptance.","authors":["John Gideon","Anshul Gupta","Megan Applegate-Kenton","Kayli Battel","Todd J Rowell","Patricio Reyes Gomez","Laporsha Dees","Emily Sumner","Avinash Balachandran","Guy Rosman"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:50:22Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:50:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23211v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23211v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23211v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":69357,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Human cognitive state (e.g., distraction, stress), gaze, and behavior interact with the driving environment in subtle ways that AI could use to assist drivers, but ground-truth labels for human cognitive state levels are costly to obtain at scale. We propose...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23208v1","title":"Distortion-Free Semantic Watermarks for Black-Box Generative Models","abstract":"We present a provably distortion-free semantic watermarking algorithm that requires only black-box sampling access to a generative model and black-box access to a semantic embedding. A watermark is called distortion-free if, averaged over its secret detection key, it leaves the generator's output distribution unchanged; this provides a formal guarantee that watermarking does not reduce output quality. Conventional such watermarks are often brittle to transformations. This is especially problematic for images, where benign operations such as compression, cropping, or resizing change the entire pixel representation. Semantic watermarks therefore require detection to be robust to content-preserving transformations. Previous methods combining these properties rely heavily on specific white-box properties of the underlying model: either to autoregressive text generation or to diffusion-specific access, such as control of the initial noise or diffusion inversion. In contrast, our construction is fully generic: It only interacts with the generative model and the semantic embedding, a function that determines the notion of \"preserving content\" under which detection should be robust, via sample and query access. We then instantiate this framework for image generation. We characterize the two competing properties required of the semantic embedding: it should remain stable under transformations that preserve an image's content, but should distinguish independent generations from the same prompt. We train an image encoder directly for this tradeoff. The resulting encoder improves separation while maintaining stability over off-the-shelf alternatives and enables strong aggregate detection while remaining robust to various transformations. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our watermark scheme on both open-source and closed models, which is possible only due to restriction to black-box access.","authors":["Aviv Yossef","Or Patashnik","Or Zamir"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:49:17Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:49:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23208v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23208v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23208v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":178010,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present a provably distortion-free semantic watermarking algorithm that requires only black-box sampling access to a generative model and black-box access to a semantic embedding. A watermark is called distortion-free if, averaged over its secret detection...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23189v1","title":"Retrieval-Augmented Correction for Generalist Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Automated segmentation is routinely used as the first step in quantitative image-based clinical workflows, where predicted masks are subsequently reviewed and corrected by human operators. Automated correction of the residual structural errors is currently addressed one anatomy at a time, so the number of trained correctors scales with the number of segmentation targets. We present GRACE, a retrieval-augmented refiner that edits an input mask under context drawn from a shared knowledge base of verified prior cases. The refiner is trained once on a multi-organ corpus spanning compact organs, cavities, branching vessels, and tumour lesions. In the held-out dataset-transfer evaluation, three held-out datasets query a fixed knowledge base built from training data only. A separate leave-one-anatomy-out experiment tests parameter-free index extension: verified cases of an unseen solid organ enter the knowledge base, and the refiner stays frozen. A light shape-aware projection head on a frozen multimodal encoder maps embedding similarity to the shape agreement of the masks, so the knowledge base returns cases that the refiner can use as a shape prior. We evaluate GRACE on 18 public datasets spanning CT, MRI and ultrasound, using nine refinement configurations and seven upstream segmentors that span weak, mid, and strong operating regimes. We report each change with the DSC of its input mask. On the weak tier, the input DSC is 0.386 and GRACE adds up to +0.35 DSC. On a strong segmentor, the input DSC is 0.932 and the mean change is small. Transfer to an imaging modality that is absent from training and from the index is partial and depends on the anatomy, and remains an open research direction. Our results suggest retrieval-conditioned refinement is a practical alternative to anatomy-specific correction models. Code: https://github.com/robustml-eurecom/grace","authors":["Vincenzo Marcianò","Xiaoming Zhang","Michela Antonelli","Sebastien Ourselin","Maria A Zuluaga"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:33:42Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:33:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23189v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23189v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23189v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":96885,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Automated segmentation is routinely used as the first step in quantitative image-based clinical workflows, where predicted masks are subsequently reviewed and corrected by human operators. Automated correction of the residual structural errors is currently ad...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23187v1","title":"Any2Building: Unifying Point Clouds and Images for Building Reconstruction via Shared Spatial Queries","abstract":"Abstracting structured buildings from limited observations is essential for 3D urban modeling and digital twins. Partial point clouds and overhead images of the same building provide incomplete 3D surface measurements and visual cues to its footprint, roof geometry, and rooftop structures, respectively. This motivates a unified framework that aligns geometric and visual cues in 3D and reconstructs buildings from either or both modalities. Building on this connection, we present Any2Building, a unified model for Level of Detail 2 (LoD2) building reconstruction from partial point clouds, overhead images, or both, with optional coarse geometric priors. At its core is an occupancy-grounded shared spatial queries representation that organizes available observations within a common 3D grid. Supervised by complete building surface voxels, these queries connect observational evidence to target geometry, enabling a shared generator to learn structural priors across tasks and providing spatial guidance for recovering missing structure. A trained voxel conditional surface-generation and low-poly remeshing pipeline then converts the predicted structure into compact LoD2 building models. To support this framework, we introduce BuildingWorld-Image, comprising approximately one million paired samples of partial point clouds, overhead images, and LoD2 building models across 18 cities. Experiments on multiple benchmarks demonstrate that Any2Building outperforms existing methods in geometric reconstruction accuracy across unimodal and multimodal point-cloud completion, single-image building generation, and prior-assisted generation, while maintaining detailed and robust recovery when point cloud input modality is degraded.","authors":["Hongye Hou","Zhuoxiao Li","Liu Xiangyu","Tengxi Wang","Jing Ou","Dongli Wu","Shuai Zhang","Tongyan Hua","Wufan Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:32:04Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:32:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23187v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23187v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23187v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":92135,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, representation, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Abstracting structured buildings from limited observations is essential for 3D urban modeling and digital twins. Partial point clouds and overhead images of the same building provide incomplete 3D surface measurements and visual cues to its footprint, roof ge...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23161v1","title":"Attribution Through the Decoder: Prediction-Space Explanations for Structured Outputs","abstract":"Networks that predict angles, gaze directions, and rotations do not output these quantities directly: they emit raw coordinates that a fixed decoder, such as normalization, maps to the final prediction. Attribution methods are usually applied to the raw coordinates, so they can credit inputs for changes the decoder discards, such as rescaling a vector when only its direction matters. The resulting explanation can highlight inputs that have no effect on the prediction. We instead attribute through the decoder differential, which for normalization decoders reduces to a scaled tangent projection. We characterize invariance to output rescaling, show that every fixed smooth scalar target on a sphere has points of zero tangential sensitivity, and show that an endpoint-projected variant of Integrated Gradients can still attribute to scale-only inputs, whereas direct angular Integrated Gradients cannot. On synthetic tasks with analytically known relevance, ambient attribution assigns up to 72% of its mass to an irrelevant region. On MPIIGaze validation data, tangent Grad-CAM produces 44% greater prediction displacement under 10% pixel deletion. On PASCAL3D+ with a 6D rotation decoder, the full differential improves mean deletion displacement by 14.1 degrees over the ambient coordinate sum and by 13.8 degrees over a decoder-invariant scalar. Results for a two-polarity tangent LayerCAM variant are mixed: it exceeds ambient-sum LayerCAM on circular regression but no difference is detected on quaternion pose. These results separate two failure sources, radial contamination and scalarization loss. The approach uses existing attribution methods with decoder-aware targets, although some variants also require changes to attribution aggregation. An invariant attribution can still miss inputs that drive the prediction, so invariance and deletion performance should each be checked.","authors":["Zaer Bin Zaqy","Brinnae Bent"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:13:50Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:13:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23161v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23161v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23161v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":157027,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Networks that predict angles, gaze directions, and rotations do not output these quantities directly: they emit raw coordinates that a fixed decoder, such as normalization, maps to the final prediction. Attribution methods are usually applied to the raw coord...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23155v1","title":"TextSphere: Efficient Text-to-Image Generation via Shared Hyperspherical Alignment and Uniformity","abstract":"Recent advances in text-to-image generation have substantially improved image quality, particularly through diffusion-based methods that require multiple inference steps. In contrast, Sphere Encoder offers an efficient generative framework capable of producing images with fewer forward steps. However, extending it to free-form text is nontrivial, because its class-conditioned design lacks cross-modal alignment. To address this challenge, we propose TextSphere, an efficient text-to-image generative framework that extends Sphere Encoder with language conditioning and cross-modal alignment. We incorporate pretrained CLIP text embeddings through adaptive layer normalization (AdaLN) to guide image generation while retaining the original spherical sampling mechanism. To bridge the cross-modal representation gap, we introduce a Spherical Semantic Alignment and Uniformity (SSAU) objective that jointly promotes semantic alignment and representation uniformity on the hypersphere. Experiments on CUB-200-2011, MS-COCO-2014, and Flickr30k demonstrate that TextSphere consistently reduces generation FID (gFID) over the text-conditioned baseline while achieving reconstruction FID (rFID) below 1.0. With only 4 and 6 sampling steps, TextSphere achieves lower gFID than the compared diffusion- and GAN-based models trained from scratch on CUB-200-2011 and MS-COCO-2014, respectively. We further observe that improved reconstruction fidelity does not necessarily yield better generation quality. In addition, we use Partial Least Squares Singular Value Decomposition (PLS-SVD) to analyze cross-modal alignment and modality gap, offering insight into the effectiveness of our approach.","authors":["Yixia Zhang","Girish A. Koushik","Xilu Wang","Wenwu Wang"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:08:53Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:08:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23155v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23155v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23155v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":81782,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Recent advances in text-to-image generation have substantially improved image quality, particularly through diffusion-based methods that require multiple inference steps. In contrast, Sphere Encoder offers an efficient generative framework capable of producin...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23153v1","title":"VIVID: Beyond Static Multi-Image Reasoning with Localized Visual Differences and Textual Entity Dynamics in Video MLLMs","abstract":"Video question answering under tight frame budgets requires preserving localized motion and event order, yet current video multimodal large language models (MLLMs) reduce comprehension to static multi-image reasoning by discarding unselected frames and encoding keyframes independently: (1) Spatially, whole-frame pooling dilutes small moving targets into static backgrounds; (2) Temporally, dropping intermediate frames or averaging tokens erases inter-frame motion, confusing direction, speed, and order. We propose VIVID (Visual Inter-frame Variations and Individual Dynamics), which moves beyond static snapshots like human vision: keyframes capture core states while inter-frame Patch Diffs preserve neighboring changes within $K_f$ keyframes. Autonomous Delta-Driven Keyframe Planning (ADKP) selects anchors from a $3\\text{ fps}$ pool and feeds surrounding patch differences into Dual-Road Change Memory (DRCM), forming Dynamic Tokens at zero extra anchor tokens alongside a camera-stabilized Active Motion Region of Interest (ROI) and chronological Entity State Chains. Adding $0.22\\%$ trainable parameters to a frozen $27\\text{B}$ backbone, VIVID-Small ($50$ examples, $\\sim 1.5$ GPU hours) beats all eleven baselines in overall accuracy across $8,083$ questions and trails only VIVID-Large ($3,600$ examples), which leads on MotionBench, Video-MME, and TempCompass.","authors":["Chengqian Ma","Yuanze Zeng","Hongyu Wang","Mingfeng Lin","YiCheng Xiao","Yu Chen","Pengyu Li","WenHao Wang","Yuexiao Ma","Xiawu Zheng","Fei Chao"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:05:51Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:05:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23153v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23153v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23153v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":74774,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video question answering under tight frame budgets requires preserving localized motion and event order, yet current video multimodal large language models (MLLMs) reduce comprehension to static multi-image reasoning by discarding unselected frames and encodi...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23144v1","title":"$π^4$: A Foundation Model for 4D Scene Geometry and Motion","abstract":"$π^4$ is a unified foundation model for 4D scene perception. From diverse, uncalibrated RGB observations in the wild---a single image, an unordered collection, or a video sequence---it recovers dense geometry, camera poses, and metric scale, together with 2D/3D point tracks and moving-object masks for multi-image inputs. It couples a geometry branch with a geometry-conditioned motion branch, which predicts image-space correspondences that are lifted into 3D using reconstructed target-view geometry. Across diverse benchmarks, $π^4$ achieves the best overall average rank in geometry evaluation, alongside competitive performance in single- and multiview 2D/3D point tracking and moving-object segmentation. With a shared backbone, the full geometry-and-motion system contains approximately 0.5B parameters---less than half the size of most baselines. It produces joint reconstructions and tracks in one forward pass, including large-scale scenes with thousands of input images despite training with contexts of at most 64 views. We further present the data and training recipe underlying these capabilities, together with empirical studies of key design choices. Code and models are available at https://github.com/yyfz/Pi4.","authors":["Yifan Wang","Weiguang Zhao","Ni Yang","Haoyu Guo","Junting Dong","Weinan Zhang","Chunhua Shen"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:59:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:59:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23144v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23144v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23144v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":101684,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：$π^4$ is a unified foundation model for 4D scene perception. From diverse, uncalibrated RGB observations in the wild---a single image, an unordered collection, or a video sequence---it recovers dense geometry, camera poses, and metric scale, together with 2D/...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23143v1","title":"TCP4D: A Unified Tool-Center-Point Tracking and Generation Model for Robot Manipulation","abstract":"Robot manipulation videos provide a rich visual experience, yet recovering robot action and gripper-state annotations from RGB observations still remains challenging. Using the embodiment-agnostic tool-center-point (TCP) pose as the action space, we present TCP4D, a unified model in which a shared 4D scene representation both tracks observed TCP motion and generates future TCP trajectories. Given a monocular video and initial 2D TCP queries, the tracker directly recovers metric TCP poses in SE(3) and gripper states alongside scene geometry and camera parameters. The generator samples local features from the same 4D representation along the recovered TCP tracks, then combines them with robot observations and language instructions to predict future TCP states. Our new cross-embodiment augmentation pipeline converts existing RGB videos into demonstrations for alternative embodiments by retargeting the recovered motion and synthesizing the corresponding observations, without recorded source-robot states. Experiments show that the tracker achieves absolute trajectory errors of 7.60 and 16.07 mm on DROID and RoboLab and adapts to a new embodiment with only ten demonstrations. The generator achieves 69.19% mean success on RoboTwin and outperforms well-established policy models on two real-world tasks, while joint training on source and augmented demonstrations improves target-robot success by 41.5% over augmented-only training.","authors":["Haosong Peng","Xiangyang Liu","Zhengshen Zhang","Yunfeng Fan","Tianyu Qi","Xin Xia","Yufeng Zhan","Wenchao Xu"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:59:42Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:59:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23143v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23143v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23143v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":115622,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Robot manipulation videos provide a rich visual experience, yet recovering robot action and gripper-state annotations from RGB observations still remains challenging. Using the embodiment-agnostic tool-center-point (TCP) pose as the action space, we present T...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23140v1","title":"GameRealm: Grounding Visual Learning in Interactive Games","abstract":"Visual foundation models are central to physical intelligence, yet their development remains constrained by fragmented training datasets, inconsistent annotation quality, and limited multimodal supervision. To address these challenges, we introduce GameRealm, a large-scale, richly annotated dataset that grounds visual observations in synchronized, game-native supervision. GameRealm encompasses over 120 million RGB frames and 1,000 hours of gameplay footage across all views. Beyond its unprecedented scale, GameRealm provides rich multimodal supervision, including metric depth, camera parameters, synchronized audio, control inputs, and 3D point trajectories. Its diversity further extends to flexible view configurations, spanning single-view, stereo, multi-view, and panoramic recordings. To enable scalable acquisition across diverse game environments, we develop a unified data collection pipeline that combines human-operated interaction with agentic exploration. Extensive experiments across diverse visual learning tasks demonstrate that GameRealm consistently strengthens visual foundation models in both visual generation and geometric learning. These results establish GameRealm as a versatile and valuable data resource, unlocking new opportunities for advancing visual intelligence.","authors":["Weiguang Zhao","Yifan Wang","Ni Yang","Yating Wang","Xingyu Miao","Yankai Jiang","Mulin Yu","Tao Lu","Haoyu Guo","Junting Dong","Weinan Zhang","Chunhua Shen"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:57:22Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:57:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23140v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23140v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23140v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":99167,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, agent, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Visual foundation models are central to physical intelligence, yet their development remains constrained by fragmented training datasets, inconsistent annotation quality, and limited multimodal supervision. To address these challenges, we introduce GameRealm,...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23127v1","title":"The Illusion of Grounding: Do Vision-Language Models Weigh Retrieved Text and Images Like an Ideal Observer?","abstract":"Retrieval-augmented vision-language models (VLMs) answer from two sources of evidence that can each fail: the retrieved text can be wrong or noisy, and the image can be degraded or contradicted. We introduce GIB-200K, 200,000 image-question items built from COCO and Visual Genome, each expanded into controlled stress conditions: hard-negative retrieval noise (1-20 documents), visual ambiguity (Gaussian and motion blur, low-resource crops) and cross-modal conflict with counterfactual flip pairs. Instead of only measuring degradation, we ask how models combine the two modalities. From ideal Bayesian cue combination we derive a testable prediction that yields formal definitions of Knowledge Inertia (text over-weighted relative to the model's own image evidence) and Sensory Capture (the reverse), and we estimate the weights with a measurement-error-corrected regression whose recovery of known weights we verify. A counterfactual flip decomposition separates modality bias from concept priors, and a Grounding Illusion Score measures how much clean accuracy is not grounded in the image. We evaluate 4 open models on one frozen, seeded evaluation split of 2,000 GIB-200K items. All models are accurate on clean inputs (92.5%-94.2%) but adverse accuracy falls to 41.3%-59.9%. The two LLaVA models over-weight the text (Knowledge Inertia, w_T/w_I = 1.229 and 1.322), whereas InternVL3 favours the image (w_T/w_I = 0.680); between 34.8% and 71.4% of the clean answers that are correct are abandoned when the document lies. A training-free consistency check that compares the answer with and without retrieval trades coverage for accuracy under misleading documents, while falling back to the image fails when the image is heavily degraded. All per-instance outputs, code, and an audit that recomputes every reported open-model number are released.","authors":["Basavaraj R M","Harshal Chandel"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:45:28Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:45:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23127v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23127v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23127v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":44070,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Retrieval-augmented vision-language models (VLMs) answer from two sources of evidence that can each fail: the retrieved text can be wrong or noisy, and the image can be degraded or contradicted. We introduce GIB-200K, 200,000 image-question items built from C...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23113v1","title":"LUTBench: Benchmarking Text-Driven Photographic Look Transfer","abstract":"Photographic color grading shapes the tone and color of a photograph to achieve a desired look. Text-driven photographic look transfer lets users specify these adjustments in natural language, with the goal of reproducing the requested look across photographs while preserving their content. However, prior research on text-driven color grading uses different datasets, text instructions, target definitions, and metrics. These differences make it difficult to compare how faithfully the methods reproduce the same look. To compare methods under a shared protocol, we introduce LUTBench. We collect 91 three-dimensional lookup tables (3D LUTs) and define one photographic look per LUT with a specified input conversion. Applying these looks to 5,000 MIT-Adobe FiveK photographs produces 455,000 input-target image pairs with reproducible target images. For each look, we use templates to turn measured tone and color changes on fixed color samples into candidate clauses. We use training image measurements to verify applicable clauses before composing the text description. The evaluation measures target fidelity, tests sensitivity to predefined input conversions, and separates generalization to unseen looks from reproduction of seen looks on new photographs. Across 16 methods, stronger text alignment does not guarantee target fidelity. However, most supervised variants improve fidelity on both seen and unseen looks.","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Boyu Wei","Chengkun Yue","Xuanhua Yin","Yiyang Li","Chupeng Liu","Zihan Deng","Weidong Cai"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:34:32Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:34:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23113v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23113v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23113v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":110115,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Photographic color grading shapes the tone and color of a photograph to achieve a desired look. Text-driven photographic look transfer lets users specify these adjustments in natural language, with the goal of reproducing the requested look across photographs...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23088v1","title":"Event-Based Person Detection for Maritime UAV Search","abstract":"Maritime search and rescue requires locating people in the water quickly, motivating the use of UAVs to survey the search area. During flight, camera motion and the longer exposures used in low light can blur small targets and hinder person detection. Event cameras record brightness changes as they occur, reducing the motion blur associated with longer exposure times. We investigate their use for maritime person detection by generating synthetic events using the v2e event simulator from public MOBDrone rescue videos and retaining the existing annotations. We compare the recurrent vision transformer model RVT-T with YOLOv8n and RT-DETR-R18 trained on exposure-matched images. On 843 test samples from held-out recordings, RVT-T achieves 15.63% person AP50:95, compared with 6.89% for YOLOv8n and 9.44% for RT-DETR-R18 under 100 ms synthetic exposure. Increasing exposure times from 50 to 200 ms progressively reduces frame-based detection accuracy, widening the gap to the event model. To evaluate the feasibility of on-board processing, we deploy our detector on a Jetson Orin Nano platform. The RVT-T model retains its accuracy and requires approximately 136 ms and 2.02 J per sample, allowing real-time processing on the UAV.","authors":["Zijie Ning","Patrick Vandewalle"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:11:00Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:11:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23088v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23088v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23088v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":38417,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Maritime search and rescue requires locating people in the water quickly, motivating the use of UAVs to survey the search area. During flight, camera motion and the longer exposures used in low light can blur small targets and hinder person detection. Event c...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23085v1","title":"Transfer Once, Ask Locally: Compact Audio-Visual Memory for On-Device Question Answering","abstract":"Audio-visual question answering on lightweight user devices requires balancing speed, memory usage, privacy, and communication. One approach is to run audio-visual encoders on a server and a lightweight language decoder on the user device, keeping questions local while offloading media encoding. However, transferring and processing the full multimodal representation remains costly. We propose a training-free, query-agnostic token pruning framework that constructs a compact, reusable memory before transmission. We define video spatio-temporal blocks and aligned audio windows as players and estimate their importance through Monte Carlo Shapley valuation. We use an embedding coverage objective that combines query-independent utility with cross-modal redundancy calibration and rewards unimodal diversity through marginal coverage gains. We retain the original token embeddings of high-scoring players under a limited token budget and transfer the resulting memory once for reuse across subsequent local questions. Experiments on Video-MME, Daily-Omni, and Video-Holmes using video-SALMONN 2+ (3B) and video-SALMONN 2 Pro (4B) show that our method achieves the best or tied-best QA accuracy among the compared compression methods across all evaluated settings. On Daily-Omni with video-SALMONN 2+ (3B) at 80\\% pruning, our method improves accuracy by 12.86--14.62 percentage points over these methods while achieving the lowest device-side LLM prefill and decoding latency, with up to $1.92\\times$ decoding speedup at comparable embedding memory usage.","authors":["Qitong Wang","Jihui Jin","Divya Kothandaraman","Vishnu Raj","Christopher Rasmussen"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:09:48Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:09:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23085v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23085v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23085v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":48293,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Audio-visual question answering on lightweight user devices requires balancing speed, memory usage, privacy, and communication. One approach is to run audio-visual encoders on a server and a lightweight language decoder on the user device, keeping questions l...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23078v1","title":"UnrealEvent: Synthetic Dataset Generation and Annotation for Event-Based Vision","abstract":"Event-based cameras provide high temporal resolution, low power consumption, and a high dynamic range, but progress in event-based object detection remains constrained by the scarcity of annotated training data. To solve this issue, this paper introduces the tool UnrealEvent, a synthetic dataset-generation pipeline implemented in Unreal Engine 5.5. The pipeline combines a V2E-inspired event-frame generator with EasySynth semantic-rendering passes, allowing rendered level sequences to be converted into paired event representations and YOLO-format bounding-box annotations without manual labeling. Events are generated from per-pixel log-luminance differences between consecutive frames, with contrast thresholds sampled independently from a normal distribution to emulate the fabrication-induced threshold mismatch of real sensors. Using this pipeline, we provide a synthetic dataset and evaluate YOLOv8 detectors under synthetic, zero-shot real, and real fine-tuning training on eTraM. The results show strong in-domain synthetic performance but a substantial synthetic-to-real gap, especially for pedestrians. Fine-tuning on a small real subset improves real-domain performance, while the remaining gap highlights the importance of asset diversity and sensor calibration.","authors":["Yentel Schoovaerts","Zijie Ning","Patrick Vandewalle"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:04:41Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:04:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23078v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23078v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23078v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":39216,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Event-based cameras provide high temporal resolution, low power consumption, and a high dynamic range, but progress in event-based object detection remains constrained by the scarcity of annotated training data. To solve this issue, this paper introduces the...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:21+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23056v1","title":"RFW-S: Everyday Deformable Objects with Independent Simulation Verification","abstract":"Large-scale robotic simulation requires diverse deformable objects with executable physical properties and realistic interaction behaviors. However, existing 3D asset collections primarily emphasize visual diversity, while scalable physical construction and verification remain challenging. We introduce RFW-S, a large-scale dataset of 83,365 simulation-verified assets spanning 485 everyday categories across five structural families: soft solids, cloth sheets, shells, garments, and bags. We develop an automated pipeline integrating reference-guided 3D generation, structure-aware physical modeling, and LLM-assisted parameter estimation and calibration. To ensure physical reliability, we introduce an independent simulation verification framework that evaluates object behaviors under interactions distinct from those used for parameter calibration. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of independent verification and characterize deformable responses under different physical configurations. We further evaluate robotic grasping in terms of both task success and object integrity, and demonstrate the physical realism of our simulated objects through sim-to-real replay of fragile-object manipulation. RFW-S provides a scalable foundation for physically grounded simulation and embodied robotic learning.","authors":["Jiarui Guo","Saining Zhang","Jiaying Wan","Jiawei Weng","Junhao Chen","Rui Zhang","Yuanbo Lyu","Zhiheng Lee","Peishuo Li","Jingrun Du","Weiqing Xiao","Weiyi Lin","Zenan Meng","Hu Wei","Lin Qu","Zongzheng Zhang","Hao Zhao","Chaojian Li"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:49:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:49:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23056v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23056v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23056v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":96484,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Large-scale robotic simulation requires diverse deformable objects with executable physical properties and realistic interaction behaviors. However, existing 3D asset collections primarily emphasize visual diversity, while scalable physical construction and v...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:21+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23055v1","title":"FrameRouter: Frame Budget Routing for Long-Video Understanding on Video-MME-v2 and Beyond","abstract":"Long-video question answering benchmarks like Video-MME-v2 typically evaluate systems under a uniform frame budget for every question, despite stark variation in the evidence each query actually demands. We analyze this mismatch on Video-MME-v2 and find that different question subsets prefer different frozen frame-budget policies, while uniformly increasing the frame count does not reliably improve all metrics. These findings motivate fixed-mean frame-budget routing, a controlled inference setting in which each question may receive a different frame budget while the average budget matches a uniform reference. We introduce FrameRouter, a training-free test-time framework that combines an evidence-demand router, a budget-constrained allocation rule, and a frozen bank of frame-sampling policies. By controlling how much visual evidence each question receives while leaving the underlying samplers unchanged, FrameRouter is orthogonal to query-aware frame selection. Across Video-MME-v2 and several long-video understanding benchmarks, FrameRouter improves long-video question answering by using the same average visual budget more selectively across questions. Code is available at https://github.com/bingjunluo/FrameRouter.","authors":["Bingjun Luo","Jialin Guo","Siqi Li"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:49:21Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:49:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23055v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23055v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23055v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":58063,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Long-video question answering benchmarks like Video-MME-v2 typically evaluate systems under a uniform frame budget for every question, despite stark variation in the evidence each query actually demands. We analyze this mismatch on Video-MME-v2 and find that...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:21+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23054v1","title":"ReTouchGS: Ultralight Render-free Aesthetic Color Enhancement and Transfer for 3D Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) enables photorealistic scene reconstruction. Existing 3DGS color transfer and interactive editing methods are guided by reference images and user-specified color adjustments. However, in many applications, users simply want aesthetically guided color enhancement that automatically makes the scene more visually appealing while maintaining consistent colors and tones across viewpoints. We present ReTouchGS, to our knowledge the first framework for reference-free aesthetic color enhancement of pretrained 3DGS models. It predicts a scene-level color lookup table (LUT) from the model and its associated camera parameters. ReTouchGS also supports reference-guided color transfer by fitting a scene-level LUT using one edited view of the same scene and its corresponding original render. A shared blend-aware color refinement network with only 44.7K parameters refines an analytic color update to realize either color mapping without rendering. It preserves geometry and delivers standard 3DGS assets in seconds. We also design the ReTouch3D benchmark to support the evaluation of aesthetic color enhancement and reference-guided color transfer across viewpoints. Experiments demonstrate high color accuracy and strong generalization to unseen scenes in both aesthetic color enhancement and reference-guided color transfer.","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Boyu Wei","Chengkun Yue","Xuanhua Yin","Yiyang Li","Haodong Chen","Qiang Qu","Weidong Cai"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:47:43Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:47:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23054v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23054v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23054v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23051v1","title":"Refin3R: Geometry-Guided Refinement for Feed-Forward 3D Reconstruction","abstract":"Feed-forward 3D reconstruction models predict dense point maps from multiple images in a single pass. Without explicit geometric constraints, however, their predictions are often inconsistent across views and lack fine-grained precision. Recent methods incorporate geometric priors such as camera pose or depth, but use them merely as auxiliary inputs, process each view independently, or refine the geometry only where the priors are available. We present Refin3R, which refines feed-forward point maps with Structure-from-Motion (SfM) points, propagating SfM geometry over the image grid. In its dual-branch architecture, a 2D branch extends SfM geometry beyond its coverage using geometry-aware backbone features, while a 3D branch reasons over the SfM points of all views, capturing multi-view structure that the image grid alone cannot represent. The two branches exchange information bidirectionally to jointly refine each view, yielding dense and accurate point maps. Across ScanNet++, ETH3D, Tanks\\&Temples, and out-of-domain human and surgical scenes, Refin3R consistently outperforms feed-forward and geometry-guided methods in point map accuracy while running faster than the strongest geometry-guided baseline.","authors":["Jongmin Lee","Jaeyeong Kim","Seungryong Kim"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:46:34Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:46:34Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23051v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23051v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23051v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23042v1","title":"Uncovering the Invisible in Routine Radiology: Cross-Modal Detection of MRI-Verified Soft-Tissue Pathologies from CBCT via Physics-Guided 3D Self-Supervised Learning","abstract":"Routine X-ray modalities like cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) are optimized for high-contrast mineralized tissues, leaving soft-tissue abnormalities buried beneath photon noise and below human visual perception. Thus, detecting non-calcified pathologies requires secondary magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). We demonstrate that sub-visual radiographic signatures of soft-tissue pathology are physically preserved within routine CBCT scans and can be extracted for opportunistic screening using a targeted, physics-guided deep learning pipeline, even under extreme paired-data scarcity. Using the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) as a model system (N = 212 paired CBCT-MRI joints), we address the severe overfitting of high-capacity 3D networks through a two-stage framework. First, we employ a frozen full-grid 3D masked autoencoder (BertsWin) pre-trained on 6,550 unlabeled CBCT volumes via the GradientConductor (GCond) optimizer and an anatomy-aware objective (PhysLoss) redirecting gradient flow into the [-300, 300] HU soft-tissue window. Second, automated localization of the condylar zenith drives a deterministic 3D cropping that eliminates 81% of background osseous variance. Evaluated via a patient-isolated 3x10-fold stratified cross-validation protocol, a statistically robust sub-visual signal for MRI-verified synovial effusion (ROC AUC = 0.793) emerges exclusively from representations pre-trained with the PhysLoss and GCond regime. Crucially, only this setup captures true 3D anatomical chirality (p < 0.001) and high intra-patient structural consistency (0.791) over inter-patient noise; conversely, identical architectures pre-trained with standard L2 loss or unmasked variance decomposition (MVC) collapse to random guessing. These findings establish a generalizable paradigm for repurposing routine, low-contrast radiographic scans into opportunistic screening tools for occult soft-tissue disease.","authors":["Evgeny Alves Limarenko"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:40:33Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:40:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23042v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23042v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23042v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23038v1","title":"When Anchors Shift the Target: Advantage-Gated Adaptation for Diffusion Reinforcement Learning","abstract":"Forward-process reinforcement learning adapts diffusion and flow-matching models by re-noising rewarded generations into regression targets. Because negative advantages induce concave updates with unbounded repulsive gradients, existing methods stabilize training using cached predictions from a moving policy copy, yet these anchors also alter what positive feedback teaches the model to fit. We formalize this conflict as anchor coupling: after separating frozen-reference regularizers, affine moving-anchor rules cannot eliminate negative curvature while preserving every positive regression target. We propose Advantage-Gated Adaptation (AGA) to decouple these roles. Positive samples retain standard target fitting, whose self-limiting update vanishes at the observed target independently of the moving anchor. Negative samples instead use the first-order tangent of the concave objective at the moving-policy prediction, preserving the local repulsion direction with a prediction-independent gradient. Within the quadratic-anchor family, these requirements uniquely determine the parameter-free gate $ω(a)=\\max(-a,0)$. Because the moving copy provides local linearization rather than retention, AGA assigns long-horizon behavior preservation to a frozen pretrained reference regularized at its operating classifier-free guidance scale, interpreted through an implicit-reward lens. Across compositional generation, text rendering, and preference alignment, our experiments show that displacing positive targets degrades visual quality and heightens sensitivity to policy-copy schedules, whereas AGA consistently improves the reward--quality trade-off.","authors":["Pu Cao","Min Zhou","Ying Shu","Tiezheng Ge","Qing Song","Lu Yang"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:37:36Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:37:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23038v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23038v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23038v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23037v1","title":"Automated Annotation-Free Synthetic Data Generation for Underwater Litter Detection: A Comparison of Generative Approaches","abstract":"Underwater object detection is often limited by scarce and imbalanced training data and by strong appearance variations caused by turbidity, lighting, and differences between sensors. Existing data augmentation strategies, such as geometric or color augmentation or fully synthetic image generation with Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), either lack semantic realism, fail to respect scene physics, or require additional annotations. In low-data domains like underwater environmental monitoring, the need for additional annotations is often the core bottleneck. Collecting new footage is hard, but labeling this dataset is even more time-consuming and costly, which limits how quickly a usable dataset can be constructed. We propose a unified fully automated zero-annotation pipeline that inserts class-conditioned trash objects into real underwater frames while preserving the original background context. Starting from sampled bounding boxes, each box is converted into an insertion mask and the object is pasted or generated within it, while the same box coordinates are automatically written as the corresponding annotation. Because labels are derived directly from the sampled boxes, the pipeline generates fully labeled training data for low-data settings without any manual annotation. Within this single pipeline we compare three strategies: copy-paste, Stable Diffusion inpainting, and DALL-E image-conditioned generation. We evaluate bounding-box faithfulness, visual realism, and the impact of the generated samples on downstream detector performance on the SeaClear underwater debris dataset. Results show that DALL-E improves detection performance in four of five domains, with relative gains of up to 96 percent over the real-only baseline.","authors":["Amala Mary Vincent","Bart De Schutter"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:36:51Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:36:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23037v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23037v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23037v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23034v1","title":"M3OPD-Reasoner: An Open Recipe for Post-Training Multimodal Reasoning with Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation","abstract":"Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (MOPD) enables a single student to absorb the skills of several domain teachers by learning from its own generated responses. We present Multimodal Model Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation Reasoner (M3OPD-Reasoner), a fully open recipe for post-training vision-language models (VLMs) over three reasoning domains: image STEM, image perception, and video. We curate two datasets, M3OPD-SFT-1.35M for supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and M3OPD-RL-193K for reinforcement learning (RL) with verifiable rewards. On these data, we compare post-training recipes that start from Qwen3.5-9B: training one model on the mixed data of all domains (MixSFT and MixRL), and training one teacher per domain with Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO), directly or after an SFT warm-up, and distilling the teachers into one student with MOPD. We evaluate on 21 image STEM, image perception, and video reasoning benchmarks after we decontaminate the training data against them. The comparison establishes MOPD of teachers trained with GSPO directly from Qwen3.5-9B as the best recipe: the student closely matches each teacher on its own domain and is on average above the mixed-training baselines on the same prompts. Since MOPD already merges the domain capabilities that MixRL would otherwise have to learn, its student is a stronger starting point for RL, and we ask whether the same data can carry the model further from there: we add MixRL on top of the MOPD student, on the same prompts and with the same settings, which, to our knowledge, no prior VLM recipe has studied. The same prompts still improve the MOPD student, with no collapse, and give our main model, M3OPD-Reasoner-9B. MOPD and MixRL can therefore work as consecutive stages that get more out of the same data. We release all data, the training code, and the checkpoints of every stage.","authors":["Nguyen Quang Trung","Hoang Phan","Shuo Sun","Longyin Zhang","AiTi Aw"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:36:16Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:36:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23034v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23034v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23034v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23025v1","title":"When a Larger Guidance Scale Repairs Text-to-Video Failures and When It Does Not","abstract":"When a text-to-video diffusion model ignores part of a prompt, the usual first fix is to raise the classifier-free guidance scale. This paper is a measurement study of when that fix works. On Mochi, the same increase repairs failures on rare compositions (\"a snowy beach\") beyond what generating again does, does not repair negations (\"a highway with no vehicles\"), where it fixes fewer failures than generating again does, and changes nothing for events that must happen late in a clip (\"a sandcastle that a wave washes flat\"). Which of these happens can be known in advance, from the prompt and one inexpensive score. The score, which we call demand, measures how strongly the denoiser reacts to the part of the prompt that the failed video leaves out, and it is read from the first quarter of denoising, before any video exists. Our main finding is a condition: raising the scale is effective only when the prompt contains no negation and no event tied to a specific time, and when demand is sufficiently high, with recovery clearly higher above a fitted threshold than below it. Published corrections for the three failure types, used as fixed probes, behave as this account predicts. A short decision rule follows: before generating again, a user can tell whether the most common fix is worth trying, and what to use when it is not.","authors":["Taewon Kang","Matthias Zwicker"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:30:37Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:30:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23025v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23025v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23025v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23007v1","title":"Tracking Ethological Behaviors of Broiler Chickens Through Computer Vision (YoloV11) Using an Android Mobile Device","abstract":"This study developed and evaluated a computer vision model for automated ethological behavior classification of broiler chickens in climate-controlled poultry houses. Using publicly available videos preprocessed and manually annotated into three classes (sitting, standing, and feeding), the YOLOv11 model was trained and deployed on an Android application via TFLite conversion. Exploratory analysis ensured dataset consistency and visual quality, while evaluation showed class-dependent performance: higher robustness for static postures (sitting, AP=0.531; precision=100%) and lower accuracy for dynamic behaviors (feeding, AP=0.292; recall=0.20). Tests with unseen images demonstrated significant improvements, achieving accuracies of 98.04% for sitting, 83.33% for standing, and 67.65% for feeding. The model showed effective generalization in real farm environments, supporting its potential for continuous behavioral monitoring, early welfare assessments, and decision-making in precision poultry farming. These findings establish a technical foundation for future mobile-based applications at commercial scale, reinforcing the role of computer vision in automated animal welfare evaluation.","authors":["Ryan Davi Oliveira de Meneses","Glauber da Rocha Balthazar"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:19:59Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:19:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23007v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23007v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23007v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22996v1","title":"The Rhythm of Generated Video: Latent Chunking Leaves Periodic Traces in Compressed Bitstreams","abstract":"Most AI-generated video detectors analyze decoded pixels and overlook how videos are produced. We show that causal video autoencoders leave an architecture-specific rhythm in compressed bitstreams: their temporal stride concentrates unpredictable content at latent-chunk boundaries, so rate-distortion optimized encoders allocate bits periodically. Without decoding pixels, we observe four-frame pulses in CogVideoX and Wan2.2 and eight-frame pulses in LTX-Video, which are absent from content-matched camera footage. Passing camera footage through a generator's autoencoder alone induces the pulse, supporting its proposed origin. A training-free peak-ratio statistic separates generated from camera video (AUC 0.84-0.94) and persists across tested quantization settings and H.264/H.265 encoding. Frame-size traces also identify four commercial generators, with 89.2%, 84.1%, and 83.0% accuracy under H.264, H.265, and AV1, beating the pixel-based UnivFD baseline (72.7-73.6%). Our results directly link generative architecture to measurable coding demand.","authors":["Chen Ye Gan","Bryan Zhen Zhang","Gene Wen","Yuxing han"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:11:46Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:11:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22996v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22996v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22996v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22995v1","title":"CAST: Calibrating Correspondences for Training-Free Biomedical In-Context Segmentation","abstract":"Biomedical image segmentation must keep up with new anatomical structures, subjects, imaging modalities, and acquisition protocols, yet dense expert annotations are scarce and models trained on one cohort often struggle to transfer. In-context segmentation (ICS) offers a way out: a new structure is specified by a few annotated exemplars at inference time. However, reliable correspondence remains challenging in biomedical images, where adjacent structures often share similar appearances, and variations across subjects and imaging conditions further obscure their distinctions. We introduce CAST, a training-free framework built on a frozen DINOv3 backbone to calibrate fine-grained correspondences. Its stability test improves feature reliability at both global and patch-specific levels, while its coherence test assesses whether candidate correspondences are supported by consistent local structural context. Together, these tests enable more reliable correspondence across diverse anatomical structures and imaging conditions. Across eight biomedical benchmarks spanning 2D and 3D segmentation, cross-subject, and cross-modality transfer, CAST outperforms the strongest overall baseline by 12.34 percentage points in Dice and ranks first across all six volumetric settings. The versatility of CAST further opens up opportunities for challenging biomedical applications, including 2D-to-3D segmentation and atlas label transfer.","authors":["Mingze Jiang","Jiashu Han","Haonan Chen","Zhiyi Li","Yilun Du","Kunzan Liu"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:10:40Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:10:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22995v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22995v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22995v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22968v1","title":"MI-Hyper: Generating Multiple Instance Models for Weight Space Transfer Learning in Histopathology via Mixed Tasks Pretrained HyperNetwork","abstract":"Zero-shot whole-slide image (WSI) prediction aims to generalize to new pathology tasks without parameter updates at deployment. MI-Zero makes these predictions by pooling patch-text similarities. Conditional weight generation, combined with supervision from diverse pathology tasks, offers an alternative: learning to construct predictive rules from task descriptions. We introduce MI-Hyper, a hypernetwork pretrained across mixed tasks to generate the aggregation and classification weights of multiple instance learning (MIL) models. We first train the generator and a shared MIL basis jointly using source labels. We then freeze the basis and refine the generator. The generated task weights are reused across cases. Across 308 tasks from 52 pathology datasets, MI-Hyper improves mean AUC, balanced accuracy and macro-F1 over MI-Zero by 1.85, 1.17 and 0.57 percentage points, respectively. On some molecular prediction tasks, both AUC and balanced accuracy improve by more than 20 points. These results support task-conditioned weight generation as a promising approach to weight-space transfer for long-sequence modeling.","authors":["Xitong Ling","Yousef Kotp","Weiming Chen","Yining Li","Jiawen Li","Minxi Ouyang","Xinyi Guo","Mingxi Fu","Yizhi Wang","Tian Guan","Lianghui Zhu","Yonghong He"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:52:19Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:52:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22968v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22968v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22968v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22967v1","title":"CodeReel: Accelerating Code-to-Video Agents with Exact Frame Reuse and Blocking-Aware Scheduling","abstract":"Coding agents make videos by writing and refining programs in a build--inspect--edit loop. A finished film costs a median of $2.85 in model calls and 11 minutes, over a third of that time in builds that block the agent. We find that much of this time is avoidable, for two reasons. First, 37.5% of the frames of a session's later films were drawn before, many shifted in time or inside an edited scene. Second, half of the builds stop at a check, where the agent needs only the verdict, but which builds stop is unknown until the checks finish. We present CodeReel, a training-free execution layer behind agents' existing commands. Exact frame reuse keys each frame by its drawing commands, regardless of its position in time, and reuses each frame whose key recurs. Blocking-aware scheduling runs the checks first and starts the render alongside them, at idle priority, unless the session's recent builds make a pass unlikely. Scheduling spends CPU to answer sooner and reuse returns it; exact keys let later versions use frames rendered for a failed one as the agent thinks. We introduce EditLoop, a benchmark of replayable agent sessions whose films are checked against renders from scratch. On it, CodeReel cuts the agent's wait by 36.3% against Rebuild, a warm pipeline with parallel rendering, and by 57.9% against Sequential, today's step-by-step pipeline. On time-shifting edits its reuse renders 43.9%, close to the 43.0% whose pixels are new, while Manim-style caching renders 99.8%. With real agents, the median wait per build is 0.62x Rebuild's in Claude Code, on topics never used in development, and 0.67x in Codex CLI. The gain holds for five models and two frameworks.","authors":["Junyi Shen","Yao Lu"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:52:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:52:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22967v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22967v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22967v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22962v1","title":"Hierarchical Diffusion Transformer for End-to-End Image Generation","abstract":"Pixel-space diffusion learns visual representations and image generation jointly in a single end-to-end training stage, without a separately pretrained visual encoder. However, plain pixel-space Transformers still trail leading two-stage latent diffusion models in generation quality and training efficiency. We hypothesize that part of this gap arises from retaining one token per patch throughout the backbone, which does not explicitly encourage a visual hierarchy that summarizes related content at progressively coarser levels. To address this, we introduce Hierarchical Diffusion Transformers (HiT), which progressively group related tokens and represent each group with a single token. The main Transformer bottleneck processes these compact group tokens, and ungrouping restores features to their original image positions. A compression budget regulates the average compression ratio across the batch, allowing group memberships and counts to vary across images and diffusion timesteps during both training and generation. The model exhibits emergent semantic structure, learning to group coherent objects and object parts without segmentation labels. In text-to-image generation, HiT further learns to group caption words with image patches depicting the same concepts, without region annotations. HiT achieves 1.48 FID on ImageNet $256\\times256$ and reaches comparable generation quality to JiT using approximately 40\\% of its cumulative training FLOPs. For text-to-image generation, HiT exceeds UniLiP's average score across five benchmarks with roughly $6\\times$ fewer denoiser parameters and $3\\times$ fewer forward FLOPs. These results suggest that learning a visual hierarchy within the generator breaks down the barrier to stronger image generation without external visual pretraining.","authors":["Xiaoyu Wu","Yifei Wang","Weihang Guo","Chen Wei","Lydia E Kavraki","Niloofar Mireshghallah","Steven Wu"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:47:56Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:47:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22962v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22962v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22962v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22953v1","title":"Fiberpose: state-of-the-art fiber morphometry and instance segmentation with a distance-transform neural network","abstract":"Analysis of fibrous structures in SEM images is a routine task in biomedical engineering and materials science. However, most available methods for fiber quantification still rely on classical image binarization followed by simple post-processing. These approaches often produce unreliable results in images with weak boundaries, heterogeneous contrast, and densely packed fibers. Here, we introduce Fiberpose, a framework that reformulates fiber analysis as direct regression of a smooth distance-transform (DT) field, together with a novel set of geometry-based algorithms specifically adapted to this representation. The predicted field directly encodes fiber geometry, while the accompanying algorithms enable instance reconstruction and measurement of diameter and orientation within a unified workflow. The framework is implemented as a standalone Python desktop application and a web tool https://fiberpose.org. On an independent dataset of fibrous SEM images, Fiberpose achieved state-of-the-art instance-segmentation accuracy in comparison with SAM2 and Cellpose-SAM, and more accurate diameter measurements than DiameterJ, Fibar and GIFT. The method remains effective in dense and weakly contrasted regions and shows qualitative generalization across microscopy modalities not represented in the training data. Fiberpose therefore provides an interpretable geometric representation and an automated pipeline for reproducible quantitative characterization of fibrous materials.","authors":["Viktor A. Balashov","Daniil S. Antonenko"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:41:46Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:41:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22953v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22953v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22953v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22951v1","title":"A high-dimensional spatial-physical coupling operator for generalizable super-resolution of three-dimensional turbulent flows","abstract":"Accurate representation of turbulent flows requires simultaneously capturing multi-scale spatial structures and the complex, nonlinear coupling between distinct physical quantities (\\textit{e.g.}, velocity components and density). In practice, however, turbulent data are often constrained by low spatial resolution due to hardware limitations and computational costs. While current data-driven super-resolution methods have shown promise by learning mappings via convolutional neural networks (CNNs), they typically treat physical variables as independent channels. This simplified approach neglects intrinsic cross-variable correlations, causing models to struggle with high-fidelity reconstruction, particularly under extremely sparse conditions or in out-of-distribution regimes. To bridge this gap, we propose an architecture-agnostic spatial-physical coupling operator that extends the standard convolution into a joint spatial-variable dimension. Unlike conventional linear channel mixing, our framework constructs a unified spatial-physical representation within CNNs to explicitly capture local inter-variable interactions. By lifting features into a higher-dimensional manifold, this operator significantly enhances the model's representation capacity. Experiments demonstrate that this approach enables strong reconstruction performance and zero-shot generalization, offering a physics-aware inductive bias for super-resolution of three-dimensional turbulence. More broadly, this work suggests that high-dimensional operators offer a promising avenue for modeling intricate multi-scale turbulent flows.","authors":["Zengyuan Zuo","Junjun Jiang","Fei Mu","Xubo Wang","Xianming Liu"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:41:02Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:41:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22951v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22951v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22951v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22925v1","title":"Code World Model 2.0: Towards Infinite Controllable Video Generation","abstract":"Visual proxies decouple explicit world-state simulation from photorealistic video synthesis, offering a controllable interface for both offline clip creation and real-time interactive streaming. Yet a practical renderer must address two challenges. Upstream proxies vary from coarse layouts to fine articulated geometry depending on the interaction, and streaming inference must operate in a few causal steps without accumulating drift across self-generated history. We present Invoker-Render, pairing a bidirectional creation model with a causal four-step streaming model under a shared conditioning interface over structural proxies, an appearance reference, and language. Trained on an aligned approximately 200-hour multi-granularity corpus, Invoker-Render honors fine geometric constraints when supplied while synthesizing plausible local motion under coarse proxies, enabling runtime selection of input detail. To bridge bidirectional creation and real-time streaming without compounding step compression and rollout drift, we introduce Progressive Forcing (ProForcing). ProForcing first trains a causal predictor and distills it into a four-step renderer using histories derived from training videos. Training then shifts to continuous self-generated rollouts. A pipelined serving stack supports efficient streaming inference. Experiments show versatile control across proxy families, granularities, and camera paths, consistent gains over recent streaming baselines, and stable 30-minute racing rollouts that preserve scene and subject appearance.","authors":["Ruoyu Wang","Hao Zhong","Yanming Yang","Jiahao Tian","Lin Liu","Biao Gong","Guoshan Lu","Yiding Tian","Chunhua Shen","Zhenzhong Lan","Chi Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:23:57Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:23:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22925v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22925v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22925v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22903v1","title":"BRepA: Graph-Based B-Rep Pretraining for Unified CAD Understanding and Generation","abstract":"Boundary representation (B-Rep) of CAD models encodes geometric primitives and their relations as a graph structure, enabling efficient shape modeling within CAD software. Learning on B-Reps for downstream tasks, such as segmentation and novel shape generation, requires specialized neural network designs. Moreover, performance improvements and accelerated training can be achieved with effective pretraining strategies, which is particularly important for tasks with limited labeled B-Rep data. In this paper, we introduce BRepA, a unified self-supervised pretraining framework that explicitly reconstructs both the geometry and topology of B-Rep objects via graph-based modeling. By learning from large-scale unlabeled CAD data only once, BRepA produces representations that generalize across tasks and significantly enhance both discriminative and generative learning. On segmentation tasks, BRepA demonstrates remarkable data efficiency. For instance, with only 1% labeled samples, BRepA pretraining improves mIoU by 32.52% compared to models trained from scratch. On generative tasks, aligning diffusion model representations with pretrained BRepA features substantially accelerates convergence, achieving nearly 2 times faster training while improving generation quality under the same computational budget, despite the diffusion model being roughly 20 times larger than BRepA. Overall, our results show that geometry- and topology-aware B-Rep pretraining provides a powerful foundation for both understanding and generating CAD structures, serving as a versatile representation backbone for diverse downstream applications. We will release the code to facilitate future research.","authors":["Dekai Zhu","Zhaiyu Chen","Stefan Gavranovic","Slobodan Ilic"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:08:35Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:08:35Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22903v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22903v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22903v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22896v1","title":"Impact of Pretraining Length, Model Size, and Annotation Budget on Downstream Clinical Tasks of Foundation Models in Radiotherapy","abstract":"Cone-beam CT (CBCT) scans acquired during radiotherapy capture changes such as tumor shrinkage and radiation-induced tissue changes, offering great potential for foundation models that help monitor individual treatment responses. However, to our knowledge, no large-scale pretrained models for radiotherapy CBCT exist, and evaluating modeling choices is difficult since downstream tasks are not known in advance. We examine how pretraining length, model size, and finetuning strategy relate to downstream clinical performance, and how metrics tracked during pretraining correlate with it. We pretrain vision transformer encoders with masked image modeling on about 150,000 raw 3D radiotherapy CBCT scans, tracking representation quality via validation loss, effective rank, and linear probing. Downstream tasks are classification of feeding tube presence ($n=51,135$ CBCTs, $1,562$ patients) and clinical characteristics (T-, N-stage, sex, age, tumor site; $n=1,278$ patients) in head and neck cancer. We compare low-rank adaptation (LoRA) and full finetuning against no pretraining, varying annotated dataset size. Pretext validation loss was the most consistent predictor of downstream performance; effective rank and linear probing were also associated, but not consistently across model sizes and finetuning methods. LoRA performed comparably to full finetuning while training only 0.5-2.5% of the parameters. Fully finetuned models consistently outperformed models without pretraining across all sizes, with AUROC 0.91-0.97 vs. 0.58-0.85 for feeding tube classification and 0.99-1.00 vs. 0.71-0.97 for sex classification. Model size had only a small, inconsistent effect, suggesting smaller models suffice at lower computational cost. Code and pretrained models (non-commercial license): https://github.com/alexCortinaU/RT-CBCT_SSL","authors":["Alejandro Cortina Uribe","Sidsel Højklint Poulsen","Ivan Richter Vogelius","Jens Petersen"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:05:19Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:05:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22896v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22896v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22896v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22880v1","title":"Learning Agent-Centric Vision-Language-Action World Models for Autonomous Driving","abstract":"Recent progress in integrating vision-language-action (VLA) models with world modeling has significantly advanced end-to-end autonomous driving by allowing systems to reason about imagined futures instead of merely reacting to current observations. However, most world VLA models generate future frames at the pixel level with plenty of dense visual tokens. Although such generated futures can be visually plausible, they preserve many driving-irrelevant appearance details, i.e., only a small portion of the tokens directly encode critical planning-relevant factors, such as agent geometry, motion dynamics, and interactions. This introduces a significant performance bottleneck. An important question thus arises: what world state should a world VLA model reason over to support planning beyond pixel-level prediction? We argue that planning-oriented world modeling should focus on a compact and structured agent-centric latent state that captures the interactions between objects in the driving scene over time. As such, we introduce AgentWorld, an agent-centric VLA world model that uses discrete agent tokens jointly for reasoning and trajectory planning. Each token is dynamically grounded to a surrounding agent associated with its object's state, such as semantic identity, 3D geometry, motion, and interaction context. AgentWorld learns these tokens through three key processes: explicit geometric grounding, reasoning-oriented supervised fine-tuning, and agent-oriented reinforcement learning. Extensive open-loop and closed-loop experiments under diverse driving scenarios show that AgentWorld enhances planning capabilities, fully exploits visual evidence and agent dynamics, and yields interpretable agent-centric world states.","authors":["Guoqing Wang","Pin Tang","Xiangxuan Ren","Chao Ma"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:53:49Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:53:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22880v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22880v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22880v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22874v1","title":"Harnessing Image Diffusion Prior for Photo-Realistic Video Restoration","abstract":"Recent image generation models produce photo-realistic results with rich details, but transferring their powerful priors to video restoration remains difficult due to temporal inconsistency from stochastic detail synthesis. Existing video restoration methods improve temporal coherence but often sacrifice visual fidelity, leaving a gap between image-level quality and video-level stability. We propose a generative video restoration method that bridges this gap by enabling temporally consistent detail synthesis from image generation priors. Our method combines noise energy rebalancing to suppress structure-disturbing low-frequency stochasticity, motion-aligned noise warping to propagate details along object motion, a self-supervised denoising encoder to mitigate encoder-induced temporal uncertainty, and decoupled temporal attention to model cross-frame dependencies. Together, these components preserve fidelity, enhance fine-grained realism, and maintain temporal consistency. Extensive experiments show that our method substantially outperforms existing video restoration approaches in visual quality, detail richness, and temporal stability.","authors":["Fanghua Yu","Hongyu An","Jinfan Hu","Xinqi Lin","Zhiyuan You","Jian Wang","Hongyang Li","Chao Dong","Jinjin Gu"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:49:02Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:49:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22874v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22874v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22874v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22857v1","title":"Beyond Co-Generation: Rethinking Jointness in Joint Audio-Video Generation","abstract":"Our aim is to advance the understanding of genuine audio-visual coordination in joint text-to-audio-video (T2AV) generation, with particular emphasis on semantic consistency. Evaluating audio-visual consistency in T2AV generation is nontrivial, as detailed text prompts obscure whether such consistency stems from genuine cross-modal coordination or merely from independent text following. To isolate the former, we introduce intrinsic jointness: the ability of audio and video to produce the same realization for details left underspecified by the prompt. Using semantically underspecified prompts (e.g., \"musical instrument playing\" rather than \"electric guitar playing\"), we investigate four recent models and reveal their limited intrinsic jointness. To address this limitation, we propose JointREPA, a simple representation alignment-based post-training method to facilitate cross-modal coordination. Since existing benchmarks use semantically detailed prompts, we introduce UNSAIDBench, a benchmark designed to evaluate intrinsic jointness under semantic underspecification. Experiments across two distinct models and three benchmarks, including UNSAIDBench, demonstrate that JointREPA consistently improves semantic consistency.","authors":["Jihoo Jung","Ji-Hoon Kim","Suyeon Lee","Gia Thinh Pham","Joon Son Chung"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:37:31Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:37:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22857v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22857v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22857v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22856v1","title":"Propose & Verify: Fast Reflection Symmetry Detection via Harmonic Fields","abstract":"Detecting multiple reflection symmetries in 3D shapes requires aggregating geometric evidence over a large space of candidate planes, making traditional voting approaches computationally expensive. We introduce the Fourier Accelerated Symmetry Transform (FAST), a training-free framework that combines geometric pair voting with efficient spectral computation. Exploiting the fact that global reflection planes pass through the shape centroid, we reduce the search to plane orientations, while geometric consistency filtering and independent witnesses suppress unreliable pair hypotheses. Our key insight is to represent these votes as a signal over plane orientations and efficiently compute a continuous, smoothed symmetry field in the spherical-harmonic domain. Specifically, we exploit the Funk-Hecke theorem, the spherical counterpart of the Fourier convolution theorem for rotationally symmetric kernels, to transform Gaussian smoothing into multiplication of harmonic coefficients. This enables efficient field evaluation, from which symmetry candidates are extracted, refined, and geometrically verified. Across two benchmarks, including a new benchmark of highly symmetric shapes, FAST achieves the highest clean multi-plane detection $F_1$ scores among evaluated methods (0.861 and 0.947), with GPU inference below 4 ms per shape: over $100\\times$ faster than the next-fastest evaluated multi-plane GPU baseline. FAST also demonstrates strong robustness to geometric noise and competitive single-plane detection, achieving 94.9% success on clean single-plane shapes.","authors":["Yishai Lavi","Shai Avidan"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:37:11Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:37:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22856v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22856v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22856v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22855v1","title":"CaFe: Causally Faithful Conditional Traffic Scenario Generation","abstract":"Language-conditioned traffic simulation enables scalable test scenarios steered by natural language. The goal is to control selected agents while surrounding traffic reacts to their observed behaviour. For example, a vehicle instructed to cut in late should cause the following vehicle to brake only when the manoeuvre becomes observable. Yet simulators can leak private instructions across agents, causing surrounding vehicles to react before the instructed behaviour becomes observable. Such premature reactions defuse desired events and alter the ensuing closed-loop scenario. Existing benchmarks measure what agents do, not what they could have known, leaving this failure unmeasured. We formalise it as intent leakage and address it through evaluation, data and modelling. Our paired intervention test holds an instructed agent's executed trajectory fixed while varying its private instruction, isolating leakage-induced changes in surrounding agents. We also construct WOMD-PACT, an automatically generated, verifiable dataset of 531,099 WOMD scenes with timed behaviour tags with reaction episodes identified through a counterfactual ghost test and grounded private instructions. Finally, we introduce CaFe, a dual-stream diffusion-forcing simulator fine-tuned in closed loop that restricts inter-agent interactions to public beliefs, preserving legitimate anticipation without leaking private intent. Our evaluation reveals that existing conditioning designs displace 23-83% of nearby uninstructed agents through private-intent leakage, even when the instructed agent's executed motion is held fixed. CaFe eliminates measurable leakage while maintaining comparable WOSAC realism (0.763), demonstrating that causal faithfulness can be enforced without sacrificing measured simulation realism.","authors":["Steven Yu Tung","Shubham Vaijanath Phoolari","Christoph Lauer","Abhinav Valada","Steven Peters"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:35:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:35:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22855v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22855v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22855v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22852v1","title":"Technical Report of Mobile Manipulator Robot for Industrial Environments","abstract":"This paper presents the design and implementation of Auriga Robotics' mobile manipulator robot, developed at the Robotics and Intelligent Automation Laboratory, Department of Electrical Engineering, Shahid Beheshti University. Hereafter, this platform is referred to as the @Work Robot. The robot targets industrial tasks, with an emphasis on improving efficiency in repetitive or hazardous work environments. It uses a 4-wheel Mecanum drive for omnidirectional movement and a 5-degree-of-freedom manipulator arm with a 3D-printed gripper for object handling and navigation. The electronics consist of custom PCB boards built around ESP32 microcontrollers, together with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX that handles high-level processing and real-time system control. On the software side, Hector SLAM provides mapping and localization, A* handles path planning, and YOLO performs real-time object detection. These modules draw on fused sensor data to deliver robust navigation and reliable collision avoidance. Overall, the system shows effective hardware-software integration, with consistent performance in autonomous navigation, object handling, and task execution in industrial-like settings.","authors":["Aria Dadnavi","Matin Jamshidi","Kian Khatibi","Shahriar Karimi Makhsous","Saina Najafi Shabestari","Seyed Sobhan Hosseini Hajibekandeh","Zahra Mahaam","Reyhane Mohasseli","Ghazal Ghoreishi","Amirhossein Gholizadeh Behbahani","Mohammad Hossein Moaiyeri"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:34:18Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:34:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22852v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22852v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22852v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22829v1","title":"Kolibri: Color Finds the Place, Refinement Sets the Pose for RGB-D Localization in Colored Point Clouds","abstract":"An agent or robot in a scanned building must locate its camera in rooms that no model saw during training. Point cloud registration methods can align each RGB-D frame with the building's colored point cloud, accurately but at a high cost. We observe that geometric refinement brings nearby initial poses to the same final pose, so the learned component only needs to find the place. Kolibri does this with a graph network of 55,712 parameters that describes 10 cm cubes of points, called cells, by their color, local shape and neighbors. With the same refinement, it matches GeDi on 96 unseen ScanNet rooms (81.4% against 80.3% within 5 cm and 5°) and on a held-out test of 10 further rooms, in 82 ms per frame on an RTX 2080 Ti. The saved time allows for search without a room label. In one point cloud of 100 rooms, Kolibri loses 1.3 points against knowing the room, while retrieving the room first loses 56.0. In a hospital emergency department scene from a real scan, it localizes 86.5% of the frames rendered from the scan in 384 ms each, where GeDi needs 91 minutes per frame on cubes of the scan for a similar recall. The method needs colors that agree between the frame and the point cloud. On gray frames and across cameras with different color responses, point cloud registration methods keep the lead.","authors":["Leonel Aguilar","Bartu Tuncay","Christoph Hoelscher"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:14:50Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:14:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22829v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22829v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22829v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22825v1","title":"A tiny object detection dataset for high-altitude open-water search and rescue","abstract":"Large aerial platforms often operate above 1.5 km to improve the efficiency of open water search and rescue (OW-SAR). However, long imaging distances, wide fields of view, and limited sensor resolution reduce objects to a few pixels, increasing their susceptibility to environmental interference and making detection harder than for conventional small objects. To our knowledge, existing datasets focus on low-altitude scenarios and do not support automatic detection at 1.5-2.0 km. We therefore present TinyWOD, a tiny object detection dataset for high-altitude OW-SAR using lifebuoys as detection targets. It comprises 11,359 manually annotated images and 19,505 annotated instances spanning diverse environments, lighting conditions, water colors, and surface ripples. Compared with existing datasets, TinyWOD contains much smaller objects and a substantially higher proportion of tiny instances; its average equivalent side length (ESL) is 12.7 pixels, with 76.96% of in stances having ESLs below 15 pixels, reflecting actual high-altitude water-rescue imaging conditions. We define APtiny, a tiny object detection metric, and benchmark mainstream and state-of-the-art networks using this and conventional metrics. All evaluated networks effectively detect tiny waterborne objects, demonstrating TinyWOD's reusability and utility. We expect this dataset to support research on tiny object detection in high-altitude OW-SAR.","authors":["Hui Xu","Chenfei Hou","Henglai Wei","Hao Wang","Yidong Jin","Xiulin Niu","Haoyang Zhang","Hui Zhang","Tianshi Xu"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:08:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:08:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22825v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22825v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22825v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22820v1","title":"Pixel is All You Need","abstract":"Text, images, audio, touch, and even robot actions can, in principle, all be rendered onto a single canvas, suggesting an ambitious goal of unifying all modalities within vision. Language provides the natural first test. To subsume it, a visual model must reason directly over text in pixels. Across a wide range of image, video, and unified multimodal generators, however, we find that their visual generation modules cannot perform symbolic reasoning over text on the canvas. They succeed only when an underlying text module or prompt rewriter already supplies the answer, effectively reducing the generator to a mere renderer, a limitation that post-training does little to resolve. To test whether this failure stems from pixels themselves or from current training paradigms, we train a family of purely pixel-space generative models from scratch without any text pathway. With images as their sole input and output, these models read questions, write intermediate steps, and produce answers directly on the canvas. Remarkably, they exhibit hallmark behaviors of large language models. Performance improves predictably with scale following a power law. Writing intermediate steps frame by frame substantially outperforms single-frame answering, echoing chain-of-thought, while reinforcement-learning post-training further strengthens reasoning. Moreover, sharing one canvas allows the same model to reason across modalities, a capability that text-only language models lack. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first systematic study of these phenomena in purely pixel-space generative models free of text tokens.","authors":["Jie Chu","Haoting Shi","Fucheng Zhang","Jiawei Liu","Junwei Liao","Ying Wen","Muning Wen"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:06:40Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:06:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22820v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22820v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22820v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22815v1","title":"Visual Harness Scales General VLMs to Medical Images","abstract":"We show that an appropriate visual harness can substantially improve the performance of general vision-language models (VLMs) across diverse medical imaging tasks. We introduce \\ours, a medical visual harness that equips general VLMs with interactive visual tools and systematic diagnostic evidence management. With \\ours, general VLMs interactively explore medical images and progressively acquire diagnostic evidence, which is preserved in a lossless memory for subsequent retrieval and use. After submitting an initial answer, the VLM receives an assessment of potential omissions and the reliability of identified findings. This assessment guides further image inspection and measurement, followed by answer revision in an iterative agentic loop until sufficient evidence is obtained. Across 11 tasks and four imaging modalities, \\ours improves performance on all tasks with Qwen3.8-27B, with notable gains in spleen measurement (63.3\\% to 98.3\\%), renal search (57.5\\% to 71.2\\%), and pulmonary nodule detection (0.0\\% to 28.2\\%). High-capability API-based VLMs achieve even higher performance with \\ours. For example, GPT-6.1 Sol improves pulmonary nodule detection from 10.8\\% to 69.2\\% with \\ours, demonstrating the complementary benefits of stronger model capabilities and an effective medical visual harness. Our results highlight the potential of visual harnesses as a new paradigm for scaling general VLMs to medical imaging tasks.","authors":["Shaowen Wan","Hanqi Jiang","Zihao Wu","Junhao Chen","Weihang You","Ruiyu Yan","Bowen Chen","Tianming Liu","Lin Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:04:55Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:04:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22815v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22815v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22815v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22790v1","title":"NexSight: Verifiable Tasks for Fine-Grained Agentic Long-Video Understanding","abstract":"Fine-grained questions about long videos ask how many times an action occurs, in what order events unfold, or exactly when a state changes. Answering them can require finding brief moments scattered across hours of footage. Agentic models that decide when and where to look promise to find these moments, yet this ability is hard both to measure and to learn. Sparse supporting evidence may establish that an action occurred without establishing its total count; verifying timeline-wide claims requires checking for missed occurrences and double counting. Answer-only supervision, in turn, does not explicitly supervise the search for evidence. We present NexSight, which builds verifiable tasks to both evaluate and train agentic long-video understanding. Its agentic pipeline, SightForge, accepts a task only if a verifier blind to the draft answer can reconstruct it from evidence densely reacquired from the raw video and counterexample checks fail to refute it. This yields SightLine, 310 evidence-traceable tasks from 108 long videos, evaluated through SightLoop, a shared multiscale harness supporting temporal exploration, local inspection, evidence integration, and revisiting while recording returned observations. On disjoint videos, solver trajectories on verified tasks become step-level supervision for observation decisions. The strongest model reaches only 54.52% on SightLine, yet 79.35% when given the annotated evidence. With under 6K examples, Sight-35B nearly doubles its base model's interactive accuracy (21.29% to 42.26%), outperforms the 397B model of its family, begins using explicit spatial inspection after post-training, which its base model never uses, and gains over 8 points on two external long-video benchmarks.","authors":["Tianci Xie","Li Chen","Pinxue Guo","Rui Zheng"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:52:28Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:52:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22790v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22790v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22790v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22769v1","title":"ForceBody: Force-Paired Parametric Body Motion with Torque Uncertainty","abstract":"Forces and torques drive human motion, central to biomechanics, robotics, and physics-aware animation, yet they are often absent from learning-based motion pipelines for body reconstruction and generation. Some motion datasets provide force annotations, but the paired motion sequences are incompatible with the parametric body models used in modern learning pipelines. In addition, existing datasets typically obtain joint torques through inverse dynamics, which is inherently sensitive to the quality of the input kinematics; however, they do not quantify the reliability of these torque estimates. We close this gap with ForceBody, the first dataset to bring ground truth force supervision into a parametric body representation, shipped with per-sample torque uncertainty. ForceBody pairs the SKEL body model with ground reaction forces and inverse-dynamics joint torques across 10,386 motion trials (9.7M frames, 26.9 hours) from 140 subjects. Each torque label is paired with a per-frame, per-joint uncertainty annotation obtained by Monte Carlo sampling through the inverse dynamics pipeline. We benchmark six architectures (MLP, Conv1D, LSTM, GRU, Mamba2, Transformer) on ground reaction force and joint torque prediction from SKEL motion. As one example use of the released uncertainty, finetuning a Transformer with per-sample uncertainty weighting reduces the MAE of joint torque estimation by 5%. Code and data are at https://github.com/DominoAI-Lab/ForceBody-NeurIPS-ED-2026.","authors":["Joonwoo Kwon","Yufei Zhang","Xiaoming Liu","Zijun Cui"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:35:10Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:35:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22769v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22769v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22769v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22755v1","title":"Teaching What to Look For: Privileged Perception Questions for Agentic On-Policy Distillation in Video Captioning","abstract":"Detailed video captioning requires comprehensive and faithful descriptions of objects, actions, and temporal events. Fixed-frame sampling, however, can miss brief motions, small objects, and other fine-grained evidence, motivating captioning agents that actively seek relevant moments and inspect local regions. Training such behavior is challenging because, unlike video question answering, generic captioning instructions do not specify what visual evidence should be acquired. We propose Perception-Question On-Policy Distillation (PQ-OPD), which uses training-only Perception Questions (PQs) to guide active video perception. PQs specify what should be inspected without revealing the corresponding answers, encouraging the teacher to explore the video more comprehensively and focus on relevant visual evidence. Along trajectories generated by the student, on-policy distillation transfers this guidance to the student's reasoning, tool calls, and captions; no PQs are required at inference. Across three detailed-captioning and two caption-to-QA benchmarks, PQ-OPD consistently improves the corresponding untrained agents across teacher-student scales and input-frame budgets. Under our evaluation protocol, PQ-OPD achieves the strongest primary-metric results among the compared open-source models on DREAM-1K, CaReBench, ShortVidBench, MotionBench, and VidCapBench-AE. Our ablations, together with analysis of temporal coverage and answer leakage, further indicates that the student internalizes the training-time guidance as a more effective video exploration policy.","authors":["Yifan Xu","Ruopeng Gao","Zhixiao Wang","Jiafu Tang","Yichun Yang","Zihao Wang","Yuanxing Zhang","Limin Wang"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:23:54Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:23:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22755v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22755v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22755v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22742v1","title":"GaussianMamba: Geometric and Relational State Space Scanning of 3D Gaussian Primitives","abstract":"3D semantic occupancy prediction requires representing complex spatial structures and modeling contextual relationships across a scene. 3D Gaussian primitives provide a sparse and adaptive representation of scene geometry and semantics, while Mamba offers efficient sequence modeling for capturing dependencies among primitives. However, Gaussian primitives are irregular and unordered, making sequence construction challenging, as conventional spatial ordering may overlook their anisotropic geometry and feature-semantic relationships. In this paper, we present GaussianMamba, a dual-branch Mamba framework that explicitly incorporates Gaussian-specific geometric and feature-semantic relationships into complementary scanning strategies. The first branch refines a locality-preserving traversal using anisotropic Gaussian support derived from primitive scale and orientation. The second branch reorders spatially organized groups according to feature-semantic affinity and adjusts local features using geometry-weighted semantic differences. The resulting sequences are processed by separate Mamba modules, and their features are fused through a support-aware mechanism that incorporates local feature discrepancies arising from the distinct scanning paths. Experiments on nuScenes-SurroundOcc and SSCBench-KITTI-360 demonstrate consistent improvements over GaussianFormer and GaussianFormer-2 in both IoU and mIoU.","authors":["A. Enes Doruk","Ozgur Erkent"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:11:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:11:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22742v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22742v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22742v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22733v1","title":"Evolve in Panorama, Render in View: Panoramic World State Evolution for Video World Models","abstract":"Video world models should maintain a world that keeps evolving as the viewpoint changes. Yet when the camera turns away and returns, current models often freeze the unobserved scene, lose objects, or change their appearance, because content outside the view has no representation that keeps changing. We present PanoEvo, which uses a Panoramic Evolver to evolve the world state in the latent space of a panoramic video diffusion model, where every viewing direction is present at every time step, so the scene evolves whether or not it is observed. Existing panoramic video models are not trained to evolve a scene from a single image or the objects within it, so we build PanoEvo-Data, an event-centric dataset from real 360° videos, and train the Panoramic Evolver coarse-to-fine as an image-to-panoramic-video model. Since each view covers only a small part of the low-resolution panorama, directly projected views are blurry and often show low-quality details or structural errors. A Perspective Renderer, built on a perspective video diffusion model, reads this state via latent-space projection and direction-restricted cross-attention to render sharp, high-quality views. PanoEvo achieves the best overall score among 23 publicly evaluated models on WRBench and the highest total score on STEVO-Bench. Code, models, and data will be released.","authors":["Longtao Jiang","Mingfei Han","Jianmin Bao","Tingjun Dai","Jinjie Wei","Zhiran Zhang","Xin Tao","Zhihui Li","Salman Khan","Xiaojun Chang"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:03:41Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:03:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22733v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22733v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22733v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22727v1","title":"SlopeGuard: Towards Generalizable AI-Generated Image Detection via Radial Spectral Slope Guidance","abstract":"AI-generated image detectors often overfit to generator-specific artifacts, hindering generalization to unseen synthesis pipelines. Existing frequency-based methods usually learn high-dimensional spectral patterns that may preserve generator-dependent variations. We explore Radial Spectral Slope, a compact descriptor of global spectral decay that captures transferable spectral trends while suppressing fine-grained variations. Our analysis shows that real and generated images exhibit systematic but generator-dependent slope differences, indicating that slope provides useful forensic evidence but is insufficient as a standalone decision criterion. This motivates combining spectral slope with semantic representations for more robust detection. Based on this insight, we propose SlopeGuard, which couples slope-aware data processing with slope guided feature learning to construct informative patch-based views, integrate Radial Spectral Slope with semantic features, and adapt prior preservation according to spectral atypicality. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SlopeGuard achieves state-of-the-art mean accuracies of 97.5% on AIGCDetectBench, 97.8% on GenImage, and 85.1% on AIGIBench, while maintaining competitive performance under blur, cropping, JPEG compression, and additive noise.","authors":["Cheng Fu","Yiheng Li","Yang Yang","Wenzheng Liu"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:00:56Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:00:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22727v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22727v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22727v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22722v1","title":"Practice in Text, Act in Pixels: Correcting Where Perception Fails","abstract":"GUI agents can learn cheaply from a text view of an interface and act on screenshots through a vision-language screen reader. Yet on a simulated mobile platform with a low-cost reader, policies that complete most tasks in text fail on many of the same tasks on screenshots. We ask where a small budget of real screenshots is best spent to close this gap. We find that the reader's misreads are systematic and recur on the same interface elements. Noise matched to the reader's error rates, a natural sim-to-real remedy, misses this structure, and in a pre-specified comparison it did not beat training the policy directly on screenshots. We therefore correct the misreads at their source with reader alignment, which fine-tunes the reader on screenshots paired with their text views. The reader learns to output each screen's text view, as in text practice, and the policy stays unchanged. Spent on the reader, the screenshot budget gives 80.9% full-task success on new task instances, against 55.6% when spent on training the policy. Given the same labeled screenshots, adapting the reader also beats adapting the policy, and a single aligned reader improves every text-trained policy we tried without retraining any of them.","authors":["Zhongling Xu","Hairong Wang"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:58:00Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:58:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22722v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22722v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22722v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22717v1","title":"PCRG: Training-Free Pruning and Caching of Reference Tokens for Video Generation","abstract":"Reference-to-video diffusion transformers pack the variational autoencoder (VAE) latents of all references and the generated tokens into one full-attention sequence, in which reference tokens make up about half. Because attention cost grows quadratically with sequence length, these tokens take up an even larger share of the attention computation, yet most of them are static, smooth or irrelevant to the output. To accelerate reference-conditioned generation, we propose pruning and caching of reference tokens for video generation (PCRG), a training-free, plug-and-play method that uses two signals the model already computes to assign each reference token to one of three tiers, namely pruned, cached or kept. The first is a low-level redundancy r from VAE latents, since a token whose latent barely changes over time or across its neighbors adds little new content. The second is a high-level importance g from the vision-language-model (VLM) conditioner, where the attention that the prompt and any first or last frames pay to a token tells whether the output needs to read it. Tokens that are not redundant are kept, and redundant tokens are cached if important and pruned otherwise. Pruned tokens leave the sequence before the first step with all rotary position embedding positions unchanged. Cached tokens have their keys and values computed once and reused, and kept tokens are processed as usual. On MiniMax-H3, already distilled to 8 timesteps and quantized to int8, PCRG is 1.57x faster end to end on our V2V-Bench and 1.49x faster on the public VACE-Benchmark, while the quality of VBench drops by only 0.24% and 0.17%, respectively. PCRG shortens the sequence rather than changing how attention is computed or which steps are run, so it also stacks on step caching and sparse attention. On V2V-Bench it reaches 1.86x with TeaCache, 1.82x with Spark at nearly unchanged fidelity and 2.15x with Veda.","authors":["Siyuan Wang","Haoran Li","Yi Yue","Tak L. Fung","Quan Chen"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:56:13Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:56:13Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22717v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22717v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22717v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22709v1","title":"Know When to Zoom: How Small Is Too Small for Vision-Language Models?","abstract":"Online vision-language models often miss small details in large images, because the service shrinks the image, or rejects it, before the model ever sees it. A car 20 pixels wide in an 8000 by 6000 drone photo reaches Claude about 6 pixels wide, and OpenAI's default rejects the photo outright. We measure how much detail survives for seven models from four vendors, using an eye exam of printed codes and 601 questions about small objects in real photos. The smallest text a model reads half the time grows from 6 pixels or less on a small image to a predicted 10 to 13 pixels on a 7680 by 4320 photo at default settings. Zooming wins much of the detail back. Splitting each photo into up to nine tiles raises accuracy by 11.1 to 14.5 percentage points for Claude and Gemini, and by avoiding rejections it lifts GPT-6.1 Sol from 50.7% to 83.7%. A two-step zoom, in which the model first names the region it needs, matches or beats tiles on Gemini and comes within 2.5 points of them on Claude, with about 40% of their image tokens. When a detail is lost, Claude and Gemini both say they cannot tell about half the time, but they fail differently in the rest: Gemini gives a confident wrong answer in 43% to 48% of these cases, Claude in only 5% to 9%. A check based on detail size, run before the call, misses most of the gain from tiles on Claude and Gemini, but Claude's own reply is a reliable trigger: tiling only after an unsure reply comes within 1.4 points of always tiling with 62% to 70% of the image tokens. We turn these findings into five rules for when an agent should zoom, and release the eye exam, every model answer and code at https://siddvoh.com/know-when-to-zoom.","authors":["Siddharth Vohra","Min Xu"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:50:14Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:50:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22709v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22709v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22709v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22704v1","title":"Social Signal Bench: Evidence-Weighted Evaluation of Co-occurring Social Signals in Video and Audio","abstract":"Recognizing social signals such as interest and hesitation requires interpreting context-dependent behaviors that may co-occur in conversation. Existing affective computing benchmarks rarely evaluate such co-occurring signals, and conventional multi-label F1 overlooks semantic relationships and annotator disagreement. We introduce Social Signal Bench, a 47-hour densely annotated benchmark for the evaluation of social signal recognition and engagement, comprising two datasets: Social Signal Bench Video (25 hours), covering ten social signals in audiovisual recordings, and Social Signal Bench Audio (22 hours), covering six signals in audio-only recordings. Each dataset pairs a public training set of online conversational video, of which 10 hours are expert-annotated and the remainder pseudo-labeled, with a private expert-labeled test set spanning four conversational domains. We also propose Evidence-Weighted F1 (EW-F1), which weights reference labels by annotator evidence and assigns graded credit to semantically related predictions through optimal per-clip matching. Controlled perturbations demonstrate that EW-F1 distinguishes semantically related errors from unrelated ones, unlike conventional F1. Under a shared evaluation protocol, 7B open models post-trained on Social Signal Bench outperform general-purpose frontier models on video and match them on audio.","authors":["Siddharth Ravi","Anders R. Bargum","Hugo Anselme","Patrizio Cugia di Sant'Orsola","Alberto Cardaci","Flora Kincso Parragi","Vincent Borowiec","Line H. Clemmensen"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:49:04Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:49:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22704v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22704v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22704v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22686v1","title":"CascadeJEPA: Enhancing 3D Geometric Representations via Spatially Coupled Cascaded Prediction","abstract":"Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) have emerged as a promising paradigm for predictive visual representation learning. However, our analysis of I-JEPA reveals a growing discrepancy between semantic and geometric learning: improved semantic recognition during pretraining is accompanied by degraded 3D geometric representations. We hypothesize that this partly stems from predicting highly abstract latent representations with incomplete spatial context, favoring semantic regularities over fine-grained geometric information. To address this issue, we introduce CascadeJEPA, a spatially coupled cascaded prediction framework that promotes richer patch-level interactions through full-grid context encoding and cross-attention between prediction stages, while largely preserving the original I-JEPA architecture. Extensive experiments demonstrate substantial improvements over I-JEPA across multiple 3D geometric tasks while maintaining strong semantic recognition performance. Furthermore, its advantages extend to future feature forecasting and world model planning, highlighting the potential of enhanced geometric representations to support predictive world modeling.","authors":["Kailai Zhou","Ziqi Chen","Xiaoning Xu","Yuefeng Wu","Zixuan Fu","Zijun Lin","Zhihao Li","Youwei Pang","Xiaoqi Zhao","Xun Cao","Bihan Wen"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:36:05Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:36:05Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22686v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22686v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22686v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22673v1","title":"FVScore: Specification-Driven Neuro-Symbolic Video Evaluation","abstract":"We introduce Faithfulness Video Score (FVSCORE), a neuro-symbolic framework for specification-driven video generation evaluation. Existing evaluators rely on holistic MLLM judgments or vision-language metrics, yet aligning their scores with human judgments and tracing them to specific temporal violations remain open challenges. FVSCORE addresses these limitations by compiling each video into explicit propositions capturing prompt requirements and implicit propositions capturing unstated physical, temporal, and cross-modal constraints. It grounds each proposition using specialized perceptual tools and evaluates the resulting confidence traces with soft temporal operators inspired by Linear Temporal Logic. A Significance-Aware Calibration step then aligns verification with human perception by retaining only perceptually significant violations before producing a timestamp- and evidence-grounded report. We conduct extensive experiments across public and complex multi-event benchmarks with the strongest video models to date, demonstrating that FVSCORE outperforms holistic and agent-based evaluators in human correlation while providing highly accurate and interpretable feedback.","authors":["Do Xuan Long","Long T. Le","Yiwen Song","Min-Yen Kan","Tomas Pfister","Yale Song"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:25:33Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:25:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22673v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22673v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22673v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22669v1","title":"Beyond the Camera: A Tri-Modal Driving World Model for Future RGB, Thermal, and LiDAR Forecasting in Adverse Weather","abstract":"Driving world models forecast what a vehicle's sensors will record next. Most forecast camera video and some add LiDAR, but thermal cameras, which still see in darkness and some adverse weather, are rarely a forecast target, and to our knowledge no world model forecasts all three sensors. We study joint forecasting of RGB, thermal, and LiDAR on real adverse-weather sequences of the DSERT-RoLL dataset. We post-train a pretrained video world model for each sensor and represent LiDAR as a depth video aligned with the RGB camera, with densification and encoding chosen by what the model's video tokenizer can reconstruct. A tri-sensor model sees all three sensors as one video. We score LiDAR in meters against measured returns and compare against persistence baselines passed through the same tokenizer as the model. With this matching, an apparent loss of the thermal model to persistence in fog disappears. On all 58 validation sequences in six weather conditions, post-trained RGB and thermal forecasts beat compression-matched persistence. Joint training lowers the LiDAR error at 2s by 2.1m at a small cost in camera fidelity, and only the tri-sensor model's LiDAR forecasts beat persistence in mean error. Blanking one sensor degrades the forecasts of the others more in snow than in fog.","authors":["Faysal Mehrab Chowdhury","Md. Mahfuzur Rahman","Ahmed Rafi Hasan","Kishor Datta Gupta","Mohd Ariful Haque","Savannah Shannon"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:24:09Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:24:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22669v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22669v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22669v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22668v1","title":"Let Samples Sing in Chorus: Mitigating Spurious Correlations via Cross-view Consensus","abstract":"Deep models often rely on spurious correlations that fail on underrepresented groups. Existing methods either correct human-specified bias attributes or infer unknown biases from the learned representation space. However, annotated attributes may differ from the shortcuts actually used by a model, while a single representation provides incomplete and potentially noisy evidence. Our key insight is that a bias obscured in one feature space may be clearer in another. In this work, we propose CHORD (Cross-view Harmonic Outlier Reversal for Debiasing), a multi-view framework that allows samples to \"sing in chorus\" across diverse representation spaces to achieve robust cross-view consensus. CHORD aggregates aggregates within-class partitions in each view, which is a proxy for specifying candidate biases, to debias the target representation and classifier without bias supervision. Experiments on seven benchmarks across vision, language, speech, and medical imaging show consistent improvements in worst-group accuracy. Code and checkpoints are available at https://github.com/davelee-uestc/chord_debiasing_public .","authors":["Weiwei Li","Junzhuo Liu","Tong Chu","Hengfu Yu","Yinjie Lei","Wen Li"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:23:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:23:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22668v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22668v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22668v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22657v1","title":"SpatialCast: Reading the 3D World via Cast Shadows","abstract":"As multimodal large language models (MLLMs) advance toward understanding and interacting with the physical world, spatial reasoning becomes essential for understanding scene structure and anticipating the effects of physical changes. Existing spatial benchmarks have made substantial progress in evaluating spatial relations, memory, and viewpoint reasoning, but less systematically assess how models connect physical scene conditions with visual observations. We introduce SpatialCast, a benchmark that uses cast shadows to evaluate this ability. Cast shadows provide observable evidence of a 3D scene. Their image appearance depends on object geometry and pose, illumination, and viewpoint. We examine this relationship through three complementary tasks that assess scene-projection consistency, predict intervention-induced changes, and infer the physical changes behind visual transitions. The benchmark contains 21,000 synthetic questions with separate scene and geometry test partitions, complemented by 200 SpatialCast-Real questions from 20 photographed scenes. Evaluations show that closed-source MLLMs outperform general-purpose open-weight MLLMs and MLLMs designed for spatial reasoning, which remain near chance on the synthetic benchmark, while even strong closed-source models struggle with shadow-length and light-height judgments. We also use SpatialCast training data for GRPO post-training, improving open-weight MLLM performance on the benchmark. Further evaluations show improvements on several external spatial benchmarks, suggesting transfer of the learned capabilities. These experiments support SpatialCast as a controlled resource for diagnosing spatial reasoning capabilities and studying their learning and generalization.","authors":["Enhan Li","Jinghao Zhou","Jiaze Zhang","Hongyang Du"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:15:08Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:15:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22657v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22657v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22657v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22655v1","title":"Unified Residual Translation for Paired Image Restoration","abstract":"Paired image restoration spans changes in imaging contrast, sensor modality, and spatial resolution. These settings differ in their input channels and target grids, raising the question of how much of the prediction pipeline must be specialized to each task. We present the Unified Residual Translator (URT), which separates fixed channel and scale mapping from learned residual prediction. A deterministic reference uses channel averaging, replication, or bicubic interpolation to match the target dimensions, while a NAFNet encoder-decoder predicts an additive correction from the complete input. Separately trained instances share the prediction rule, $\\ell_1$ objective, and optimizer across MRI contrast synthesis, visible-to-infrared translation, SAR-to-optical translation, and real-camera super-resolution. Across six task and scale settings, URT achieves the highest PSNR and SSIM among the evaluated baselines on IXI, SEN1-2, and RealSR $\\times3$ and $\\times4$. It reaches 30.774dB on IXI and 13.478dB on SEN1-2, with luminance PSNR of 33.596, 28.742, and 27.342dB at the three RealSR scales. On LLVIP, it ranks second in PSNR and SSIM. A study of four spectral alternatives identifies complementary benefits of fitted references and bandwise residual gains. Together, these results support explicit dimensional alignment and learned residual correction as a common formulation for heterogeneous paired imaging tasks.","authors":["Joyanta Jyoti Mondal","Mridul Banik","Ibne Farabi Shihab","Md. Shifatul Ahsan Apurba"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:12:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:12:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22655v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22655v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22655v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22652v1","title":"OmniTongue: A Comprehensive MLLM Benchmark for Automated Tongue Diagnosis in Traditional Chinese Medicine","abstract":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have excelled in Western medical imaging, yet their potential in Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) tongue diagnosis remains systematically unevaluated. Tongue diagnosis requires sophisticated global and local perception, serving as a rigorous touchstone for assessing the visual understanding depth of MLLMs. However, existing datasets are limited by single dimensions, coarse granularity, and ambiguous labels, hindering the evaluation of authentic clinical capabilities. To bridge this gap, we introduce OmniTongue, the first comprehensive, fine-grained Visual Question Answering benchmark for TCM tongue diagnosis. This benchmark integrates 1,351 expert-validated images and 21,616 instruction pairs. Its core design features 16 clinical dimensions for multidimensional evaluation, region-specific annotations for pathological localization, and 66 fine-grained labels for precise diagnostic representation. Extensive evaluations of existing MLLMs reveal significant performance gaps, demonstrating that medical-specific models lack domain advantages, while universally poor Macro-F1 scores expose a reliance on statistical shortcuts rather than genuine understanding of tongue pathology. These findings not only highlight the current limitations of MLLMs in interpreting regional medical features but also underscore OmniTongue's significance for developing robust automated tongue diagnosis models. The code and dataset will be made publicly available in a future release.","authors":["Liuyi Xu","Yun Guo","Shusheng Li","Xuewen Zhang","Yining Qian","An-Yang Lu"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:10:17Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:10:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22652v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22652v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22652v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22645v1","title":"REVEAL: Reconstructing Video Amodal Content via Language and Flow Guidance","abstract":"Amodal perception enables humans to perceive entire objects even when parts are occluded, a remarkable cognitive skill that artificial intelligence struggles to replicate. Recent diffusion-based methods have extended amodal completion from images to videos, yet they lack auxiliary priors to guide the reconstruction of heavily occluded content and to maintain temporal consistency over long sequences. We present REVEAL (REconstructing VidEo Amodal content via Language and flow guidance), a unified diffusion-based framework that integrates complementary motion and semantic priors for video amodal completion. A user-provided text query serves a dual role: it drives open-vocabulary video segmentation to obtain visible masks, and it provides semantic guidance for texture reconstruction. For amodal segmentation, we introduce optical flow guidance: by warping visible masks from previous frames to the current frame, the flow-warped mask propagates visible content into occluded regions and approximates the object's current shape, providing a strong shape prior even under simultaneous deformation and occlusion. For texture completion, text guidance constrains the reconstruction of occluded appearance while providing a stable semantic reference that helps maintain visual coherence throughout the reconstruction. We also contribute LAVAT, a new benchmark featuring long video sequences paired with text descriptions, enabling evaluation of text-guided video amodal completion under heavy occlusion. Extensive experiments demonstrate that REVEAL achieves state-of-the-art performance on existing benchmarks while maintaining high-quality temporal consistency over extended sequences. The project page is available at: https://uark-aicv.github.io/REVEAL/.","authors":["Minh Tran","Winston Bounsavy","Ngan Le"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:00:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:00:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22645v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22645v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22645v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22637v1","title":"OVET-Consensus: Rank-Preserving Counterfactual Event Verification for Video Moment Grounding","abstract":"Modern video moment retrieval systems often propose windows that overlap the target event, yet still select the wrong one. We study this scorer bottleneck on QVHighlights with a fixed QD-DETR-derived temporal expert: the parent-aware temporal expert reaches 64.71 R1@0.5, while diagnostic oracles over the same candidate family reach 75.68 R1@0.5 within the parent-reranked pool and 94.97 R1@0.5 over the full 100-candidate evidence pool. We introduce OVET-Consensus, a rank-preserving counterfactual event verification layer that keeps the temporal expert's ranked list as the default and permits only high-confidence rank-1 overrides from a policy calibrated on held-out training videos. The verifier uses parent-aware interval evidence, structured Qwen3-VL event judgments, VideoLLaMA3 pairwise temporal signals, and dense temporal evidence, but never lets those signals freely reorder the top-K list. On QVHighlights validation, OVET-Consensus improves the parent-aware temporal expert from 64.71 to 65.35 R1@0.5 with a positive paired-bootstrap interval, while mAP is largely unchanged, from 42.67 to 42.74; it makes 20 overrides, with 15 helpful corrections and 3 harmful corrections. The results point to a practical constraint on verifier design: semantic compatibility is useful evidence, but temporal grounding still requires deciding where the event starts, changes, and ends inside a visually compatible scene.","authors":["Alibay Osmanli","Shaogang Gong"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:52:55Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:52:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22637v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22637v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22637v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22608v1","title":"PlaceTally: A Test-Time Memory Model for Constructing Place Graphs","abstract":"Counting distinct objects in a video is hard when the camera returns to places it has seen. A vision-language model that reads the whole video answers with a few small numbers, and one that reads it in parts counts an object again in every part that shows it. Object trackers need the category before the video is read, or a fixed vocabulary. We observe that a memory for counting does not need to know what it is looking for, only where it has been: objects rarely leave a room between visits, so parts of a video grouped by place share almost no objects, and a place can be recognised before any question is asked. Building on this, we introduce PlaceTally, a question-blind test-time memory model that constructs a place graph for each video. Each video fragment joins the place of its best earlier match or opens a new place, because a strong match is rarely wrong while a true return often matches weakly. A frozen vision-language model counts each place once from at most 128 of its frames, so every read has a bounded input and the memory grows with the number of places; the graph is built 15 times faster than real time. On RevisitCount, our benchmark of real room re-recordings, it scores 62.5% mean relative accuracy on test rooms and 61.2% on held-out rooms, against 51.2% and 44.3% for the same model reading the whole video. It stays level as rooms return more or less often, while a fixed correction of summed visits moves from 38.5% to 61.4%.","authors":["Alibay Osmanli","Shaogang Gong"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:23:50Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:23:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22608v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22608v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22608v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22592v1","title":"Distilling World Action Models for Efficient Bimanual Manipulation via Anchored Path Matching","abstract":"World Action Models (WAMs) jointly generate future video and robot actions through iterative denoising. Few-step distillation reduces the number of model evaluations, but its coarse updates can move the student away from the teacher's trajectory. We propose Anchored Path Matching (APM), which supervises endpoint and short-transition predictions from states reached by the student. From each state, we continue the teacher to zero noise and use its endpoint and first intermediate state as targets. Modality-routed adapters learn to match both targets from the same starting state. When the two gradients conflict, anchor-priority updates preserve the endpoint gradient and remove the opposing component of the local gradient within each modality. One checkpoint supports 1-, 2-, and 4-step joint generation. The sampling budget can be chosen at deployment without retraining or teacher queries. On RoboTwin 2.0, we train only on Clean demonstrations and evaluate directly on the domain-randomized Random setting. At one step, APM improves closed-loop success over continued base distillation at matched inference budgets by 2.0 percentage points on Clean and 1.4 on Random. Video peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) rises from 30.169 to 32.317 dB, with gains in structural similarity (SSIM) and learned perceptual image patch similarity (LPIPS). On two real-robot tasks, APM improves success over the one-step teacher and reduces control-cycle latency by $21.3\\times$ relative to the eight-step teacher.Project page: https://jialonglong.github.io/Anchored-Path-Matching/.","authors":["Jialong Liu","Junjie He","Yandu Chen","Bin Zhou","Heng Su","Peijun Tang","Jianan Wang","Changhao Chen"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:15:00Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:15:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22592v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22592v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22592v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22591v1","title":"PACT: Plan-Action Consistency Training by Preserving Correct Plans and Repairing Actions in GUI Agents","abstract":"GUI agents can formulate a correct immediate plan in their reasoning yet emit an action that fails to execute it, creating an execution gap. This recurring mismatch undermines reliable task completion, as a single execution error can derail a multi-step workflow. In outcome-supervised reinforcement learning, broadcasting a negative response-level advantage across all generated tokens risks penalizing valid planning together with erroneous execution. To address this credit-assignment conflict, we introduce PACT (Plan-Action Consistency Training), a training framework that combines reference-aligned plan protection and plan-aligned action repair without altering the original scalar action reward. Using annotated reference actions to identify execution gaps, PACT protects the verified plan by masking negative policy-gradient contributions across its token span. Keeping the generated reasoning unchanged as context, it then favors the reference action over the emitted erroneous action through a preference surrogate with stop-gradient pair weights. Compared with the outcome-only baseline, PACT raises AndroidWorld task success from 60.34% to 68.97% (+8.63 pp), while improving rule-based plan-action consistency on AndroidControl High from 83.71% to 89.74% (+6.03 pp). Adapting PACT to desktop agents also improves task success from 40.92% to 44.17% (+3.25 pp) on the full 369-task OSWorld benchmark. Together, these results establish PACT as an effective credit-assignment framework for bridging execution gaps and improving task completion across mobile and desktop GUI environments.","authors":["Zhuohan Cai","Zhangxuan Gu","Runze Li","Beitong Zhou","Ming Wen","Hongbo Yu","Shuheng Shen","Changhua Meng"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:14:59Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:14:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22591v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22591v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22591v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22577v1","title":"Semantic-Guided Geometric Decoding for Token-Efficient Visual Grounding","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) provide a unified interface for language-conditioned visual perception. In pixel-to-sequence grounding, autoregressive coordinate generation requires multiple sequential decoding steps per box, increasing inference cost with the number of instances. Pixel-to-embedding methods reduce this overhead but lack explicit spatial encoding when using a shared special token. We introduce SemGeo, a token-efficient visual grounding framework that represents each instance with a single grid token for coarse localization. A geometric decoder reuses visual features and hidden states to recover precise discrete box coordinates in parallel. To train proposal generation and box localization together, we introduce Gaussian soft labels that encode coordinate distances during supervised fine-tuning (SFT). Reinforcement post-training further combines box-set rewards with coordinate supervision on model-generated proposals. This stage trains the language model and geometric decoder on their own predictions to mitigate the mismatch between teacher-forced training and inference. Across eight benchmarks spanning object detection, referring object detection, and document layout grounding, SemGeo achieves the highest F1@mIou among compared MLLMs on six benchmarks. It also delivers $2.07\\times$ and $1.25\\times$ the mean box-generation throughput of representative grounding baselines under vLLM and Transformers, respectively, while achieving higher accuracy in both comparisons.","authors":["Zhaojie Chen","Junyan Yang","Le Yang","Qi Dang","Yucheng Huang","Qianyi Che","Zhaoqiang Chen","Xinqi Zhu","Qiang Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:02:17Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:02:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22577v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22577v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22577v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"}],"favorites":[],"latest":{"generated_at_utc":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","category":"cs.CV","keywords":["video","retrieval","3d","agent","representation","instance","multimodal","diffusion"],"fetch_window_hours":72,"daily_window_hours":30,"match_count":71,"new_or_recent_papers":[{"id":"2611.23228v1","title":"Spool: Low-Latency Streaming Inference for Hybrid Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Applications such as visual accessibility agents and robotics require answering questions about recent video content promptly, over a bounded window of past frames (e.g., the last two minutes). Hybrid vision-language models (VLMs), which interleave attention with linear recurrent layers such as Gated DeltaNet (GDN), are attractive for this setting, but maintaining a sliding window is expensive. The reason is that recurrent layers combine all past frames into a shared state, so expiring old frames normally requires re-running the entire window through the model. We observe that the recurrent update is affine in the state with input-dependent coefficients, so the effect of each frame can be captured as a transformation that composes associatively. We present Spool, a training-free framework that caches per-frame state transformations and composes them efficiently as the window advances, removing expired frames from each recurrent layer's state without replaying frames through the backbone. Removal is exact within each layer; as with key-value eviction in attention, expired frames can retain residual influence through the cached inputs computed by earlier layers. We design a dedicated data structure and GPU kernels to achieve low caching and composition overhead. Across five open-source hybrid VLMs and four video reasoning benchmarks, Spool achieves the lowest p95 time-to-first-token in all 20 model-benchmark tests, reducing it by a geometric mean of $6.6\\times$ over the faster of two recent vLLM baselines, while improving accuracy by 2.3 percentage points on average.","authors":["Rui Ning","Om Chabra","Hari Balakrishnan"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:59:27Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:59:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23228v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23228v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23228v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":86149,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Applications such as visual accessibility agents and robotics require answering questions about recent video content promptly, over a bounded window of past frames (e.g., the last two minutes). Hybrid vision-language models (VLMs), which interleave attention...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23226v1","title":"FastFrames: Video Generation at Interactive Latency","abstract":"Autoregressive video diffusion models generate frames based on new observations and inputs, making them a promising medium for interactive visual experiences. However, existing models are orders of magnitude too slow for fluid interaction. This paper introduces FastFrames, a family of fast autoregressive video diffusion models that achieves interactive latency without compromising quality. We profile the inference pipeline of state-of-the-art models and find that operations often treated as fixed implementation details, such as VAE decoding and KV cache updates, in fact account for over half of end-to-end inference latency. We challenge the assumption that these components are indispensable and replace them with lightweight alternatives. Our 1.3B variant runs at 36 ms per frame, or 28 responses per second (RPS), on a single B200 GPU while matching the generation quality of a state-of-the-art model that is nearly 8x slower. Our 14B variant runs at 45 ms per frame, or 22 RPS, on an 8x B200 GPU node and yields significantly better visual quality and realism. We show that FastFrames enables real-time interaction in applications such as camera-conditioned and physics-conditioned video generation.","authors":["Gene Chou","Guandao Yang","Fujun Luan","Lars Mescheder","Wei Dong","Stephan Richter","Vladlen Koltun"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:59:13Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:59:13Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23226v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23226v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23226v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":72450,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Autoregressive video diffusion models generate frames based on new observations and inputs, making them a promising medium for interactive visual experiences. However, existing models are orders of magnitude too slow for fluid interaction. This paper introduc...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23212v1","title":"EviSlide-MIL: Multi-Query Pooling and Shared Prediction for Class-Incremental Whole-Slide Image Classification","abstract":"Histopathological analysis of whole-slide images (WSIs) plays a critical role in cancer diagnosis, and multiple instance learning (MIL) is widely adopted for WSI analysis under weak supervision. As new tumor subtypes are introduced, extending existing MIL models requires learning new classes while retaining knowledge of previously learned ones. This presents a class-incremental learning challenge, where models must acquire new classes with limited access to historical training data and recognize all learned classes without task identifiers at inference. A major obstacle is forgetting, as updates to feature aggregation and prediction modules can degrade performance on previously learned classes. To address this challenge, we propose \\textbf{EviSlide-MIL}, a class-incremental WSI framework that integrates multiple learnable queries per class with a shared prediction module. The queries aggregate frozen patch features into class-specific representations, while the shared prediction module limits parameter growth across incremental stages. By freezing queries associated with previously learned classes, EviSlide-MIL preserves their attention patterns for identical inputs, while experience replay and knowledge distillation further mitigate forgetting as shared parameters are updated. Experiments on eight tumor subtypes from The Cancer Genome Atlas demonstrate that EviSlide-MIL achieves improved performance and reduced forgetting compared with competing methods across most learning settings, demonstrating its potential for scalable and knowledge-preserving WSI classification. Code and pretrained models will be released.","authors":["Zhouhan Qian","Sicheng Chen","Chad Wong","Tianyi Zhang","Enhui Chai","Zeyu Liu","Fei Xia"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:50:42Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:50:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23212v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23212v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23212v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":70817,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Histopathological analysis of whole-slide images (WSIs) plays a critical role in cancer diagnosis, and multiple instance learning (MIL) is widely adopted for WSI analysis under weak supervision. As new tumor subtypes are introduced, extending existing MIL mod...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23211v1","title":"Multimodal Masked Diffusion Autoencoder for Modeling Impaired Driving","abstract":"Human cognitive state (e.g., distraction, stress), gaze, and behavior interact with the driving environment in subtle ways that AI could use to assist drivers, but ground-truth labels for human cognitive state levels are costly to obtain at scale. We propose a multimodal masked-diffusion autoencoder, ID-MAE, that adds a self-supervised objective to impairment prediction: alongside predicting the impairment label as a masked token, it reconstructs masked gaze and trajectory. To probe the effects of different modalities and types of impairments on model inference, we introduce DIVERT, a driving-simulator dataset containing 103 hours of driving data (n = 109) with ten impairment inductions spanning cognitive, visual, and emotional states, recorded with gaze, vehicle state, and scene video. We demonstrate how ID-MAE affords significant improvements in impairment inference results on both the DIVERT dataset and existing datasets, and enables strong results in additional tasks, such as trajectory and gaze prediction. For location-matched pairs, our model achieves a paired AUC of 0.706 for impairment detection on DIVERT, compared to 0.590 without reconstruction loss, improving over existing baselines. We probe the design space in terms of input modalities, masking scheme, positional encoding, attention mechanism, and decoding. We also investigate the interaction between various impairments in training and inference, demonstrating that visually demanding distractions are separable from cognitive distractions. Code and data will be available upon acceptance.","authors":["John Gideon","Anshul Gupta","Megan Applegate-Kenton","Kayli Battel","Todd J Rowell","Patricio Reyes Gomez","Laporsha Dees","Emily Sumner","Avinash Balachandran","Guy Rosman"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:50:22Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:50:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23211v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23211v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23211v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":69357,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Human cognitive state (e.g., distraction, stress), gaze, and behavior interact with the driving environment in subtle ways that AI could use to assist drivers, but ground-truth labels for human cognitive state levels are costly to obtain at scale. We propose...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23208v1","title":"Distortion-Free Semantic Watermarks for Black-Box Generative Models","abstract":"We present a provably distortion-free semantic watermarking algorithm that requires only black-box sampling access to a generative model and black-box access to a semantic embedding. A watermark is called distortion-free if, averaged over its secret detection key, it leaves the generator's output distribution unchanged; this provides a formal guarantee that watermarking does not reduce output quality. Conventional such watermarks are often brittle to transformations. This is especially problematic for images, where benign operations such as compression, cropping, or resizing change the entire pixel representation. Semantic watermarks therefore require detection to be robust to content-preserving transformations. Previous methods combining these properties rely heavily on specific white-box properties of the underlying model: either to autoregressive text generation or to diffusion-specific access, such as control of the initial noise or diffusion inversion. In contrast, our construction is fully generic: It only interacts with the generative model and the semantic embedding, a function that determines the notion of \"preserving content\" under which detection should be robust, via sample and query access. We then instantiate this framework for image generation. We characterize the two competing properties required of the semantic embedding: it should remain stable under transformations that preserve an image's content, but should distinguish independent generations from the same prompt. We train an image encoder directly for this tradeoff. The resulting encoder improves separation while maintaining stability over off-the-shelf alternatives and enables strong aggregate detection while remaining robust to various transformations. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our watermark scheme on both open-source and closed models, which is possible only due to restriction to black-box access.","authors":["Aviv Yossef","Or Patashnik","Or Zamir"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:49:17Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:49:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23208v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23208v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23208v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":178010,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present a provably distortion-free semantic watermarking algorithm that requires only black-box sampling access to a generative model and black-box access to a semantic embedding. A watermark is called distortion-free if, averaged over its secret detection...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23189v1","title":"Retrieval-Augmented Correction for Generalist Medical Image Segmentation","abstract":"Automated segmentation is routinely used as the first step in quantitative image-based clinical workflows, where predicted masks are subsequently reviewed and corrected by human operators. Automated correction of the residual structural errors is currently addressed one anatomy at a time, so the number of trained correctors scales with the number of segmentation targets. We present GRACE, a retrieval-augmented refiner that edits an input mask under context drawn from a shared knowledge base of verified prior cases. The refiner is trained once on a multi-organ corpus spanning compact organs, cavities, branching vessels, and tumour lesions. In the held-out dataset-transfer evaluation, three held-out datasets query a fixed knowledge base built from training data only. A separate leave-one-anatomy-out experiment tests parameter-free index extension: verified cases of an unseen solid organ enter the knowledge base, and the refiner stays frozen. A light shape-aware projection head on a frozen multimodal encoder maps embedding similarity to the shape agreement of the masks, so the knowledge base returns cases that the refiner can use as a shape prior. We evaluate GRACE on 18 public datasets spanning CT, MRI and ultrasound, using nine refinement configurations and seven upstream segmentors that span weak, mid, and strong operating regimes. We report each change with the DSC of its input mask. On the weak tier, the input DSC is 0.386 and GRACE adds up to +0.35 DSC. On a strong segmentor, the input DSC is 0.932 and the mean change is small. Transfer to an imaging modality that is absent from training and from the index is partial and depends on the anatomy, and remains an open research direction. Our results suggest retrieval-conditioned refinement is a practical alternative to anatomy-specific correction models. Code: https://github.com/robustml-eurecom/grace","authors":["Vincenzo Marcianò","Xiaoming Zhang","Michela Antonelli","Sebastien Ourselin","Maria A Zuluaga"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:33:42Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:33:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23189v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23189v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23189v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":96885,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Automated segmentation is routinely used as the first step in quantitative image-based clinical workflows, where predicted masks are subsequently reviewed and corrected by human operators. Automated correction of the residual structural errors is currently ad...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23187v1","title":"Any2Building: Unifying Point Clouds and Images for Building Reconstruction via Shared Spatial Queries","abstract":"Abstracting structured buildings from limited observations is essential for 3D urban modeling and digital twins. Partial point clouds and overhead images of the same building provide incomplete 3D surface measurements and visual cues to its footprint, roof geometry, and rooftop structures, respectively. This motivates a unified framework that aligns geometric and visual cues in 3D and reconstructs buildings from either or both modalities. Building on this connection, we present Any2Building, a unified model for Level of Detail 2 (LoD2) building reconstruction from partial point clouds, overhead images, or both, with optional coarse geometric priors. At its core is an occupancy-grounded shared spatial queries representation that organizes available observations within a common 3D grid. Supervised by complete building surface voxels, these queries connect observational evidence to target geometry, enabling a shared generator to learn structural priors across tasks and providing spatial guidance for recovering missing structure. A trained voxel conditional surface-generation and low-poly remeshing pipeline then converts the predicted structure into compact LoD2 building models. To support this framework, we introduce BuildingWorld-Image, comprising approximately one million paired samples of partial point clouds, overhead images, and LoD2 building models across 18 cities. Experiments on multiple benchmarks demonstrate that Any2Building outperforms existing methods in geometric reconstruction accuracy across unimodal and multimodal point-cloud completion, single-image building generation, and prior-assisted generation, while maintaining detailed and robust recovery when point cloud input modality is degraded.","authors":["Hongye Hou","Zhuoxiao Li","Liu Xiangyu","Tengxi Wang","Jing Ou","Dongli Wu","Shuai Zhang","Tongyan Hua","Wufan Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:32:04Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:32:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23187v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23187v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23187v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":92135,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, representation, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Abstracting structured buildings from limited observations is essential for 3D urban modeling and digital twins. Partial point clouds and overhead images of the same building provide incomplete 3D surface measurements and visual cues to its footprint, roof ge...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23161v1","title":"Attribution Through the Decoder: Prediction-Space Explanations for Structured Outputs","abstract":"Networks that predict angles, gaze directions, and rotations do not output these quantities directly: they emit raw coordinates that a fixed decoder, such as normalization, maps to the final prediction. Attribution methods are usually applied to the raw coordinates, so they can credit inputs for changes the decoder discards, such as rescaling a vector when only its direction matters. The resulting explanation can highlight inputs that have no effect on the prediction. We instead attribute through the decoder differential, which for normalization decoders reduces to a scaled tangent projection. We characterize invariance to output rescaling, show that every fixed smooth scalar target on a sphere has points of zero tangential sensitivity, and show that an endpoint-projected variant of Integrated Gradients can still attribute to scale-only inputs, whereas direct angular Integrated Gradients cannot. On synthetic tasks with analytically known relevance, ambient attribution assigns up to 72% of its mass to an irrelevant region. On MPIIGaze validation data, tangent Grad-CAM produces 44% greater prediction displacement under 10% pixel deletion. On PASCAL3D+ with a 6D rotation decoder, the full differential improves mean deletion displacement by 14.1 degrees over the ambient coordinate sum and by 13.8 degrees over a decoder-invariant scalar. Results for a two-polarity tangent LayerCAM variant are mixed: it exceeds ambient-sum LayerCAM on circular regression but no difference is detected on quaternion pose. These results separate two failure sources, radial contamination and scalarization loss. The approach uses existing attribution methods with decoder-aware targets, although some variants also require changes to attribution aggregation. An invariant attribution can still miss inputs that drive the prediction, so invariance and deletion performance should each be checked.","authors":["Zaer Bin Zaqy","Brinnae Bent"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:13:50Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:13:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23161v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23161v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23161v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":157027,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Networks that predict angles, gaze directions, and rotations do not output these quantities directly: they emit raw coordinates that a fixed decoder, such as normalization, maps to the final prediction. Attribution methods are usually applied to the raw coord...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:19+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23155v1","title":"TextSphere: Efficient Text-to-Image Generation via Shared Hyperspherical Alignment and Uniformity","abstract":"Recent advances in text-to-image generation have substantially improved image quality, particularly through diffusion-based methods that require multiple inference steps. In contrast, Sphere Encoder offers an efficient generative framework capable of producing images with fewer forward steps. However, extending it to free-form text is nontrivial, because its class-conditioned design lacks cross-modal alignment. To address this challenge, we propose TextSphere, an efficient text-to-image generative framework that extends Sphere Encoder with language conditioning and cross-modal alignment. We incorporate pretrained CLIP text embeddings through adaptive layer normalization (AdaLN) to guide image generation while retaining the original spherical sampling mechanism. To bridge the cross-modal representation gap, we introduce a Spherical Semantic Alignment and Uniformity (SSAU) objective that jointly promotes semantic alignment and representation uniformity on the hypersphere. Experiments on CUB-200-2011, MS-COCO-2014, and Flickr30k demonstrate that TextSphere consistently reduces generation FID (gFID) over the text-conditioned baseline while achieving reconstruction FID (rFID) below 1.0. With only 4 and 6 sampling steps, TextSphere achieves lower gFID than the compared diffusion- and GAN-based models trained from scratch on CUB-200-2011 and MS-COCO-2014, respectively. We further observe that improved reconstruction fidelity does not necessarily yield better generation quality. In addition, we use Partial Least Squares Singular Value Decomposition (PLS-SVD) to analyze cross-modal alignment and modality gap, offering insight into the effectiveness of our approach.","authors":["Yixia Zhang","Girish A. Koushik","Xilu Wang","Wenwu Wang"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:08:53Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:08:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23155v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23155v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23155v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":81782,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Recent advances in text-to-image generation have substantially improved image quality, particularly through diffusion-based methods that require multiple inference steps. In contrast, Sphere Encoder offers an efficient generative framework capable of producin...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23153v1","title":"VIVID: Beyond Static Multi-Image Reasoning with Localized Visual Differences and Textual Entity Dynamics in Video MLLMs","abstract":"Video question answering under tight frame budgets requires preserving localized motion and event order, yet current video multimodal large language models (MLLMs) reduce comprehension to static multi-image reasoning by discarding unselected frames and encoding keyframes independently: (1) Spatially, whole-frame pooling dilutes small moving targets into static backgrounds; (2) Temporally, dropping intermediate frames or averaging tokens erases inter-frame motion, confusing direction, speed, and order. We propose VIVID (Visual Inter-frame Variations and Individual Dynamics), which moves beyond static snapshots like human vision: keyframes capture core states while inter-frame Patch Diffs preserve neighboring changes within $K_f$ keyframes. Autonomous Delta-Driven Keyframe Planning (ADKP) selects anchors from a $3\\text{ fps}$ pool and feeds surrounding patch differences into Dual-Road Change Memory (DRCM), forming Dynamic Tokens at zero extra anchor tokens alongside a camera-stabilized Active Motion Region of Interest (ROI) and chronological Entity State Chains. Adding $0.22\\%$ trainable parameters to a frozen $27\\text{B}$ backbone, VIVID-Small ($50$ examples, $\\sim 1.5$ GPU hours) beats all eleven baselines in overall accuracy across $8,083$ questions and trails only VIVID-Large ($3,600$ examples), which leads on MotionBench, Video-MME, and TempCompass.","authors":["Chengqian Ma","Yuanze Zeng","Hongyu Wang","Mingfeng Lin","YiCheng Xiao","Yu Chen","Pengyu Li","WenHao Wang","Yuexiao Ma","Xiawu Zheng","Fei Chao"],"published":"2026-11-17T18:05:51Z","updated":"2026-11-17T18:05:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23153v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23153v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23153v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":74774,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Video question answering under tight frame budgets requires preserving localized motion and event order, yet current video multimodal large language models (MLLMs) reduce comprehension to static multi-image reasoning by discarding unselected frames and encodi...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23144v1","title":"$π^4$: A Foundation Model for 4D Scene Geometry and Motion","abstract":"$π^4$ is a unified foundation model for 4D scene perception. From diverse, uncalibrated RGB observations in the wild---a single image, an unordered collection, or a video sequence---it recovers dense geometry, camera poses, and metric scale, together with 2D/3D point tracks and moving-object masks for multi-image inputs. It couples a geometry branch with a geometry-conditioned motion branch, which predicts image-space correspondences that are lifted into 3D using reconstructed target-view geometry. Across diverse benchmarks, $π^4$ achieves the best overall average rank in geometry evaluation, alongside competitive performance in single- and multiview 2D/3D point tracking and moving-object segmentation. With a shared backbone, the full geometry-and-motion system contains approximately 0.5B parameters---less than half the size of most baselines. It produces joint reconstructions and tracks in one forward pass, including large-scale scenes with thousands of input images despite training with contexts of at most 64 views. We further present the data and training recipe underlying these capabilities, together with empirical studies of key design choices. Code and models are available at https://github.com/yyfz/Pi4.","authors":["Yifan Wang","Weiguang Zhao","Ni Yang","Haoyu Guo","Junting Dong","Weinan Zhang","Chunhua Shen"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:59:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:59:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23144v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23144v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23144v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":101684,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：$π^4$ is a unified foundation model for 4D scene perception. From diverse, uncalibrated RGB observations in the wild---a single image, an unordered collection, or a video sequence---it recovers dense geometry, camera poses, and metric scale, together with 2D/...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23143v1","title":"TCP4D: A Unified Tool-Center-Point Tracking and Generation Model for Robot Manipulation","abstract":"Robot manipulation videos provide a rich visual experience, yet recovering robot action and gripper-state annotations from RGB observations still remains challenging. Using the embodiment-agnostic tool-center-point (TCP) pose as the action space, we present TCP4D, a unified model in which a shared 4D scene representation both tracks observed TCP motion and generates future TCP trajectories. Given a monocular video and initial 2D TCP queries, the tracker directly recovers metric TCP poses in SE(3) and gripper states alongside scene geometry and camera parameters. The generator samples local features from the same 4D representation along the recovered TCP tracks, then combines them with robot observations and language instructions to predict future TCP states. Our new cross-embodiment augmentation pipeline converts existing RGB videos into demonstrations for alternative embodiments by retargeting the recovered motion and synthesizing the corresponding observations, without recorded source-robot states. Experiments show that the tracker achieves absolute trajectory errors of 7.60 and 16.07 mm on DROID and RoboLab and adapts to a new embodiment with only ten demonstrations. The generator achieves 69.19% mean success on RoboTwin and outperforms well-established policy models on two real-world tasks, while joint training on source and augmented demonstrations improves target-robot success by 41.5% over augmented-only training.","authors":["Haosong Peng","Xiangyang Liu","Zhengshen Zhang","Yunfeng Fan","Tianyu Qi","Xin Xia","Yufeng Zhan","Wenchao Xu"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:59:42Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:59:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23143v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23143v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23143v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":115622,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Robot manipulation videos provide a rich visual experience, yet recovering robot action and gripper-state annotations from RGB observations still remains challenging. Using the embodiment-agnostic tool-center-point (TCP) pose as the action space, we present T...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23140v1","title":"GameRealm: Grounding Visual Learning in Interactive Games","abstract":"Visual foundation models are central to physical intelligence, yet their development remains constrained by fragmented training datasets, inconsistent annotation quality, and limited multimodal supervision. To address these challenges, we introduce GameRealm, a large-scale, richly annotated dataset that grounds visual observations in synchronized, game-native supervision. GameRealm encompasses over 120 million RGB frames and 1,000 hours of gameplay footage across all views. Beyond its unprecedented scale, GameRealm provides rich multimodal supervision, including metric depth, camera parameters, synchronized audio, control inputs, and 3D point trajectories. Its diversity further extends to flexible view configurations, spanning single-view, stereo, multi-view, and panoramic recordings. To enable scalable acquisition across diverse game environments, we develop a unified data collection pipeline that combines human-operated interaction with agentic exploration. Extensive experiments across diverse visual learning tasks demonstrate that GameRealm consistently strengthens visual foundation models in both visual generation and geometric learning. These results establish GameRealm as a versatile and valuable data resource, unlocking new opportunities for advancing visual intelligence.","authors":["Weiguang Zhao","Yifan Wang","Ni Yang","Yating Wang","Xingyu Miao","Yankai Jiang","Mulin Yu","Tao Lu","Haoyu Guo","Junting Dong","Weinan Zhang","Chunhua Shen"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:57:22Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:57:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23140v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23140v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23140v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":99167,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d, agent, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Visual foundation models are central to physical intelligence, yet their development remains constrained by fragmented training datasets, inconsistent annotation quality, and limited multimodal supervision. To address these challenges, we introduce GameRealm,...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23127v1","title":"The Illusion of Grounding: Do Vision-Language Models Weigh Retrieved Text and Images Like an Ideal Observer?","abstract":"Retrieval-augmented vision-language models (VLMs) answer from two sources of evidence that can each fail: the retrieved text can be wrong or noisy, and the image can be degraded or contradicted. We introduce GIB-200K, 200,000 image-question items built from COCO and Visual Genome, each expanded into controlled stress conditions: hard-negative retrieval noise (1-20 documents), visual ambiguity (Gaussian and motion blur, low-resource crops) and cross-modal conflict with counterfactual flip pairs. Instead of only measuring degradation, we ask how models combine the two modalities. From ideal Bayesian cue combination we derive a testable prediction that yields formal definitions of Knowledge Inertia (text over-weighted relative to the model's own image evidence) and Sensory Capture (the reverse), and we estimate the weights with a measurement-error-corrected regression whose recovery of known weights we verify. A counterfactual flip decomposition separates modality bias from concept priors, and a Grounding Illusion Score measures how much clean accuracy is not grounded in the image. We evaluate 4 open models on one frozen, seeded evaluation split of 2,000 GIB-200K items. All models are accurate on clean inputs (92.5%-94.2%) but adverse accuracy falls to 41.3%-59.9%. The two LLaVA models over-weight the text (Knowledge Inertia, w_T/w_I = 1.229 and 1.322), whereas InternVL3 favours the image (w_T/w_I = 0.680); between 34.8% and 71.4% of the clean answers that are correct are abandoned when the document lies. A training-free consistency check that compares the answer with and without retrieval trades coverage for accuracy under misleading documents, while falling back to the image fails when the image is heavily degraded. All per-instance outputs, code, and an audit that recomputes every reported open-model number are released.","authors":["Basavaraj R M","Harshal Chandel"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:45:28Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:45:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23127v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23127v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23127v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":44070,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Retrieval-augmented vision-language models (VLMs) answer from two sources of evidence that can each fail: the retrieved text can be wrong or noisy, and the image can be degraded or contradicted. We introduce GIB-200K, 200,000 image-question items built from C...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23113v1","title":"LUTBench: Benchmarking Text-Driven Photographic Look Transfer","abstract":"Photographic color grading shapes the tone and color of a photograph to achieve a desired look. Text-driven photographic look transfer lets users specify these adjustments in natural language, with the goal of reproducing the requested look across photographs while preserving their content. However, prior research on text-driven color grading uses different datasets, text instructions, target definitions, and metrics. These differences make it difficult to compare how faithfully the methods reproduce the same look. To compare methods under a shared protocol, we introduce LUTBench. We collect 91 three-dimensional lookup tables (3D LUTs) and define one photographic look per LUT with a specified input conversion. Applying these looks to 5,000 MIT-Adobe FiveK photographs produces 455,000 input-target image pairs with reproducible target images. For each look, we use templates to turn measured tone and color changes on fixed color samples into candidate clauses. We use training image measurements to verify applicable clauses before composing the text description. The evaluation measures target fidelity, tests sensitivity to predefined input conversions, and separates generalization to unseen looks from reproduction of seen looks on new photographs. Across 16 methods, stronger text alignment does not guarantee target fidelity. However, most supervised variants improve fidelity on both seen and unseen looks.","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Boyu Wei","Chengkun Yue","Xuanhua Yin","Yiyang Li","Chupeng Liu","Zihan Deng","Weidong Cai"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:34:32Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:34:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23113v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23113v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23113v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":110115,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Photographic color grading shapes the tone and color of a photograph to achieve a desired look. Text-driven photographic look transfer lets users specify these adjustments in natural language, with the goal of reproducing the requested look across photographs...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23088v1","title":"Event-Based Person Detection for Maritime UAV Search","abstract":"Maritime search and rescue requires locating people in the water quickly, motivating the use of UAVs to survey the search area. During flight, camera motion and the longer exposures used in low light can blur small targets and hinder person detection. Event cameras record brightness changes as they occur, reducing the motion blur associated with longer exposure times. We investigate their use for maritime person detection by generating synthetic events using the v2e event simulator from public MOBDrone rescue videos and retaining the existing annotations. We compare the recurrent vision transformer model RVT-T with YOLOv8n and RT-DETR-R18 trained on exposure-matched images. On 843 test samples from held-out recordings, RVT-T achieves 15.63% person AP50:95, compared with 6.89% for YOLOv8n and 9.44% for RT-DETR-R18 under 100 ms synthetic exposure. Increasing exposure times from 50 to 200 ms progressively reduces frame-based detection accuracy, widening the gap to the event model. To evaluate the feasibility of on-board processing, we deploy our detector on a Jetson Orin Nano platform. The RVT-T model retains its accuracy and requires approximately 136 ms and 2.02 J per sample, allowing real-time processing on the UAV.","authors":["Zijie Ning","Patrick Vandewalle"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:11:00Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:11:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23088v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23088v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23088v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":38417,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Maritime search and rescue requires locating people in the water quickly, motivating the use of UAVs to survey the search area. During flight, camera motion and the longer exposures used in low light can blur small targets and hinder person detection. Event c...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23085v1","title":"Transfer Once, Ask Locally: Compact Audio-Visual Memory for On-Device Question Answering","abstract":"Audio-visual question answering on lightweight user devices requires balancing speed, memory usage, privacy, and communication. One approach is to run audio-visual encoders on a server and a lightweight language decoder on the user device, keeping questions local while offloading media encoding. However, transferring and processing the full multimodal representation remains costly. We propose a training-free, query-agnostic token pruning framework that constructs a compact, reusable memory before transmission. We define video spatio-temporal blocks and aligned audio windows as players and estimate their importance through Monte Carlo Shapley valuation. We use an embedding coverage objective that combines query-independent utility with cross-modal redundancy calibration and rewards unimodal diversity through marginal coverage gains. We retain the original token embeddings of high-scoring players under a limited token budget and transfer the resulting memory once for reuse across subsequent local questions. Experiments on Video-MME, Daily-Omni, and Video-Holmes using video-SALMONN 2+ (3B) and video-SALMONN 2 Pro (4B) show that our method achieves the best or tied-best QA accuracy among the compared compression methods across all evaluated settings. On Daily-Omni with video-SALMONN 2+ (3B) at 80\\% pruning, our method improves accuracy by 12.86--14.62 percentage points over these methods while achieving the lowest device-side LLM prefill and decoding latency, with up to $1.92\\times$ decoding speedup at comparable embedding memory usage.","authors":["Qitong Wang","Jihui Jin","Divya Kothandaraman","Vishnu Raj","Christopher Rasmussen"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:09:48Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:09:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23085v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23085v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23085v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":48293,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, representation, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Audio-visual question answering on lightweight user devices requires balancing speed, memory usage, privacy, and communication. One approach is to run audio-visual encoders on a server and a lightweight language decoder on the user device, keeping questions l...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:20+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23078v1","title":"UnrealEvent: Synthetic Dataset Generation and Annotation for Event-Based Vision","abstract":"Event-based cameras provide high temporal resolution, low power consumption, and a high dynamic range, but progress in event-based object detection remains constrained by the scarcity of annotated training data. To solve this issue, this paper introduces the tool UnrealEvent, a synthetic dataset-generation pipeline implemented in Unreal Engine 5.5. The pipeline combines a V2E-inspired event-frame generator with EasySynth semantic-rendering passes, allowing rendered level sequences to be converted into paired event representations and YOLO-format bounding-box annotations without manual labeling. Events are generated from per-pixel log-luminance differences between consecutive frames, with contrast thresholds sampled independently from a normal distribution to emulate the fabrication-induced threshold mismatch of real sensors. Using this pipeline, we provide a synthetic dataset and evaluate YOLOv8 detectors under synthetic, zero-shot real, and real fine-tuning training on eTraM. The results show strong in-domain synthetic performance but a substantial synthetic-to-real gap, especially for pedestrians. Fine-tuning on a small real subset improves real-domain performance, while the remaining gap highlights the importance of asset diversity and sensor calibration.","authors":["Yentel Schoovaerts","Zijie Ning","Patrick Vandewalle"],"published":"2026-11-17T17:04:41Z","updated":"2026-11-17T17:04:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23078v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23078v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23078v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":39216,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Event-based cameras provide high temporal resolution, low power consumption, and a high dynamic range, but progress in event-based object detection remains constrained by the scarcity of annotated training data. To solve this issue, this paper introduces the...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:21+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23056v1","title":"RFW-S: Everyday Deformable Objects with Independent Simulation Verification","abstract":"Large-scale robotic simulation requires diverse deformable objects with executable physical properties and realistic interaction behaviors. However, existing 3D asset collections primarily emphasize visual diversity, while scalable physical construction and verification remain challenging. We introduce RFW-S, a large-scale dataset of 83,365 simulation-verified assets spanning 485 everyday categories across five structural families: soft solids, cloth sheets, shells, garments, and bags. We develop an automated pipeline integrating reference-guided 3D generation, structure-aware physical modeling, and LLM-assisted parameter estimation and calibration. To ensure physical reliability, we introduce an independent simulation verification framework that evaluates object behaviors under interactions distinct from those used for parameter calibration. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of independent verification and characterize deformable responses under different physical configurations. We further evaluate robotic grasping in terms of both task success and object integrity, and demonstrate the physical realism of our simulated objects through sim-to-real replay of fragile-object manipulation. RFW-S provides a scalable foundation for physically grounded simulation and embodied robotic learning.","authors":["Jiarui Guo","Saining Zhang","Jiaying Wan","Jiawei Weng","Junhao Chen","Rui Zhang","Yuanbo Lyu","Zhiheng Lee","Peishuo Li","Jingrun Du","Weiqing Xiao","Weiyi Lin","Zenan Meng","Hu Wei","Lin Qu","Zongzheng Zhang","Hao Zhao","Chaojian Li"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:49:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:49:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23056v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23056v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23056v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":96484,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Large-scale robotic simulation requires diverse deformable objects with executable physical properties and realistic interaction behaviors. However, existing 3D asset collections primarily emphasize visual diversity, while scalable physical construction and v...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:21+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23055v1","title":"FrameRouter: Frame Budget Routing for Long-Video Understanding on Video-MME-v2 and Beyond","abstract":"Long-video question answering benchmarks like Video-MME-v2 typically evaluate systems under a uniform frame budget for every question, despite stark variation in the evidence each query actually demands. We analyze this mismatch on Video-MME-v2 and find that different question subsets prefer different frozen frame-budget policies, while uniformly increasing the frame count does not reliably improve all metrics. These findings motivate fixed-mean frame-budget routing, a controlled inference setting in which each question may receive a different frame budget while the average budget matches a uniform reference. We introduce FrameRouter, a training-free test-time framework that combines an evidence-demand router, a budget-constrained allocation rule, and a frozen bank of frame-sampling policies. By controlling how much visual evidence each question receives while leaving the underlying samplers unchanged, FrameRouter is orthogonal to query-aware frame selection. Across Video-MME-v2 and several long-video understanding benchmarks, FrameRouter improves long-video question answering by using the same average visual budget more selectively across questions. Code is available at https://github.com/bingjunluo/FrameRouter.","authors":["Bingjun Luo","Jialin Guo","Siqi Li"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:49:21Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:49:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23055v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23055v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23055v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":58063,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Long-video question answering benchmarks like Video-MME-v2 typically evaluate systems under a uniform frame budget for every question, despite stark variation in the evidence each query actually demands. We analyze this mismatch on Video-MME-v2 and find that...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:21+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23054v1","title":"ReTouchGS: Ultralight Render-free Aesthetic Color Enhancement and Transfer for 3D Gaussian Splatting","abstract":"3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) enables photorealistic scene reconstruction. Existing 3DGS color transfer and interactive editing methods are guided by reference images and user-specified color adjustments. However, in many applications, users simply want aesthetically guided color enhancement that automatically makes the scene more visually appealing while maintaining consistent colors and tones across viewpoints. We present ReTouchGS, to our knowledge the first framework for reference-free aesthetic color enhancement of pretrained 3DGS models. It predicts a scene-level color lookup table (LUT) from the model and its associated camera parameters. ReTouchGS also supports reference-guided color transfer by fitting a scene-level LUT using one edited view of the same scene and its corresponding original render. A shared blend-aware color refinement network with only 44.7K parameters refines an analytic color update to realize either color mapping without rendering. It preserves geometry and delivers standard 3DGS assets in seconds. We also design the ReTouch3D benchmark to support the evaluation of aesthetic color enhancement and reference-guided color transfer across viewpoints. Experiments demonstrate high color accuracy and strong generalization to unseen scenes in both aesthetic color enhancement and reference-guided color transfer.","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Boyu Wei","Chengkun Yue","Xuanhua Yin","Yiyang Li","Haodong Chen","Qiang Qu","Weidong Cai"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:47:43Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:47:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23054v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23054v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23054v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23051v1","title":"Refin3R: Geometry-Guided Refinement for Feed-Forward 3D Reconstruction","abstract":"Feed-forward 3D reconstruction models predict dense point maps from multiple images in a single pass. Without explicit geometric constraints, however, their predictions are often inconsistent across views and lack fine-grained precision. Recent methods incorporate geometric priors such as camera pose or depth, but use them merely as auxiliary inputs, process each view independently, or refine the geometry only where the priors are available. We present Refin3R, which refines feed-forward point maps with Structure-from-Motion (SfM) points, propagating SfM geometry over the image grid. In its dual-branch architecture, a 2D branch extends SfM geometry beyond its coverage using geometry-aware backbone features, while a 3D branch reasons over the SfM points of all views, capturing multi-view structure that the image grid alone cannot represent. The two branches exchange information bidirectionally to jointly refine each view, yielding dense and accurate point maps. Across ScanNet++, ETH3D, Tanks\\&Temples, and out-of-domain human and surgical scenes, Refin3R consistently outperforms feed-forward and geometry-guided methods in point map accuracy while running faster than the strongest geometry-guided baseline.","authors":["Jongmin Lee","Jaeyeong Kim","Seungryong Kim"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:46:34Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:46:34Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23051v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23051v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23051v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23042v1","title":"Uncovering the Invisible in Routine Radiology: Cross-Modal Detection of MRI-Verified Soft-Tissue Pathologies from CBCT via Physics-Guided 3D Self-Supervised Learning","abstract":"Routine X-ray modalities like cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT) are optimized for high-contrast mineralized tissues, leaving soft-tissue abnormalities buried beneath photon noise and below human visual perception. Thus, detecting non-calcified pathologies requires secondary magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). We demonstrate that sub-visual radiographic signatures of soft-tissue pathology are physically preserved within routine CBCT scans and can be extracted for opportunistic screening using a targeted, physics-guided deep learning pipeline, even under extreme paired-data scarcity. Using the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) as a model system (N = 212 paired CBCT-MRI joints), we address the severe overfitting of high-capacity 3D networks through a two-stage framework. First, we employ a frozen full-grid 3D masked autoencoder (BertsWin) pre-trained on 6,550 unlabeled CBCT volumes via the GradientConductor (GCond) optimizer and an anatomy-aware objective (PhysLoss) redirecting gradient flow into the [-300, 300] HU soft-tissue window. Second, automated localization of the condylar zenith drives a deterministic 3D cropping that eliminates 81% of background osseous variance. Evaluated via a patient-isolated 3x10-fold stratified cross-validation protocol, a statistically robust sub-visual signal for MRI-verified synovial effusion (ROC AUC = 0.793) emerges exclusively from representations pre-trained with the PhysLoss and GCond regime. Crucially, only this setup captures true 3D anatomical chirality (p < 0.001) and high intra-patient structural consistency (0.791) over inter-patient noise; conversely, identical architectures pre-trained with standard L2 loss or unmasked variance decomposition (MVC) collapse to random guessing. These findings establish a generalizable paradigm for repurposing routine, low-contrast radiographic scans into opportunistic screening tools for occult soft-tissue disease.","authors":["Evgeny Alves Limarenko"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:40:33Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:40:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23042v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23042v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23042v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23038v1","title":"When Anchors Shift the Target: Advantage-Gated Adaptation for Diffusion Reinforcement Learning","abstract":"Forward-process reinforcement learning adapts diffusion and flow-matching models by re-noising rewarded generations into regression targets. Because negative advantages induce concave updates with unbounded repulsive gradients, existing methods stabilize training using cached predictions from a moving policy copy, yet these anchors also alter what positive feedback teaches the model to fit. We formalize this conflict as anchor coupling: after separating frozen-reference regularizers, affine moving-anchor rules cannot eliminate negative curvature while preserving every positive regression target. We propose Advantage-Gated Adaptation (AGA) to decouple these roles. Positive samples retain standard target fitting, whose self-limiting update vanishes at the observed target independently of the moving anchor. Negative samples instead use the first-order tangent of the concave objective at the moving-policy prediction, preserving the local repulsion direction with a prediction-independent gradient. Within the quadratic-anchor family, these requirements uniquely determine the parameter-free gate $ω(a)=\\max(-a,0)$. Because the moving copy provides local linearization rather than retention, AGA assigns long-horizon behavior preservation to a frozen pretrained reference regularized at its operating classifier-free guidance scale, interpreted through an implicit-reward lens. Across compositional generation, text rendering, and preference alignment, our experiments show that displacing positive targets degrades visual quality and heightens sensitivity to policy-copy schedules, whereas AGA consistently improves the reward--quality trade-off.","authors":["Pu Cao","Min Zhou","Ying Shu","Tiezheng Ge","Qing Song","Lu Yang"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:37:36Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:37:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23038v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23038v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23038v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23037v1","title":"Automated Annotation-Free Synthetic Data Generation for Underwater Litter Detection: A Comparison of Generative Approaches","abstract":"Underwater object detection is often limited by scarce and imbalanced training data and by strong appearance variations caused by turbidity, lighting, and differences between sensors. Existing data augmentation strategies, such as geometric or color augmentation or fully synthetic image generation with Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), either lack semantic realism, fail to respect scene physics, or require additional annotations. In low-data domains like underwater environmental monitoring, the need for additional annotations is often the core bottleneck. Collecting new footage is hard, but labeling this dataset is even more time-consuming and costly, which limits how quickly a usable dataset can be constructed. We propose a unified fully automated zero-annotation pipeline that inserts class-conditioned trash objects into real underwater frames while preserving the original background context. Starting from sampled bounding boxes, each box is converted into an insertion mask and the object is pasted or generated within it, while the same box coordinates are automatically written as the corresponding annotation. Because labels are derived directly from the sampled boxes, the pipeline generates fully labeled training data for low-data settings without any manual annotation. Within this single pipeline we compare three strategies: copy-paste, Stable Diffusion inpainting, and DALL-E image-conditioned generation. We evaluate bounding-box faithfulness, visual realism, and the impact of the generated samples on downstream detector performance on the SeaClear underwater debris dataset. Results show that DALL-E improves detection performance in four of five domains, with relative gains of up to 96 percent over the real-only baseline.","authors":["Amala Mary Vincent","Bart De Schutter"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:36:51Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:36:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23037v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23037v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23037v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23034v1","title":"M3OPD-Reasoner: An Open Recipe for Post-Training Multimodal Reasoning with Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation","abstract":"Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation (MOPD) enables a single student to absorb the skills of several domain teachers by learning from its own generated responses. We present Multimodal Model Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation Reasoner (M3OPD-Reasoner), a fully open recipe for post-training vision-language models (VLMs) over three reasoning domains: image STEM, image perception, and video. We curate two datasets, M3OPD-SFT-1.35M for supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and M3OPD-RL-193K for reinforcement learning (RL) with verifiable rewards. On these data, we compare post-training recipes that start from Qwen3.5-9B: training one model on the mixed data of all domains (MixSFT and MixRL), and training one teacher per domain with Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO), directly or after an SFT warm-up, and distilling the teachers into one student with MOPD. We evaluate on 21 image STEM, image perception, and video reasoning benchmarks after we decontaminate the training data against them. The comparison establishes MOPD of teachers trained with GSPO directly from Qwen3.5-9B as the best recipe: the student closely matches each teacher on its own domain and is on average above the mixed-training baselines on the same prompts. Since MOPD already merges the domain capabilities that MixRL would otherwise have to learn, its student is a stronger starting point for RL, and we ask whether the same data can carry the model further from there: we add MixRL on top of the MOPD student, on the same prompts and with the same settings, which, to our knowledge, no prior VLM recipe has studied. The same prompts still improve the MOPD student, with no collapse, and give our main model, M3OPD-Reasoner-9B. MOPD and MixRL can therefore work as consecutive stages that get more out of the same data. We release all data, the training code, and the checkpoints of every stage.","authors":["Nguyen Quang Trung","Hoang Phan","Shuo Sun","Longyin Zhang","AiTi Aw"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:36:16Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:36:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23034v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23034v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23034v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23025v1","title":"When a Larger Guidance Scale Repairs Text-to-Video Failures and When It Does Not","abstract":"When a text-to-video diffusion model ignores part of a prompt, the usual first fix is to raise the classifier-free guidance scale. This paper is a measurement study of when that fix works. On Mochi, the same increase repairs failures on rare compositions (\"a snowy beach\") beyond what generating again does, does not repair negations (\"a highway with no vehicles\"), where it fixes fewer failures than generating again does, and changes nothing for events that must happen late in a clip (\"a sandcastle that a wave washes flat\"). Which of these happens can be known in advance, from the prompt and one inexpensive score. The score, which we call demand, measures how strongly the denoiser reacts to the part of the prompt that the failed video leaves out, and it is read from the first quarter of denoising, before any video exists. Our main finding is a condition: raising the scale is effective only when the prompt contains no negation and no event tied to a specific time, and when demand is sufficiently high, with recovery clearly higher above a fitted threshold than below it. Published corrections for the three failure types, used as fixed probes, behave as this account predicts. A short decision rule follows: before generating again, a user can tell whether the most common fix is worth trying, and what to use when it is not.","authors":["Taewon Kang","Matthias Zwicker"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:30:37Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:30:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23025v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23025v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23025v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.23007v1","title":"Tracking Ethological Behaviors of Broiler Chickens Through Computer Vision (YoloV11) Using an Android Mobile Device","abstract":"This study developed and evaluated a computer vision model for automated ethological behavior classification of broiler chickens in climate-controlled poultry houses. Using publicly available videos preprocessed and manually annotated into three classes (sitting, standing, and feeding), the YOLOv11 model was trained and deployed on an Android application via TFLite conversion. Exploratory analysis ensured dataset consistency and visual quality, while evaluation showed class-dependent performance: higher robustness for static postures (sitting, AP=0.531; precision=100%) and lower accuracy for dynamic behaviors (feeding, AP=0.292; recall=0.20). Tests with unseen images demonstrated significant improvements, achieving accuracies of 98.04% for sitting, 83.33% for standing, and 67.65% for feeding. The model showed effective generalization in real farm environments, supporting its potential for continuous behavioral monitoring, early welfare assessments, and decision-making in precision poultry farming. These findings establish a technical foundation for future mobile-based applications at commercial scale, reinforcing the role of computer vision in automated animal welfare evaluation.","authors":["Ryan Davi Oliveira de Meneses","Glauber da Rocha Balthazar"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:19:59Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:19:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23007v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.23007v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23007v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22996v1","title":"The Rhythm of Generated Video: Latent Chunking Leaves Periodic Traces in Compressed Bitstreams","abstract":"Most AI-generated video detectors analyze decoded pixels and overlook how videos are produced. We show that causal video autoencoders leave an architecture-specific rhythm in compressed bitstreams: their temporal stride concentrates unpredictable content at latent-chunk boundaries, so rate-distortion optimized encoders allocate bits periodically. Without decoding pixels, we observe four-frame pulses in CogVideoX and Wan2.2 and eight-frame pulses in LTX-Video, which are absent from content-matched camera footage. Passing camera footage through a generator's autoencoder alone induces the pulse, supporting its proposed origin. A training-free peak-ratio statistic separates generated from camera video (AUC 0.84-0.94) and persists across tested quantization settings and H.264/H.265 encoding. Frame-size traces also identify four commercial generators, with 89.2%, 84.1%, and 83.0% accuracy under H.264, H.265, and AV1, beating the pixel-based UnivFD baseline (72.7-73.6%). Our results directly link generative architecture to measurable coding demand.","authors":["Chen Ye Gan","Bryan Zhen Zhang","Gene Wen","Yuxing han"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:11:46Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:11:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22996v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22996v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22996v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22995v1","title":"CAST: Calibrating Correspondences for Training-Free Biomedical In-Context Segmentation","abstract":"Biomedical image segmentation must keep up with new anatomical structures, subjects, imaging modalities, and acquisition protocols, yet dense expert annotations are scarce and models trained on one cohort often struggle to transfer. In-context segmentation (ICS) offers a way out: a new structure is specified by a few annotated exemplars at inference time. However, reliable correspondence remains challenging in biomedical images, where adjacent structures often share similar appearances, and variations across subjects and imaging conditions further obscure their distinctions. We introduce CAST, a training-free framework built on a frozen DINOv3 backbone to calibrate fine-grained correspondences. Its stability test improves feature reliability at both global and patch-specific levels, while its coherence test assesses whether candidate correspondences are supported by consistent local structural context. Together, these tests enable more reliable correspondence across diverse anatomical structures and imaging conditions. Across eight biomedical benchmarks spanning 2D and 3D segmentation, cross-subject, and cross-modality transfer, CAST outperforms the strongest overall baseline by 12.34 percentage points in Dice and ranks first across all six volumetric settings. The versatility of CAST further opens up opportunities for challenging biomedical applications, including 2D-to-3D segmentation and atlas label transfer.","authors":["Mingze Jiang","Jiashu Han","Haonan Chen","Zhiyi Li","Yilun Du","Kunzan Liu"],"published":"2026-11-17T16:10:40Z","updated":"2026-11-17T16:10:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22995v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22995v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22995v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22968v1","title":"MI-Hyper: Generating Multiple Instance Models for Weight Space Transfer Learning in Histopathology via Mixed Tasks Pretrained HyperNetwork","abstract":"Zero-shot whole-slide image (WSI) prediction aims to generalize to new pathology tasks without parameter updates at deployment. MI-Zero makes these predictions by pooling patch-text similarities. Conditional weight generation, combined with supervision from diverse pathology tasks, offers an alternative: learning to construct predictive rules from task descriptions. We introduce MI-Hyper, a hypernetwork pretrained across mixed tasks to generate the aggregation and classification weights of multiple instance learning (MIL) models. We first train the generator and a shared MIL basis jointly using source labels. We then freeze the basis and refine the generator. The generated task weights are reused across cases. Across 308 tasks from 52 pathology datasets, MI-Hyper improves mean AUC, balanced accuracy and macro-F1 over MI-Zero by 1.85, 1.17 and 0.57 percentage points, respectively. On some molecular prediction tasks, both AUC and balanced accuracy improve by more than 20 points. These results support task-conditioned weight generation as a promising approach to weight-space transfer for long-sequence modeling.","authors":["Xitong Ling","Yousef Kotp","Weiming Chen","Yining Li","Jiawen Li","Minxi Ouyang","Xinyi Guo","Mingxi Fu","Yizhi Wang","Tian Guan","Lianghui Zhu","Yonghong He"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:52:19Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:52:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22968v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22968v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22968v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22967v1","title":"CodeReel: Accelerating Code-to-Video Agents with Exact Frame Reuse and Blocking-Aware Scheduling","abstract":"Coding agents make videos by writing and refining programs in a build--inspect--edit loop. A finished film costs a median of $2.85 in model calls and 11 minutes, over a third of that time in builds that block the agent. We find that much of this time is avoidable, for two reasons. First, 37.5% of the frames of a session's later films were drawn before, many shifted in time or inside an edited scene. Second, half of the builds stop at a check, where the agent needs only the verdict, but which builds stop is unknown until the checks finish. We present CodeReel, a training-free execution layer behind agents' existing commands. Exact frame reuse keys each frame by its drawing commands, regardless of its position in time, and reuses each frame whose key recurs. Blocking-aware scheduling runs the checks first and starts the render alongside them, at idle priority, unless the session's recent builds make a pass unlikely. Scheduling spends CPU to answer sooner and reuse returns it; exact keys let later versions use frames rendered for a failed one as the agent thinks. We introduce EditLoop, a benchmark of replayable agent sessions whose films are checked against renders from scratch. On it, CodeReel cuts the agent's wait by 36.3% against Rebuild, a warm pipeline with parallel rendering, and by 57.9% against Sequential, today's step-by-step pipeline. On time-shifting edits its reuse renders 43.9%, close to the 43.0% whose pixels are new, while Manim-style caching renders 99.8%. With real agents, the median wait per build is 0.62x Rebuild's in Claude Code, on topics never used in development, and 0.67x in Codex CLI. The gain holds for five models and two frameworks.","authors":["Junyi Shen","Yao Lu"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:52:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:52:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22967v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22967v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22967v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22962v1","title":"Hierarchical Diffusion Transformer for End-to-End Image Generation","abstract":"Pixel-space diffusion learns visual representations and image generation jointly in a single end-to-end training stage, without a separately pretrained visual encoder. However, plain pixel-space Transformers still trail leading two-stage latent diffusion models in generation quality and training efficiency. We hypothesize that part of this gap arises from retaining one token per patch throughout the backbone, which does not explicitly encourage a visual hierarchy that summarizes related content at progressively coarser levels. To address this, we introduce Hierarchical Diffusion Transformers (HiT), which progressively group related tokens and represent each group with a single token. The main Transformer bottleneck processes these compact group tokens, and ungrouping restores features to their original image positions. A compression budget regulates the average compression ratio across the batch, allowing group memberships and counts to vary across images and diffusion timesteps during both training and generation. The model exhibits emergent semantic structure, learning to group coherent objects and object parts without segmentation labels. In text-to-image generation, HiT further learns to group caption words with image patches depicting the same concepts, without region annotations. HiT achieves 1.48 FID on ImageNet $256\\times256$ and reaches comparable generation quality to JiT using approximately 40\\% of its cumulative training FLOPs. For text-to-image generation, HiT exceeds UniLiP's average score across five benchmarks with roughly $6\\times$ fewer denoiser parameters and $3\\times$ fewer forward FLOPs. These results suggest that learning a visual hierarchy within the generator breaks down the barrier to stronger image generation without external visual pretraining.","authors":["Xiaoyu Wu","Yifei Wang","Weihang Guo","Chen Wei","Lydia E Kavraki","Niloofar Mireshghallah","Steven Wu"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:47:56Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:47:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22962v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22962v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22962v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22953v1","title":"Fiberpose: state-of-the-art fiber morphometry and instance segmentation with a distance-transform neural network","abstract":"Analysis of fibrous structures in SEM images is a routine task in biomedical engineering and materials science. However, most available methods for fiber quantification still rely on classical image binarization followed by simple post-processing. These approaches often produce unreliable results in images with weak boundaries, heterogeneous contrast, and densely packed fibers. Here, we introduce Fiberpose, a framework that reformulates fiber analysis as direct regression of a smooth distance-transform (DT) field, together with a novel set of geometry-based algorithms specifically adapted to this representation. The predicted field directly encodes fiber geometry, while the accompanying algorithms enable instance reconstruction and measurement of diameter and orientation within a unified workflow. The framework is implemented as a standalone Python desktop application and a web tool https://fiberpose.org. On an independent dataset of fibrous SEM images, Fiberpose achieved state-of-the-art instance-segmentation accuracy in comparison with SAM2 and Cellpose-SAM, and more accurate diameter measurements than DiameterJ, Fibar and GIFT. The method remains effective in dense and weakly contrasted regions and shows qualitative generalization across microscopy modalities not represented in the training data. Fiberpose therefore provides an interpretable geometric representation and an automated pipeline for reproducible quantitative characterization of fibrous materials.","authors":["Viktor A. Balashov","Daniil S. Antonenko"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:41:46Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:41:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22953v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22953v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22953v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22951v1","title":"A high-dimensional spatial-physical coupling operator for generalizable super-resolution of three-dimensional turbulent flows","abstract":"Accurate representation of turbulent flows requires simultaneously capturing multi-scale spatial structures and the complex, nonlinear coupling between distinct physical quantities (\\textit{e.g.}, velocity components and density). In practice, however, turbulent data are often constrained by low spatial resolution due to hardware limitations and computational costs. While current data-driven super-resolution methods have shown promise by learning mappings via convolutional neural networks (CNNs), they typically treat physical variables as independent channels. This simplified approach neglects intrinsic cross-variable correlations, causing models to struggle with high-fidelity reconstruction, particularly under extremely sparse conditions or in out-of-distribution regimes. To bridge this gap, we propose an architecture-agnostic spatial-physical coupling operator that extends the standard convolution into a joint spatial-variable dimension. Unlike conventional linear channel mixing, our framework constructs a unified spatial-physical representation within CNNs to explicitly capture local inter-variable interactions. By lifting features into a higher-dimensional manifold, this operator significantly enhances the model's representation capacity. Experiments demonstrate that this approach enables strong reconstruction performance and zero-shot generalization, offering a physics-aware inductive bias for super-resolution of three-dimensional turbulence. More broadly, this work suggests that high-dimensional operators offer a promising avenue for modeling intricate multi-scale turbulent flows.","authors":["Zengyuan Zuo","Junjun Jiang","Fei Mu","Xubo Wang","Xianming Liu"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:41:02Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:41:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22951v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22951v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22951v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22925v1","title":"Code World Model 2.0: Towards Infinite Controllable Video Generation","abstract":"Visual proxies decouple explicit world-state simulation from photorealistic video synthesis, offering a controllable interface for both offline clip creation and real-time interactive streaming. Yet a practical renderer must address two challenges. Upstream proxies vary from coarse layouts to fine articulated geometry depending on the interaction, and streaming inference must operate in a few causal steps without accumulating drift across self-generated history. We present Invoker-Render, pairing a bidirectional creation model with a causal four-step streaming model under a shared conditioning interface over structural proxies, an appearance reference, and language. Trained on an aligned approximately 200-hour multi-granularity corpus, Invoker-Render honors fine geometric constraints when supplied while synthesizing plausible local motion under coarse proxies, enabling runtime selection of input detail. To bridge bidirectional creation and real-time streaming without compounding step compression and rollout drift, we introduce Progressive Forcing (ProForcing). ProForcing first trains a causal predictor and distills it into a four-step renderer using histories derived from training videos. Training then shifts to continuous self-generated rollouts. A pipelined serving stack supports efficient streaming inference. Experiments show versatile control across proxy families, granularities, and camera paths, consistent gains over recent streaming baselines, and stable 30-minute racing rollouts that preserve scene and subject appearance.","authors":["Ruoyu Wang","Hao Zhong","Yanming Yang","Jiahao Tian","Lin Liu","Biao Gong","Guoshan Lu","Yiding Tian","Chunhua Shen","Zhenzhong Lan","Chi Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:23:57Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:23:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22925v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22925v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22925v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22903v1","title":"BRepA: Graph-Based B-Rep Pretraining for Unified CAD Understanding and Generation","abstract":"Boundary representation (B-Rep) of CAD models encodes geometric primitives and their relations as a graph structure, enabling efficient shape modeling within CAD software. Learning on B-Reps for downstream tasks, such as segmentation and novel shape generation, requires specialized neural network designs. Moreover, performance improvements and accelerated training can be achieved with effective pretraining strategies, which is particularly important for tasks with limited labeled B-Rep data. In this paper, we introduce BRepA, a unified self-supervised pretraining framework that explicitly reconstructs both the geometry and topology of B-Rep objects via graph-based modeling. By learning from large-scale unlabeled CAD data only once, BRepA produces representations that generalize across tasks and significantly enhance both discriminative and generative learning. On segmentation tasks, BRepA demonstrates remarkable data efficiency. For instance, with only 1% labeled samples, BRepA pretraining improves mIoU by 32.52% compared to models trained from scratch. On generative tasks, aligning diffusion model representations with pretrained BRepA features substantially accelerates convergence, achieving nearly 2 times faster training while improving generation quality under the same computational budget, despite the diffusion model being roughly 20 times larger than BRepA. Overall, our results show that geometry- and topology-aware B-Rep pretraining provides a powerful foundation for both understanding and generating CAD structures, serving as a versatile representation backbone for diverse downstream applications. We will release the code to facilitate future research.","authors":["Dekai Zhu","Zhaiyu Chen","Stefan Gavranovic","Slobodan Ilic"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:08:35Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:08:35Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22903v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22903v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22903v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","instance","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22896v1","title":"Impact of Pretraining Length, Model Size, and Annotation Budget on Downstream Clinical Tasks of Foundation Models in Radiotherapy","abstract":"Cone-beam CT (CBCT) scans acquired during radiotherapy capture changes such as tumor shrinkage and radiation-induced tissue changes, offering great potential for foundation models that help monitor individual treatment responses. However, to our knowledge, no large-scale pretrained models for radiotherapy CBCT exist, and evaluating modeling choices is difficult since downstream tasks are not known in advance. We examine how pretraining length, model size, and finetuning strategy relate to downstream clinical performance, and how metrics tracked during pretraining correlate with it. We pretrain vision transformer encoders with masked image modeling on about 150,000 raw 3D radiotherapy CBCT scans, tracking representation quality via validation loss, effective rank, and linear probing. Downstream tasks are classification of feeding tube presence ($n=51,135$ CBCTs, $1,562$ patients) and clinical characteristics (T-, N-stage, sex, age, tumor site; $n=1,278$ patients) in head and neck cancer. We compare low-rank adaptation (LoRA) and full finetuning against no pretraining, varying annotated dataset size. Pretext validation loss was the most consistent predictor of downstream performance; effective rank and linear probing were also associated, but not consistently across model sizes and finetuning methods. LoRA performed comparably to full finetuning while training only 0.5-2.5% of the parameters. Fully finetuned models consistently outperformed models without pretraining across all sizes, with AUROC 0.91-0.97 vs. 0.58-0.85 for feeding tube classification and 0.99-1.00 vs. 0.71-0.97 for sex classification. Model size had only a small, inconsistent effect, suggesting smaller models suffice at lower computational cost. Code and pretrained models (non-commercial license): https://github.com/alexCortinaU/RT-CBCT_SSL","authors":["Alejandro Cortina Uribe","Sidsel Højklint Poulsen","Ivan Richter Vogelius","Jens Petersen"],"published":"2026-11-17T15:05:19Z","updated":"2026-11-17T15:05:19Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22896v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22896v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22896v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22880v1","title":"Learning Agent-Centric Vision-Language-Action World Models for Autonomous Driving","abstract":"Recent progress in integrating vision-language-action (VLA) models with world modeling has significantly advanced end-to-end autonomous driving by allowing systems to reason about imagined futures instead of merely reacting to current observations. However, most world VLA models generate future frames at the pixel level with plenty of dense visual tokens. Although such generated futures can be visually plausible, they preserve many driving-irrelevant appearance details, i.e., only a small portion of the tokens directly encode critical planning-relevant factors, such as agent geometry, motion dynamics, and interactions. This introduces a significant performance bottleneck. An important question thus arises: what world state should a world VLA model reason over to support planning beyond pixel-level prediction? We argue that planning-oriented world modeling should focus on a compact and structured agent-centric latent state that captures the interactions between objects in the driving scene over time. As such, we introduce AgentWorld, an agent-centric VLA world model that uses discrete agent tokens jointly for reasoning and trajectory planning. Each token is dynamically grounded to a surrounding agent associated with its object's state, such as semantic identity, 3D geometry, motion, and interaction context. AgentWorld learns these tokens through three key processes: explicit geometric grounding, reasoning-oriented supervised fine-tuning, and agent-oriented reinforcement learning. Extensive open-loop and closed-loop experiments under diverse driving scenarios show that AgentWorld enhances planning capabilities, fully exploits visual evidence and agent dynamics, and yields interpretable agent-centric world states.","authors":["Guoqing Wang","Pin Tang","Xiangxuan Ren","Chao Ma"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:53:49Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:53:49Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22880v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22880v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22880v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22874v1","title":"Harnessing Image Diffusion Prior for Photo-Realistic Video Restoration","abstract":"Recent image generation models produce photo-realistic results with rich details, but transferring their powerful priors to video restoration remains difficult due to temporal inconsistency from stochastic detail synthesis. Existing video restoration methods improve temporal coherence but often sacrifice visual fidelity, leaving a gap between image-level quality and video-level stability. We propose a generative video restoration method that bridges this gap by enabling temporally consistent detail synthesis from image generation priors. Our method combines noise energy rebalancing to suppress structure-disturbing low-frequency stochasticity, motion-aligned noise warping to propagate details along object motion, a self-supervised denoising encoder to mitigate encoder-induced temporal uncertainty, and decoupled temporal attention to model cross-frame dependencies. Together, these components preserve fidelity, enhance fine-grained realism, and maintain temporal consistency. Extensive experiments show that our method substantially outperforms existing video restoration approaches in visual quality, detail richness, and temporal stability.","authors":["Fanghua Yu","Hongyu An","Jinfan Hu","Xinqi Lin","Zhiyuan You","Jian Wang","Hongyang Li","Chao Dong","Jinjin Gu"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:49:02Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:49:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22874v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22874v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22874v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22857v1","title":"Beyond Co-Generation: Rethinking Jointness in Joint Audio-Video Generation","abstract":"Our aim is to advance the understanding of genuine audio-visual coordination in joint text-to-audio-video (T2AV) generation, with particular emphasis on semantic consistency. Evaluating audio-visual consistency in T2AV generation is nontrivial, as detailed text prompts obscure whether such consistency stems from genuine cross-modal coordination or merely from independent text following. To isolate the former, we introduce intrinsic jointness: the ability of audio and video to produce the same realization for details left underspecified by the prompt. Using semantically underspecified prompts (e.g., \"musical instrument playing\" rather than \"electric guitar playing\"), we investigate four recent models and reveal their limited intrinsic jointness. To address this limitation, we propose JointREPA, a simple representation alignment-based post-training method to facilitate cross-modal coordination. Since existing benchmarks use semantically detailed prompts, we introduce UNSAIDBench, a benchmark designed to evaluate intrinsic jointness under semantic underspecification. Experiments across two distinct models and three benchmarks, including UNSAIDBench, demonstrate that JointREPA consistently improves semantic consistency.","authors":["Jihoo Jung","Ji-Hoon Kim","Suyeon Lee","Gia Thinh Pham","Joon Son Chung"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:37:31Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:37:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22857v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22857v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22857v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22856v1","title":"Propose & Verify: Fast Reflection Symmetry Detection via Harmonic Fields","abstract":"Detecting multiple reflection symmetries in 3D shapes requires aggregating geometric evidence over a large space of candidate planes, making traditional voting approaches computationally expensive. We introduce the Fourier Accelerated Symmetry Transform (FAST), a training-free framework that combines geometric pair voting with efficient spectral computation. Exploiting the fact that global reflection planes pass through the shape centroid, we reduce the search to plane orientations, while geometric consistency filtering and independent witnesses suppress unreliable pair hypotheses. Our key insight is to represent these votes as a signal over plane orientations and efficiently compute a continuous, smoothed symmetry field in the spherical-harmonic domain. Specifically, we exploit the Funk-Hecke theorem, the spherical counterpart of the Fourier convolution theorem for rotationally symmetric kernels, to transform Gaussian smoothing into multiplication of harmonic coefficients. This enables efficient field evaluation, from which symmetry candidates are extracted, refined, and geometrically verified. Across two benchmarks, including a new benchmark of highly symmetric shapes, FAST achieves the highest clean multi-plane detection $F_1$ scores among evaluated methods (0.861 and 0.947), with GPU inference below 4 ms per shape: over $100\\times$ faster than the next-fastest evaluated multi-plane GPU baseline. FAST also demonstrates strong robustness to geometric noise and competitive single-plane detection, achieving 94.9% success on clean single-plane shapes.","authors":["Yishai Lavi","Shai Avidan"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:37:11Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:37:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22856v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22856v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22856v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22855v1","title":"CaFe: Causally Faithful Conditional Traffic Scenario Generation","abstract":"Language-conditioned traffic simulation enables scalable test scenarios steered by natural language. The goal is to control selected agents while surrounding traffic reacts to their observed behaviour. For example, a vehicle instructed to cut in late should cause the following vehicle to brake only when the manoeuvre becomes observable. Yet simulators can leak private instructions across agents, causing surrounding vehicles to react before the instructed behaviour becomes observable. Such premature reactions defuse desired events and alter the ensuing closed-loop scenario. Existing benchmarks measure what agents do, not what they could have known, leaving this failure unmeasured. We formalise it as intent leakage and address it through evaluation, data and modelling. Our paired intervention test holds an instructed agent's executed trajectory fixed while varying its private instruction, isolating leakage-induced changes in surrounding agents. We also construct WOMD-PACT, an automatically generated, verifiable dataset of 531,099 WOMD scenes with timed behaviour tags with reaction episodes identified through a counterfactual ghost test and grounded private instructions. Finally, we introduce CaFe, a dual-stream diffusion-forcing simulator fine-tuned in closed loop that restricts inter-agent interactions to public beliefs, preserving legitimate anticipation without leaking private intent. Our evaluation reveals that existing conditioning designs displace 23-83% of nearby uninstructed agents through private-intent leakage, even when the instructed agent's executed motion is held fixed. CaFe eliminates measurable leakage while maintaining comparable WOSAC realism (0.763), demonstrating that causal faithfulness can be enforced without sacrificing measured simulation realism.","authors":["Steven Yu Tung","Shubham Vaijanath Phoolari","Christoph Lauer","Abhinav Valada","Steven Peters"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:35:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:35:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22855v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22855v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22855v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22852v1","title":"Technical Report of Mobile Manipulator Robot for Industrial Environments","abstract":"This paper presents the design and implementation of Auriga Robotics' mobile manipulator robot, developed at the Robotics and Intelligent Automation Laboratory, Department of Electrical Engineering, Shahid Beheshti University. Hereafter, this platform is referred to as the @Work Robot. The robot targets industrial tasks, with an emphasis on improving efficiency in repetitive or hazardous work environments. It uses a 4-wheel Mecanum drive for omnidirectional movement and a 5-degree-of-freedom manipulator arm with a 3D-printed gripper for object handling and navigation. The electronics consist of custom PCB boards built around ESP32 microcontrollers, together with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX that handles high-level processing and real-time system control. On the software side, Hector SLAM provides mapping and localization, A* handles path planning, and YOLO performs real-time object detection. These modules draw on fused sensor data to deliver robust navigation and reliable collision avoidance. Overall, the system shows effective hardware-software integration, with consistent performance in autonomous navigation, object handling, and task execution in industrial-like settings.","authors":["Aria Dadnavi","Matin Jamshidi","Kian Khatibi","Shahriar Karimi Makhsous","Saina Najafi Shabestari","Seyed Sobhan Hosseini Hajibekandeh","Zahra Mahaam","Reyhane Mohasseli","Ghazal Ghoreishi","Amirhossein Gholizadeh Behbahani","Mohammad Hossein Moaiyeri"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:34:18Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:34:18Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22852v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22852v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22852v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22829v1","title":"Kolibri: Color Finds the Place, Refinement Sets the Pose for RGB-D Localization in Colored Point Clouds","abstract":"An agent or robot in a scanned building must locate its camera in rooms that no model saw during training. Point cloud registration methods can align each RGB-D frame with the building's colored point cloud, accurately but at a high cost. We observe that geometric refinement brings nearby initial poses to the same final pose, so the learned component only needs to find the place. Kolibri does this with a graph network of 55,712 parameters that describes 10 cm cubes of points, called cells, by their color, local shape and neighbors. With the same refinement, it matches GeDi on 96 unseen ScanNet rooms (81.4% against 80.3% within 5 cm and 5°) and on a held-out test of 10 further rooms, in 82 ms per frame on an RTX 2080 Ti. The saved time allows for search without a room label. In one point cloud of 100 rooms, Kolibri loses 1.3 points against knowing the room, while retrieving the room first loses 56.0. In a hospital emergency department scene from a real scan, it localizes 86.5% of the frames rendered from the scan in 384 ms each, where GeDi needs 91 minutes per frame on cubes of the scan for a similar recall. The method needs colors that agree between the frame and the point cloud. On gray frames and across cameras with different color responses, point cloud registration methods keep the lead.","authors":["Leonel Aguilar","Bartu Tuncay","Christoph Hoelscher"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:14:50Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:14:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22829v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22829v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22829v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22825v1","title":"A tiny object detection dataset for high-altitude open-water search and rescue","abstract":"Large aerial platforms often operate above 1.5 km to improve the efficiency of open water search and rescue (OW-SAR). However, long imaging distances, wide fields of view, and limited sensor resolution reduce objects to a few pixels, increasing their susceptibility to environmental interference and making detection harder than for conventional small objects. To our knowledge, existing datasets focus on low-altitude scenarios and do not support automatic detection at 1.5-2.0 km. We therefore present TinyWOD, a tiny object detection dataset for high-altitude OW-SAR using lifebuoys as detection targets. It comprises 11,359 manually annotated images and 19,505 annotated instances spanning diverse environments, lighting conditions, water colors, and surface ripples. Compared with existing datasets, TinyWOD contains much smaller objects and a substantially higher proportion of tiny instances; its average equivalent side length (ESL) is 12.7 pixels, with 76.96% of in stances having ESLs below 15 pixels, reflecting actual high-altitude water-rescue imaging conditions. We define APtiny, a tiny object detection metric, and benchmark mainstream and state-of-the-art networks using this and conventional metrics. All evaluated networks effectively detect tiny waterborne objects, demonstrating TinyWOD's reusability and utility. We expect this dataset to support research on tiny object detection in high-altitude OW-SAR.","authors":["Hui Xu","Chenfei Hou","Henglai Wei","Hao Wang","Yidong Jin","Xiulin Niu","Haoyang Zhang","Hui Zhang","Tianshi Xu"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:08:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:08:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22825v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22825v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22825v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22820v1","title":"Pixel is All You Need","abstract":"Text, images, audio, touch, and even robot actions can, in principle, all be rendered onto a single canvas, suggesting an ambitious goal of unifying all modalities within vision. Language provides the natural first test. To subsume it, a visual model must reason directly over text in pixels. Across a wide range of image, video, and unified multimodal generators, however, we find that their visual generation modules cannot perform symbolic reasoning over text on the canvas. They succeed only when an underlying text module or prompt rewriter already supplies the answer, effectively reducing the generator to a mere renderer, a limitation that post-training does little to resolve. To test whether this failure stems from pixels themselves or from current training paradigms, we train a family of purely pixel-space generative models from scratch without any text pathway. With images as their sole input and output, these models read questions, write intermediate steps, and produce answers directly on the canvas. Remarkably, they exhibit hallmark behaviors of large language models. Performance improves predictably with scale following a power law. Writing intermediate steps frame by frame substantially outperforms single-frame answering, echoing chain-of-thought, while reinforcement-learning post-training further strengthens reasoning. Moreover, sharing one canvas allows the same model to reason across modalities, a capability that text-only language models lack. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first systematic study of these phenomena in purely pixel-space generative models free of text tokens.","authors":["Jie Chu","Haoting Shi","Fucheng Zhang","Jiawei Liu","Junwei Liao","Ying Wen","Muning Wen"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:06:40Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:06:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22820v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22820v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22820v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22815v1","title":"Visual Harness Scales General VLMs to Medical Images","abstract":"We show that an appropriate visual harness can substantially improve the performance of general vision-language models (VLMs) across diverse medical imaging tasks. We introduce \\ours, a medical visual harness that equips general VLMs with interactive visual tools and systematic diagnostic evidence management. With \\ours, general VLMs interactively explore medical images and progressively acquire diagnostic evidence, which is preserved in a lossless memory for subsequent retrieval and use. After submitting an initial answer, the VLM receives an assessment of potential omissions and the reliability of identified findings. This assessment guides further image inspection and measurement, followed by answer revision in an iterative agentic loop until sufficient evidence is obtained. Across 11 tasks and four imaging modalities, \\ours improves performance on all tasks with Qwen3.8-27B, with notable gains in spleen measurement (63.3\\% to 98.3\\%), renal search (57.5\\% to 71.2\\%), and pulmonary nodule detection (0.0\\% to 28.2\\%). High-capability API-based VLMs achieve even higher performance with \\ours. For example, GPT-6.1 Sol improves pulmonary nodule detection from 10.8\\% to 69.2\\% with \\ours, demonstrating the complementary benefits of stronger model capabilities and an effective medical visual harness. Our results highlight the potential of visual harnesses as a new paradigm for scaling general VLMs to medical imaging tasks.","authors":["Shaowen Wan","Hanqi Jiang","Zihao Wu","Junhao Chen","Weihang You","Ruiyu Yan","Bowen Chen","Tianming Liu","Lin Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-17T14:04:55Z","updated":"2026-11-17T14:04:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22815v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22815v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22815v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22790v1","title":"NexSight: Verifiable Tasks for Fine-Grained Agentic Long-Video Understanding","abstract":"Fine-grained questions about long videos ask how many times an action occurs, in what order events unfold, or exactly when a state changes. Answering them can require finding brief moments scattered across hours of footage. Agentic models that decide when and where to look promise to find these moments, yet this ability is hard both to measure and to learn. Sparse supporting evidence may establish that an action occurred without establishing its total count; verifying timeline-wide claims requires checking for missed occurrences and double counting. Answer-only supervision, in turn, does not explicitly supervise the search for evidence. We present NexSight, which builds verifiable tasks to both evaluate and train agentic long-video understanding. Its agentic pipeline, SightForge, accepts a task only if a verifier blind to the draft answer can reconstruct it from evidence densely reacquired from the raw video and counterexample checks fail to refute it. This yields SightLine, 310 evidence-traceable tasks from 108 long videos, evaluated through SightLoop, a shared multiscale harness supporting temporal exploration, local inspection, evidence integration, and revisiting while recording returned observations. On disjoint videos, solver trajectories on verified tasks become step-level supervision for observation decisions. The strongest model reaches only 54.52% on SightLine, yet 79.35% when given the annotated evidence. With under 6K examples, Sight-35B nearly doubles its base model's interactive accuracy (21.29% to 42.26%), outperforms the 397B model of its family, begins using explicit spatial inspection after post-training, which its base model never uses, and gains over 8 points on two external long-video benchmarks.","authors":["Tianci Xie","Li Chen","Pinxue Guo","Rui Zheng"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:52:28Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:52:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22790v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22790v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22790v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22769v1","title":"ForceBody: Force-Paired Parametric Body Motion with Torque Uncertainty","abstract":"Forces and torques drive human motion, central to biomechanics, robotics, and physics-aware animation, yet they are often absent from learning-based motion pipelines for body reconstruction and generation. Some motion datasets provide force annotations, but the paired motion sequences are incompatible with the parametric body models used in modern learning pipelines. In addition, existing datasets typically obtain joint torques through inverse dynamics, which is inherently sensitive to the quality of the input kinematics; however, they do not quantify the reliability of these torque estimates. We close this gap with ForceBody, the first dataset to bring ground truth force supervision into a parametric body representation, shipped with per-sample torque uncertainty. ForceBody pairs the SKEL body model with ground reaction forces and inverse-dynamics joint torques across 10,386 motion trials (9.7M frames, 26.9 hours) from 140 subjects. Each torque label is paired with a per-frame, per-joint uncertainty annotation obtained by Monte Carlo sampling through the inverse dynamics pipeline. We benchmark six architectures (MLP, Conv1D, LSTM, GRU, Mamba2, Transformer) on ground reaction force and joint torque prediction from SKEL motion. As one example use of the released uncertainty, finetuning a Transformer with per-sample uncertainty weighting reduces the MAE of joint torque estimation by 5%. Code and data are at https://github.com/DominoAI-Lab/ForceBody-NeurIPS-ED-2026.","authors":["Joonwoo Kwon","Yufei Zhang","Xiaoming Liu","Zijun Cui"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:35:10Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:35:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22769v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22769v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22769v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22755v1","title":"Teaching What to Look For: Privileged Perception Questions for Agentic On-Policy Distillation in Video Captioning","abstract":"Detailed video captioning requires comprehensive and faithful descriptions of objects, actions, and temporal events. Fixed-frame sampling, however, can miss brief motions, small objects, and other fine-grained evidence, motivating captioning agents that actively seek relevant moments and inspect local regions. Training such behavior is challenging because, unlike video question answering, generic captioning instructions do not specify what visual evidence should be acquired. We propose Perception-Question On-Policy Distillation (PQ-OPD), which uses training-only Perception Questions (PQs) to guide active video perception. PQs specify what should be inspected without revealing the corresponding answers, encouraging the teacher to explore the video more comprehensively and focus on relevant visual evidence. Along trajectories generated by the student, on-policy distillation transfers this guidance to the student's reasoning, tool calls, and captions; no PQs are required at inference. Across three detailed-captioning and two caption-to-QA benchmarks, PQ-OPD consistently improves the corresponding untrained agents across teacher-student scales and input-frame budgets. Under our evaluation protocol, PQ-OPD achieves the strongest primary-metric results among the compared open-source models on DREAM-1K, CaReBench, ShortVidBench, MotionBench, and VidCapBench-AE. Our ablations, together with analysis of temporal coverage and answer leakage, further indicates that the student internalizes the training-time guidance as a more effective video exploration policy.","authors":["Yifan Xu","Ruopeng Gao","Zhixiao Wang","Jiafu Tang","Yichun Yang","Zihao Wang","Yuanxing Zhang","Limin Wang"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:23:54Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:23:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22755v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22755v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22755v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22742v1","title":"GaussianMamba: Geometric and Relational State Space Scanning of 3D Gaussian Primitives","abstract":"3D semantic occupancy prediction requires representing complex spatial structures and modeling contextual relationships across a scene. 3D Gaussian primitives provide a sparse and adaptive representation of scene geometry and semantics, while Mamba offers efficient sequence modeling for capturing dependencies among primitives. However, Gaussian primitives are irregular and unordered, making sequence construction challenging, as conventional spatial ordering may overlook their anisotropic geometry and feature-semantic relationships. In this paper, we present GaussianMamba, a dual-branch Mamba framework that explicitly incorporates Gaussian-specific geometric and feature-semantic relationships into complementary scanning strategies. The first branch refines a locality-preserving traversal using anisotropic Gaussian support derived from primitive scale and orientation. The second branch reorders spatially organized groups according to feature-semantic affinity and adjusts local features using geometry-weighted semantic differences. The resulting sequences are processed by separate Mamba modules, and their features are fused through a support-aware mechanism that incorporates local feature discrepancies arising from the distinct scanning paths. Experiments on nuScenes-SurroundOcc and SSCBench-KITTI-360 demonstrate consistent improvements over GaussianFormer and GaussianFormer-2 in both IoU and mIoU.","authors":["A. Enes Doruk","Ozgur Erkent"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:11:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:11:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22742v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22742v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22742v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22733v1","title":"Evolve in Panorama, Render in View: Panoramic World State Evolution for Video World Models","abstract":"Video world models should maintain a world that keeps evolving as the viewpoint changes. Yet when the camera turns away and returns, current models often freeze the unobserved scene, lose objects, or change their appearance, because content outside the view has no representation that keeps changing. We present PanoEvo, which uses a Panoramic Evolver to evolve the world state in the latent space of a panoramic video diffusion model, where every viewing direction is present at every time step, so the scene evolves whether or not it is observed. Existing panoramic video models are not trained to evolve a scene from a single image or the objects within it, so we build PanoEvo-Data, an event-centric dataset from real 360° videos, and train the Panoramic Evolver coarse-to-fine as an image-to-panoramic-video model. Since each view covers only a small part of the low-resolution panorama, directly projected views are blurry and often show low-quality details or structural errors. A Perspective Renderer, built on a perspective video diffusion model, reads this state via latent-space projection and direction-restricted cross-attention to render sharp, high-quality views. PanoEvo achieves the best overall score among 23 publicly evaluated models on WRBench and the highest total score on STEVO-Bench. Code, models, and data will be released.","authors":["Longtao Jiang","Mingfei Han","Jianmin Bao","Tingjun Dai","Jinjie Wei","Zhiran Zhang","Xin Tao","Zhihui Li","Salman Khan","Xiaojun Chang"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:03:41Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:03:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22733v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22733v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22733v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22727v1","title":"SlopeGuard: Towards Generalizable AI-Generated Image Detection via Radial Spectral Slope Guidance","abstract":"AI-generated image detectors often overfit to generator-specific artifacts, hindering generalization to unseen synthesis pipelines. Existing frequency-based methods usually learn high-dimensional spectral patterns that may preserve generator-dependent variations. We explore Radial Spectral Slope, a compact descriptor of global spectral decay that captures transferable spectral trends while suppressing fine-grained variations. Our analysis shows that real and generated images exhibit systematic but generator-dependent slope differences, indicating that slope provides useful forensic evidence but is insufficient as a standalone decision criterion. This motivates combining spectral slope with semantic representations for more robust detection. Based on this insight, we propose SlopeGuard, which couples slope-aware data processing with slope guided feature learning to construct informative patch-based views, integrate Radial Spectral Slope with semantic features, and adapt prior preservation according to spectral atypicality. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SlopeGuard achieves state-of-the-art mean accuracies of 97.5% on AIGCDetectBench, 97.8% on GenImage, and 85.1% on AIGIBench, while maintaining competitive performance under blur, cropping, JPEG compression, and additive noise.","authors":["Cheng Fu","Yiheng Li","Yang Yang","Wenzheng Liu"],"published":"2026-11-17T13:00:56Z","updated":"2026-11-17T13:00:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22727v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22727v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22727v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22722v1","title":"Practice in Text, Act in Pixels: Correcting Where Perception Fails","abstract":"GUI agents can learn cheaply from a text view of an interface and act on screenshots through a vision-language screen reader. Yet on a simulated mobile platform with a low-cost reader, policies that complete most tasks in text fail on many of the same tasks on screenshots. We ask where a small budget of real screenshots is best spent to close this gap. We find that the reader's misreads are systematic and recur on the same interface elements. Noise matched to the reader's error rates, a natural sim-to-real remedy, misses this structure, and in a pre-specified comparison it did not beat training the policy directly on screenshots. We therefore correct the misreads at their source with reader alignment, which fine-tunes the reader on screenshots paired with their text views. The reader learns to output each screen's text view, as in text practice, and the policy stays unchanged. Spent on the reader, the screenshot budget gives 80.9% full-task success on new task instances, against 55.6% when spent on training the policy. Given the same labeled screenshots, adapting the reader also beats adapting the policy, and a single aligned reader improves every text-trained policy we tried without retraining any of them.","authors":["Zhongling Xu","Hairong Wang"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:58:00Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:58:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22722v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22722v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22722v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22717v1","title":"PCRG: Training-Free Pruning and Caching of Reference Tokens for Video Generation","abstract":"Reference-to-video diffusion transformers pack the variational autoencoder (VAE) latents of all references and the generated tokens into one full-attention sequence, in which reference tokens make up about half. Because attention cost grows quadratically with sequence length, these tokens take up an even larger share of the attention computation, yet most of them are static, smooth or irrelevant to the output. To accelerate reference-conditioned generation, we propose pruning and caching of reference tokens for video generation (PCRG), a training-free, plug-and-play method that uses two signals the model already computes to assign each reference token to one of three tiers, namely pruned, cached or kept. The first is a low-level redundancy r from VAE latents, since a token whose latent barely changes over time or across its neighbors adds little new content. The second is a high-level importance g from the vision-language-model (VLM) conditioner, where the attention that the prompt and any first or last frames pay to a token tells whether the output needs to read it. Tokens that are not redundant are kept, and redundant tokens are cached if important and pruned otherwise. Pruned tokens leave the sequence before the first step with all rotary position embedding positions unchanged. Cached tokens have their keys and values computed once and reused, and kept tokens are processed as usual. On MiniMax-H3, already distilled to 8 timesteps and quantized to int8, PCRG is 1.57x faster end to end on our V2V-Bench and 1.49x faster on the public VACE-Benchmark, while the quality of VBench drops by only 0.24% and 0.17%, respectively. PCRG shortens the sequence rather than changing how attention is computed or which steps are run, so it also stacks on step caching and sparse attention. On V2V-Bench it reaches 1.86x with TeaCache, 1.82x with Spark at nearly unchanged fidelity and 2.15x with Veda.","authors":["Siyuan Wang","Haoran Li","Yi Yue","Tak L. Fung","Quan Chen"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:56:13Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:56:13Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22717v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22717v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22717v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22709v1","title":"Know When to Zoom: How Small Is Too Small for Vision-Language Models?","abstract":"Online vision-language models often miss small details in large images, because the service shrinks the image, or rejects it, before the model ever sees it. A car 20 pixels wide in an 8000 by 6000 drone photo reaches Claude about 6 pixels wide, and OpenAI's default rejects the photo outright. We measure how much detail survives for seven models from four vendors, using an eye exam of printed codes and 601 questions about small objects in real photos. The smallest text a model reads half the time grows from 6 pixels or less on a small image to a predicted 10 to 13 pixels on a 7680 by 4320 photo at default settings. Zooming wins much of the detail back. Splitting each photo into up to nine tiles raises accuracy by 11.1 to 14.5 percentage points for Claude and Gemini, and by avoiding rejections it lifts GPT-6.1 Sol from 50.7% to 83.7%. A two-step zoom, in which the model first names the region it needs, matches or beats tiles on Gemini and comes within 2.5 points of them on Claude, with about 40% of their image tokens. When a detail is lost, Claude and Gemini both say they cannot tell about half the time, but they fail differently in the rest: Gemini gives a confident wrong answer in 43% to 48% of these cases, Claude in only 5% to 9%. A check based on detail size, run before the call, misses most of the gain from tiles on Claude and Gemini, but Claude's own reply is a reliable trigger: tiling only after an unsure reply comes within 1.4 points of always tiling with 62% to 70% of the image tokens. We turn these findings into five rules for when an agent should zoom, and release the eye exam, every model answer and code at https://siddvoh.com/know-when-to-zoom.","authors":["Siddharth Vohra","Min Xu"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:50:14Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:50:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22709v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22709v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22709v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22704v1","title":"Social Signal Bench: Evidence-Weighted Evaluation of Co-occurring Social Signals in Video and Audio","abstract":"Recognizing social signals such as interest and hesitation requires interpreting context-dependent behaviors that may co-occur in conversation. Existing affective computing benchmarks rarely evaluate such co-occurring signals, and conventional multi-label F1 overlooks semantic relationships and annotator disagreement. We introduce Social Signal Bench, a 47-hour densely annotated benchmark for the evaluation of social signal recognition and engagement, comprising two datasets: Social Signal Bench Video (25 hours), covering ten social signals in audiovisual recordings, and Social Signal Bench Audio (22 hours), covering six signals in audio-only recordings. Each dataset pairs a public training set of online conversational video, of which 10 hours are expert-annotated and the remainder pseudo-labeled, with a private expert-labeled test set spanning four conversational domains. We also propose Evidence-Weighted F1 (EW-F1), which weights reference labels by annotator evidence and assigns graded credit to semantically related predictions through optimal per-clip matching. Controlled perturbations demonstrate that EW-F1 distinguishes semantically related errors from unrelated ones, unlike conventional F1. Under a shared evaluation protocol, 7B open models post-trained on Social Signal Bench outperform general-purpose frontier models on video and match them on audio.","authors":["Siddharth Ravi","Anders R. Bargum","Hugo Anselme","Patrizio Cugia di Sant'Orsola","Alberto Cardaci","Flora Kincso Parragi","Vincent Borowiec","Line H. Clemmensen"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:49:04Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:49:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22704v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22704v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22704v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22686v1","title":"CascadeJEPA: Enhancing 3D Geometric Representations via Spatially Coupled Cascaded Prediction","abstract":"Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs) have emerged as a promising paradigm for predictive visual representation learning. However, our analysis of I-JEPA reveals a growing discrepancy between semantic and geometric learning: improved semantic recognition during pretraining is accompanied by degraded 3D geometric representations. We hypothesize that this partly stems from predicting highly abstract latent representations with incomplete spatial context, favoring semantic regularities over fine-grained geometric information. To address this issue, we introduce CascadeJEPA, a spatially coupled cascaded prediction framework that promotes richer patch-level interactions through full-grid context encoding and cross-attention between prediction stages, while largely preserving the original I-JEPA architecture. Extensive experiments demonstrate substantial improvements over I-JEPA across multiple 3D geometric tasks while maintaining strong semantic recognition performance. Furthermore, its advantages extend to future feature forecasting and world model planning, highlighting the potential of enhanced geometric representations to support predictive world modeling.","authors":["Kailai Zhou","Ziqi Chen","Xiaoning Xu","Yuefeng Wu","Zixuan Fu","Zijun Lin","Zhihao Li","Youwei Pang","Xiaoqi Zhao","Xun Cao","Bihan Wen"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:36:05Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:36:05Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22686v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22686v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22686v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22673v1","title":"FVScore: Specification-Driven Neuro-Symbolic Video Evaluation","abstract":"We introduce Faithfulness Video Score (FVSCORE), a neuro-symbolic framework for specification-driven video generation evaluation. Existing evaluators rely on holistic MLLM judgments or vision-language metrics, yet aligning their scores with human judgments and tracing them to specific temporal violations remain open challenges. FVSCORE addresses these limitations by compiling each video into explicit propositions capturing prompt requirements and implicit propositions capturing unstated physical, temporal, and cross-modal constraints. It grounds each proposition using specialized perceptual tools and evaluates the resulting confidence traces with soft temporal operators inspired by Linear Temporal Logic. A Significance-Aware Calibration step then aligns verification with human perception by retaining only perceptually significant violations before producing a timestamp- and evidence-grounded report. We conduct extensive experiments across public and complex multi-event benchmarks with the strongest video models to date, demonstrating that FVSCORE outperforms holistic and agent-based evaluators in human correlation while providing highly accurate and interpretable feedback.","authors":["Do Xuan Long","Long T. Le","Yiwen Song","Min-Yen Kan","Tomas Pfister","Yale Song"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:25:33Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:25:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22673v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22673v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22673v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22669v1","title":"Beyond the Camera: A Tri-Modal Driving World Model for Future RGB, Thermal, and LiDAR Forecasting in Adverse Weather","abstract":"Driving world models forecast what a vehicle's sensors will record next. Most forecast camera video and some add LiDAR, but thermal cameras, which still see in darkness and some adverse weather, are rarely a forecast target, and to our knowledge no world model forecasts all three sensors. We study joint forecasting of RGB, thermal, and LiDAR on real adverse-weather sequences of the DSERT-RoLL dataset. We post-train a pretrained video world model for each sensor and represent LiDAR as a depth video aligned with the RGB camera, with densification and encoding chosen by what the model's video tokenizer can reconstruct. A tri-sensor model sees all three sensors as one video. We score LiDAR in meters against measured returns and compare against persistence baselines passed through the same tokenizer as the model. With this matching, an apparent loss of the thermal model to persistence in fog disappears. On all 58 validation sequences in six weather conditions, post-trained RGB and thermal forecasts beat compression-matched persistence. Joint training lowers the LiDAR error at 2s by 2.1m at a small cost in camera fidelity, and only the tri-sensor model's LiDAR forecasts beat persistence in mean error. Blanking one sensor degrades the forecasts of the others more in snow than in fog.","authors":["Faysal Mehrab Chowdhury","Md. Mahfuzur Rahman","Ahmed Rafi Hasan","Kishor Datta Gupta","Mohd Ariful Haque","Savannah Shannon"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:24:09Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:24:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22669v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22669v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22669v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22668v1","title":"Let Samples Sing in Chorus: Mitigating Spurious Correlations via Cross-view Consensus","abstract":"Deep models often rely on spurious correlations that fail on underrepresented groups. Existing methods either correct human-specified bias attributes or infer unknown biases from the learned representation space. However, annotated attributes may differ from the shortcuts actually used by a model, while a single representation provides incomplete and potentially noisy evidence. Our key insight is that a bias obscured in one feature space may be clearer in another. In this work, we propose CHORD (Cross-view Harmonic Outlier Reversal for Debiasing), a multi-view framework that allows samples to \"sing in chorus\" across diverse representation spaces to achieve robust cross-view consensus. CHORD aggregates aggregates within-class partitions in each view, which is a proxy for specifying candidate biases, to debias the target representation and classifier without bias supervision. Experiments on seven benchmarks across vision, language, speech, and medical imaging show consistent improvements in worst-group accuracy. Code and checkpoints are available at https://github.com/davelee-uestc/chord_debiasing_public .","authors":["Weiwei Li","Junzhuo Liu","Tong Chu","Hengfu Yu","Yinjie Lei","Wen Li"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:23:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:23:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22668v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22668v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22668v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22657v1","title":"SpatialCast: Reading the 3D World via Cast Shadows","abstract":"As multimodal large language models (MLLMs) advance toward understanding and interacting with the physical world, spatial reasoning becomes essential for understanding scene structure and anticipating the effects of physical changes. Existing spatial benchmarks have made substantial progress in evaluating spatial relations, memory, and viewpoint reasoning, but less systematically assess how models connect physical scene conditions with visual observations. We introduce SpatialCast, a benchmark that uses cast shadows to evaluate this ability. Cast shadows provide observable evidence of a 3D scene. Their image appearance depends on object geometry and pose, illumination, and viewpoint. We examine this relationship through three complementary tasks that assess scene-projection consistency, predict intervention-induced changes, and infer the physical changes behind visual transitions. The benchmark contains 21,000 synthetic questions with separate scene and geometry test partitions, complemented by 200 SpatialCast-Real questions from 20 photographed scenes. Evaluations show that closed-source MLLMs outperform general-purpose open-weight MLLMs and MLLMs designed for spatial reasoning, which remain near chance on the synthetic benchmark, while even strong closed-source models struggle with shadow-length and light-height judgments. We also use SpatialCast training data for GRPO post-training, improving open-weight MLLM performance on the benchmark. Further evaluations show improvements on several external spatial benchmarks, suggesting transfer of the learned capabilities. These experiments support SpatialCast as a controlled resource for diagnosing spatial reasoning capabilities and studying their learning and generalization.","authors":["Enhan Li","Jinghao Zhou","Jiaze Zhang","Hongyang Du"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:15:08Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:15:08Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22657v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22657v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22657v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22655v1","title":"Unified Residual Translation for Paired Image Restoration","abstract":"Paired image restoration spans changes in imaging contrast, sensor modality, and spatial resolution. These settings differ in their input channels and target grids, raising the question of how much of the prediction pipeline must be specialized to each task. We present the Unified Residual Translator (URT), which separates fixed channel and scale mapping from learned residual prediction. A deterministic reference uses channel averaging, replication, or bicubic interpolation to match the target dimensions, while a NAFNet encoder-decoder predicts an additive correction from the complete input. Separately trained instances share the prediction rule, $\\ell_1$ objective, and optimizer across MRI contrast synthesis, visible-to-infrared translation, SAR-to-optical translation, and real-camera super-resolution. Across six task and scale settings, URT achieves the highest PSNR and SSIM among the evaluated baselines on IXI, SEN1-2, and RealSR $\\times3$ and $\\times4$. It reaches 30.774dB on IXI and 13.478dB on SEN1-2, with luminance PSNR of 33.596, 28.742, and 27.342dB at the three RealSR scales. On LLVIP, it ranks second in PSNR and SSIM. A study of four spectral alternatives identifies complementary benefits of fitted references and bandwise residual gains. Together, these results support explicit dimensional alignment and learned residual correction as a common formulation for heterogeneous paired imaging tasks.","authors":["Joyanta Jyoti Mondal","Mridul Banik","Ibne Farabi Shihab","Md. Shifatul Ahsan Apurba"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:12:52Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:12:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22655v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22655v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22655v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22652v1","title":"OmniTongue: A Comprehensive MLLM Benchmark for Automated Tongue Diagnosis in Traditional Chinese Medicine","abstract":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have excelled in Western medical imaging, yet their potential in Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) tongue diagnosis remains systematically unevaluated. Tongue diagnosis requires sophisticated global and local perception, serving as a rigorous touchstone for assessing the visual understanding depth of MLLMs. However, existing datasets are limited by single dimensions, coarse granularity, and ambiguous labels, hindering the evaluation of authentic clinical capabilities. To bridge this gap, we introduce OmniTongue, the first comprehensive, fine-grained Visual Question Answering benchmark for TCM tongue diagnosis. This benchmark integrates 1,351 expert-validated images and 21,616 instruction pairs. Its core design features 16 clinical dimensions for multidimensional evaluation, region-specific annotations for pathological localization, and 66 fine-grained labels for precise diagnostic representation. Extensive evaluations of existing MLLMs reveal significant performance gaps, demonstrating that medical-specific models lack domain advantages, while universally poor Macro-F1 scores expose a reliance on statistical shortcuts rather than genuine understanding of tongue pathology. These findings not only highlight the current limitations of MLLMs in interpreting regional medical features but also underscore OmniTongue's significance for developing robust automated tongue diagnosis models. The code and dataset will be made publicly available in a future release.","authors":["Liuyi Xu","Yun Guo","Shusheng Li","Xuewen Zhang","Yining Qian","An-Yang Lu"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:10:17Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:10:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22652v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22652v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22652v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22645v1","title":"REVEAL: Reconstructing Video Amodal Content via Language and Flow Guidance","abstract":"Amodal perception enables humans to perceive entire objects even when parts are occluded, a remarkable cognitive skill that artificial intelligence struggles to replicate. Recent diffusion-based methods have extended amodal completion from images to videos, yet they lack auxiliary priors to guide the reconstruction of heavily occluded content and to maintain temporal consistency over long sequences. We present REVEAL (REconstructing VidEo Amodal content via Language and flow guidance), a unified diffusion-based framework that integrates complementary motion and semantic priors for video amodal completion. A user-provided text query serves a dual role: it drives open-vocabulary video segmentation to obtain visible masks, and it provides semantic guidance for texture reconstruction. For amodal segmentation, we introduce optical flow guidance: by warping visible masks from previous frames to the current frame, the flow-warped mask propagates visible content into occluded regions and approximates the object's current shape, providing a strong shape prior even under simultaneous deformation and occlusion. For texture completion, text guidance constrains the reconstruction of occluded appearance while providing a stable semantic reference that helps maintain visual coherence throughout the reconstruction. We also contribute LAVAT, a new benchmark featuring long video sequences paired with text descriptions, enabling evaluation of text-guided video amodal completion under heavy occlusion. Extensive experiments demonstrate that REVEAL achieves state-of-the-art performance on existing benchmarks while maintaining high-quality temporal consistency over extended sequences. The project page is available at: https://uark-aicv.github.io/REVEAL/.","authors":["Minh Tran","Winston Bounsavy","Ngan Le"],"published":"2026-11-17T12:00:15Z","updated":"2026-11-17T12:00:15Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22645v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22645v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22645v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22637v1","title":"OVET-Consensus: Rank-Preserving Counterfactual Event Verification for Video Moment Grounding","abstract":"Modern video moment retrieval systems often propose windows that overlap the target event, yet still select the wrong one. We study this scorer bottleneck on QVHighlights with a fixed QD-DETR-derived temporal expert: the parent-aware temporal expert reaches 64.71 R1@0.5, while diagnostic oracles over the same candidate family reach 75.68 R1@0.5 within the parent-reranked pool and 94.97 R1@0.5 over the full 100-candidate evidence pool. We introduce OVET-Consensus, a rank-preserving counterfactual event verification layer that keeps the temporal expert's ranked list as the default and permits only high-confidence rank-1 overrides from a policy calibrated on held-out training videos. The verifier uses parent-aware interval evidence, structured Qwen3-VL event judgments, VideoLLaMA3 pairwise temporal signals, and dense temporal evidence, but never lets those signals freely reorder the top-K list. On QVHighlights validation, OVET-Consensus improves the parent-aware temporal expert from 64.71 to 65.35 R1@0.5 with a positive paired-bootstrap interval, while mAP is largely unchanged, from 42.67 to 42.74; it makes 20 overrides, with 15 helpful corrections and 3 harmful corrections. The results point to a practical constraint on verifier design: semantic compatibility is useful evidence, but temporal grounding still requires deciding where the event starts, changes, and ends inside a visually compatible scene.","authors":["Alibay Osmanli","Shaogang Gong"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:52:55Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:52:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22637v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22637v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22637v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","retrieval"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22608v1","title":"PlaceTally: A Test-Time Memory Model for Constructing Place Graphs","abstract":"Counting distinct objects in a video is hard when the camera returns to places it has seen. A vision-language model that reads the whole video answers with a few small numbers, and one that reads it in parts counts an object again in every part that shows it. Object trackers need the category before the video is read, or a fixed vocabulary. We observe that a memory for counting does not need to know what it is looking for, only where it has been: objects rarely leave a room between visits, so parts of a video grouped by place share almost no objects, and a place can be recognised before any question is asked. Building on this, we introduce PlaceTally, a question-blind test-time memory model that constructs a place graph for each video. Each video fragment joins the place of its best earlier match or opens a new place, because a strong match is rarely wrong while a true return often matches weakly. A frozen vision-language model counts each place once from at most 128 of its frames, so every read has a bounded input and the memory grows with the number of places; the graph is built 15 times faster than real time. On RevisitCount, our benchmark of real room re-recordings, it scores 62.5% mean relative accuracy on test rooms and 61.2% on held-out rooms, against 51.2% and 44.3% for the same model reading the whole video. It stays level as rooms return more or less often, while a fixed correction of summed visits moves from 38.5% to 61.4%.","authors":["Alibay Osmanli","Shaogang Gong"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:23:50Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:23:50Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22608v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22608v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22608v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22592v1","title":"Distilling World Action Models for Efficient Bimanual Manipulation via Anchored Path Matching","abstract":"World Action Models (WAMs) jointly generate future video and robot actions through iterative denoising. Few-step distillation reduces the number of model evaluations, but its coarse updates can move the student away from the teacher's trajectory. We propose Anchored Path Matching (APM), which supervises endpoint and short-transition predictions from states reached by the student. From each state, we continue the teacher to zero noise and use its endpoint and first intermediate state as targets. Modality-routed adapters learn to match both targets from the same starting state. When the two gradients conflict, anchor-priority updates preserve the endpoint gradient and remove the opposing component of the local gradient within each modality. One checkpoint supports 1-, 2-, and 4-step joint generation. The sampling budget can be chosen at deployment without retraining or teacher queries. On RoboTwin 2.0, we train only on Clean demonstrations and evaluate directly on the domain-randomized Random setting. At one step, APM improves closed-loop success over continued base distillation at matched inference budgets by 2.0 percentage points on Clean and 1.4 on Random. Video peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) rises from 30.169 to 32.317 dB, with gains in structural similarity (SSIM) and learned perceptual image patch similarity (LPIPS). On two real-robot tasks, APM improves success over the one-step teacher and reduces control-cycle latency by $21.3\\times$ relative to the eight-step teacher.Project page: https://jialonglong.github.io/Anchored-Path-Matching/.","authors":["Jialong Liu","Junjie He","Yandu Chen","Bin Zhou","Heng Su","Peijun Tang","Jianan Wang","Changhao Chen"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:15:00Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:15:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22592v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22592v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22592v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22591v1","title":"PACT: Plan-Action Consistency Training by Preserving Correct Plans and Repairing Actions in GUI Agents","abstract":"GUI agents can formulate a correct immediate plan in their reasoning yet emit an action that fails to execute it, creating an execution gap. This recurring mismatch undermines reliable task completion, as a single execution error can derail a multi-step workflow. In outcome-supervised reinforcement learning, broadcasting a negative response-level advantage across all generated tokens risks penalizing valid planning together with erroneous execution. To address this credit-assignment conflict, we introduce PACT (Plan-Action Consistency Training), a training framework that combines reference-aligned plan protection and plan-aligned action repair without altering the original scalar action reward. Using annotated reference actions to identify execution gaps, PACT protects the verified plan by masking negative policy-gradient contributions across its token span. Keeping the generated reasoning unchanged as context, it then favors the reference action over the emitted erroneous action through a preference surrogate with stop-gradient pair weights. Compared with the outcome-only baseline, PACT raises AndroidWorld task success from 60.34% to 68.97% (+8.63 pp), while improving rule-based plan-action consistency on AndroidControl High from 83.71% to 89.74% (+6.03 pp). Adapting PACT to desktop agents also improves task success from 40.92% to 44.17% (+3.25 pp) on the full 369-task OSWorld benchmark. Together, these results establish PACT as an effective credit-assignment framework for bridging execution gaps and improving task completion across mobile and desktop GUI environments.","authors":["Zhuohan Cai","Zhangxuan Gu","Runze Li","Beitong Zhou","Ming Wen","Hongbo Yu","Shuheng Shen","Changhua Meng"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:14:59Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:14:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22591v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22591v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22591v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"},{"id":"2611.22577v1","title":"Semantic-Guided Geometric Decoding for Token-Efficient Visual Grounding","abstract":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) provide a unified interface for language-conditioned visual perception. In pixel-to-sequence grounding, autoregressive coordinate generation requires multiple sequential decoding steps per box, increasing inference cost with the number of instances. Pixel-to-embedding methods reduce this overhead but lack explicit spatial encoding when using a shared special token. We introduce SemGeo, a token-efficient visual grounding framework that represents each instance with a single grid token for coarse localization. A geometric decoder reuses visual features and hidden states to recover precise discrete box coordinates in parallel. To train proposal generation and box localization together, we introduce Gaussian soft labels that encode coordinate distances during supervised fine-tuning (SFT). Reinforcement post-training further combines box-set rewards with coordinate supervision on model-generated proposals. This stage trains the language model and geometric decoder on their own predictions to mitigate the mismatch between teacher-forced training and inference. Across eight benchmarks spanning object detection, referring object detection, and document layout grounding, SemGeo achieves the highest F1@mIou among compared MLLMs on six benchmarks. It also delivers $2.07\\times$ and $1.25\\times$ the mean box-generation throughput of representative grounding baselines under vLLM and Transformers, respectively, while achieving higher accuracy in both comparisons.","authors":["Zhaojie Chen","Junyan Yang","Le Yang","Qi Dang","Yucheng Huang","Qianyi Che","Zhaoqiang Chen","Xinqi Zhu","Qiang Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-17T11:02:17Z","updated":"2026-11-17T11:02:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22577v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.22577v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22577v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["instance","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-18T08:08:18+00:00"}],"tracked_paper_count":71,"storage_mode":"latest_overwrite"}};
